{"name":"Learning Spell Lists","sort":80000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"apprentissage-listes","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"apprentissage-listes","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Apprentissage des Listes","pages":[{"name":"Apprentissage des Listes","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Apprentissage des Listes</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>Dépenser des Points de Développement ne suffit pas à apprendre une Liste. Le personnage doit disposer d’une <strong>source réelle de savoir</strong> : maître, texte, tradition, inscription ou autre source appropriée.</p><ul><li>Vérifier d’abord Profession/Royaume et coût RMU.</li><li>Vérifier ensuite la disponibilité à l’époque et dans la Culture.</li><li>Une source exceptionnelle peut permettre de dépasser un plafond normal.</li><li>Les <strong>Eldar</strong> peuvent apprendre certaines Listes de Mentalisme par pratique et expérimentation ; pendant cet auto-apprentissage, la Liste est traitée comme Restreinte.</li></ul><p>L’apprentissage MERP-RMU est une condition extérieure ajoutée au développement RMU ; il ne modifie pas le <code>listType</code> natif des Items.</p></section><h2>Apprendre une Liste ne consiste pas seulement à acheter des rangs</h2><p>Dans RMU, les Listes de Sorts sont des Compétences développées avec des Points de Développement. MERP-RMU conserve cette mécanique mais ajoute une condition : le personnage doit avoir accès au savoir correspondant.</p><p>La question supplémentaire est simple : <strong>où le personnage apprend-il ce qu’il est en train d’acheter ?</strong> La réponse doit être cohérente dans le monde.</p><h2>Le maître</h2><p>La méthode la plus directe est l’enseignement par un maître. Celui-ci doit connaître suffisamment la Liste pour transmettre les niveaux recherchés. Un personnage connaissant une Liste jusqu’au niveau 10 ne peut normalement pas enseigner le niveau 15.</p><h2>Maître et tradition</h2><p>Un maître appartient généralement à une tradition, une Culture, une institution ou une lignée de savoir. Deux personnages peuvent donc apprendre la même Liste par des méthodes et dans des cadres culturels très différents sans que la mécanique RMU change.</p><h2>Plusieurs maîtres et transmission partielle</h2><p>Un personnage peut apprendre les premiers niveaux auprès de sa tradition d’origine puis poursuivre auprès d’un maître plus ancien. Une tradition peut connaître les niveaux 1 à 8 et avoir perdu le reste ; le personnage n’est pas bloqué arbitrairement, personne autour de lui ne sait simplement comment poursuivre.</p><h2>Textes magiques</h2><p>Un livre, un parchemin, des notes, une inscription ou une autre forme écrite peut servir de source. Le texte peut être complet, partiel, mutilé, chiffré ou écrit dans une langue ancienne. Une source n’accorde jamais automatiquement les rangs : elle rend l’apprentissage possible.</p><h2>Textes anciens</h2><p>Un texte issu d’un Âge antérieur peut conserver des niveaux qui dépassent le plafond normalement disponible dans la campagne. Il constitue alors une source exceptionnelle et l’une des principales manières de contourner le déclin général du savoir.</p><h2>Langue du texte</h2><p>Un texte n’est utile que si le personnage peut le comprendre. Langue, écriture et vocabulaire magique deviennent donc importants. Une traduction peut aider mais perdre des nuances techniques.</p><h2>Runes et inscriptions</h2><p>Une rune peut stocker un sort selon RMU ou servir de support à une connaissance. Les deux fonctions ne sont pas identiques. La compétence Runes peut intervenir sans transformer toute inscription en manuel d’apprentissage.</p><h2>Recherche personnelle</h2><p>Le personnage peut reconstituer une partie d’un savoir à partir de fragments, d’objets ou de plusieurs textes. Cela ne signifie pas qu’il puisse inventer librement n’importe quelle Liste à partir de rien ; une source de connaissance doit exister.</p><h2>L’exception des Eldar</h2><p>Les Eldar constituent l’exception majeure : la magie leur vient naturellement et ils peuvent apprendre certaines Listes de Mentalisme par pratique et expérimentation sans maître ni tome. Cette faculté leur est explicitement propre.</p><h3>Liste temporairement Restreinte</h3><p>Pendant cet apprentissage autonome, la Liste est considérée comme Restreinte. Ce statut décrit la difficulté d’acquisition et ne doit pas conduire à modifier le <code>listType</code> natif de l’Item. L’automatisation devra appliquer un traitement temporaire.</p><h3>Une exception réservée aux Eldar</h3><p>Un Magicien humain isolé ne peut pas simplement décider qu’il expérimentera jusqu’à découvrir les niveaux suivants d’une Liste. Tous les Quendi ne doivent pas non plus être assimilés automatiquement aux Eldar ; le groupe elfique exact doit être vérifié.</p><h2>Listes de Profession</h2><p>Les Listes de Profession sont apprises dans le cadre de la formation correspondante : Magicien pour les Listes élémentaires, Alchimiste pour ses enchantements, Soigneur pour ses Listes de guérison mentale, etc. La Profession suppose une tradition mais ne crée pas une infrastructure illimitée.</p><h2>Profession autorisée et source disponible</h2><p>Une Culture peut autoriser la Profession Magicien sans posséder un maître capable d’enseigner toutes ses Listes à haut niveau. Le personnage peut donc commencer sa formation puis devoir voyager pour poursuivre son développement.</p><h2>Listes Open, Restricted et Maléfiques</h2><p>Open décrit une classification mécanique et non une disponibilité géographique automatique. Restricted représente un accès plus difficile. Les Listes Maléfiques peuvent dépendre de textes interdits ou de maîtres de l’Ombre ; certaines sont en outre Sorcellerie.</p><h2>Connaître n’est pas lancer</h2><p>Étudier une Liste de Sorcellerie ne donne pas automatiquement des Points de Corruption : la règle de Corruption porte sur l’utilisation. De même, l’étude ne déclenche pas le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre tant qu’aucun sort n’est effectivement lancé.</p><h2>Temps d’apprentissage</h2><p>Les pages étudiées ne donnent pas de durée universelle fixe par rang. Il ne faut donc pas inventer arbitrairement un nombre de jours. L’apprentissage demande une période réelle d’étude ou de pratique selon les mécanismes RMU et la situation.</p><h2>Entre les aventures et en voyage</h2><p>Une grande partie de la formation peut se produire entre les aventures, auprès d’un maître ou dans une bibliothèque. Elle peut aussi se poursuivre en voyage si le personnage possède réellement les moyens d’apprentissage : maître accompagnant le groupe, livre, pratique elfique, etc.</p><h2>Sources exceptionnelles</h2><p>Un maître ancien, un texte du Premier ou Deuxième Âge, une communauté ayant préservé une tradition perdue ou une découverte unique peuvent permettre de dépasser les limites normales. La source doit expliquer précisément pourquoi le personnage peut progresser.</p><h2>Bibliothèques</h2><p>Une bibliothèque peut servir de source collective. Sa qualité et son état déterminent jusqu’où elle permet l’étude. Une grande collection elfique ou khazâd peut conserver des savoirs très supérieurs à ceux d’un fonds local.</p><h2>Atelier et Alchimiste</h2><p>Pour un Alchimiste, certains apprentissages peuvent aussi nécessiter un atelier : le maître ou le texte fournit la connaissance, tandis que la manipulation réelle des matériaux permet de l’intégrer. Les règles précises dépendront des Listes RMU concernées.</p><h2>Observation d’un sort</h2><p>Voir quelqu’un lancer un sort ne suffit normalement pas à apprendre la Liste. L’observation peut révéler l’existence d’un effet mais pas la technique complète. L’auto-apprentissage des Eldar montre précisément que cette déduction autonome est exceptionnelle.</p><h2>Objets comme sources</h2><p>Un objet magique peut fournir des indices sur la manière dont un effet a été créé ou stocké. Pour servir réellement de source d’apprentissage, il faut que son fonctionnement permette de reconstituer le savoir ; cela se décide au cas par cas.</p><h2>Accès par Profession et Royaume</h2><p>La découverte d’un maître ne transforme pas une Liste étrangère en Liste de base et l’existence d’un livre ne permet pas d’ignorer les restrictions de Royaume. Les coûts et catégories RMU continuent à s’appliquer.</p><h2>Pratiquer peut être dangereux</h2><p>L’étude théorique ne déclenche pas le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre ; la pratique réelle oui. Un Magicien apprenant un sort élémentaire spectaculaire doit donc choisir avec soin le lieu où il s’exerce.</p><h2>Havres et lieux d’apprentissage</h2><p>Les Havres et autres lieux protégés deviennent importants parce qu’ils réunissent à la fois maîtres, bibliothèques et contexte plus sûr pour pratiquer. Cela contribue à expliquer pourquoi certaines traditions conservent davantage de savoir.</p><h2>Automatisation future</h2><p>Lors de l’achat d’un rang, le module pourra vérifier Profession, Royaume, type de Liste, Race, Culture, Âge, année, plafond de disponibilité et existence d’une source enregistrée. Il affichera si l’achat est normal, exceptionnel, sans source connue ou normalement indisponible.</p><h2>Sources d’apprentissage sur l’Actor</h2><p>Il serait utile de pouvoir enregistrer sur l’Actor des sources comme « Maître — Fire Law jusqu’au niveau 15 », « Tome — Fire Law niveaux 1–12 » ou « Fragment — Light Law niveaux 11–15 ». Une source peut être possédée, accessible ou perdue sans supprimer les rangs déjà appris.</p><h2>Mode informatif et mode strict</h2><p>Par défaut, un mode informatif avertira lorsqu’une source manque sans bloquer automatiquement l’achat. Un mode strict pourra empêcher l’acquisition jusqu’à ce qu’une source ou une exception MJ soit enregistrée.</p><h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>Les Points de Développement représentent la capacité à assimiler une Liste mais ne créent pas la connaissance nécessaire. Un personnage connaît un nouveau sort parce qu’un maître, un texte, une tradition, une découverte ou — dans le cas particulier des Eldar — sa propre nature lui a permis de l’apprendre.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Learning Spell Lists","pages":[{"name":"Learning Spell Lists","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Learning Spell Lists</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Spending Development Points is not enough to learn a List. The character must have access to a <strong>real source of knowledge</strong>: a teacher, text, tradition, inscription, or another appropriate source.</p><ul><li>First check Profession/Realm and RMU cost.</li><li>Then check availability in the relevant Age and Culture.</li><li>An exceptional source can allow a character to exceed a normal ceiling.</li><li>The <strong>Eldar</strong> can learn certain Mentalism Lists through practice and experimentation; during this self-teaching, the List is treated as Restricted.</li></ul><p>MERP-RMU learning is an external condition added to RMU development; it does not alter the native <code>listType</code> of Items.</p></section><h2>Learning a List Is Not Just Buying Ranks</h2><p>In RMU, Spell Lists are Skills developed with Development Points. MERP-RMU keeps this mechanic but adds one condition: the character must have access to the corresponding knowledge.</p><p>The additional question is simple: <strong>where does the character learn what they are buying?</strong> The answer must make sense within the world.</p><h2>The Teacher</h2><p>The most direct method is instruction by a teacher. The teacher must know enough of the List to transmit the levels sought. A character who knows a List only through level 10 normally cannot teach level 15.</p><h2>Teacher and Tradition</h2><p>A teacher generally belongs to a tradition, Culture, institution, or lineage of knowledge. Two characters may therefore learn the same List through very different methods and cultural contexts without changing the RMU mechanics.</p><h2>Several Teachers and Partial Transmission</h2><p>A character may learn the first levels from their original tradition and later continue with a more ancient teacher. A tradition may know levels 1 through 8 and have lost the rest; the character is not arbitrarily blocked, but simply has no one nearby who knows how to continue.</p><h2>Magical Texts</h2><p>A book, scroll, notes, inscription, or another written source can serve as a source of knowledge. The text may be complete, partial, damaged, encoded, or written in an ancient language. A source never grants ranks automatically: it makes learning possible.</p><h2>Ancient Texts</h2><p>A text from an earlier Age can preserve levels beyond the ceiling normally available in the campaign. It then becomes an exceptional source and one of the main ways to overcome the general decline of knowledge.</p><h2>Language of the Text</h2><p>A text is useful only if the character can understand it. Language, script, and magical vocabulary therefore matter. A translation can help but may lose technical nuances.</p><h2>Runes and Inscriptions</h2><p>A rune can store a spell according to RMU or serve as a medium for knowledge. These two functions are not the same. The Runes Skill may be relevant without turning every inscription into a learning manual.</p><h2>Personal Research</h2><p>A character may reconstruct part of a body of knowledge from fragments, objects, or several texts. This does not mean that any List can be freely invented from nothing; some source of knowledge must exist.</p><h2>The Eldar Exception</h2><p>The Eldar are the major exception: magic comes naturally to them and they can learn certain Mentalism Lists through practice and experimentation without a teacher or tome. This ability is explicitly theirs.</p><h3>List Temporarily Treated as Restricted</h3><p>During this independent learning, the List is considered Restricted. This status describes the difficulty of acquisition and must not lead to changing the native <code>listType</code> of the Item. Automation should apply a temporary treatment.</p><h3>An Exception Reserved for the Eldar</h3><p>An isolated human Magician cannot simply decide to experiment until the next levels of a List are discovered. Nor should every Quendi automatically be treated as Eldar; the exact Elven group must be checked.</p><h2>Profession Lists</h2><p>Profession Lists are learned as part of the corresponding training: Magician for elemental Lists, Alchemist for enchantments, Lay Healer for Mentalism healing Lists, and so on. The Profession implies a tradition but does not create unlimited infrastructure.</p><h2>Allowed Profession and Available Source</h2><p>A Culture may allow the Magician Profession without possessing a teacher capable of teaching all its Lists to high levels. A character may therefore begin training and later have to travel in order to continue development.</p><h2>Open, Restricted, and Evil Lists</h2><p>Open describes a mechanical classification, not automatic geographical availability. Restricted represents more difficult access. Evil Lists may depend on forbidden texts or teachers of the Shadow; some are also Sorcery.</p><h2>Knowing Is Not Casting</h2><p>Studying a Sorcery List does not automatically grant Corruption Points: the Corruption rule concerns use. Likewise, study does not trigger the Risk of Attracting the Shadow as long as no spell is actually cast.</p><h2>Learning Time</h2><p>The pages examined do not provide a universal fixed duration per rank. An arbitrary number of days should therefore not be invented. Learning requires a real period of study or practice according to RMU mechanisms and the situation.</p><h2>Between Adventures and While Travelling</h2><p>Much training can occur between adventures, with a teacher or in a library. It can also continue during travel if the character genuinely has the means to learn: a teacher travelling with the group, a book, Elven practice, and so on.</p><h2>Exceptional Sources</h2><p>An ancient teacher, a text from the First or Second Age, a community that has preserved a lost tradition, or a unique discovery can permit progress beyond normal limits. The source should explain precisely why the character can advance.</p><h2>Libraries</h2><p>A library can serve as a collective source. Its quality and condition determine how far study can proceed. A great Elven or Khazâd collection may preserve knowledge far beyond that of a local archive.</p><h2>Workshop and Alchemist</h2><p>For an Alchemist, some learning may also require a workshop: the teacher or text provides the knowledge, while actual manipulation of materials makes it possible to internalise that knowledge. Exact rules depend on the RMU Lists concerned.</p><h2>Observing a Spell</h2><p>Seeing someone cast a spell is not normally enough to learn the List. Observation may reveal that an effect exists, but not the complete technique. Eldarin self-teaching shows precisely that such independent deduction is exceptional.</p><h2>Objects as Sources</h2><p>A magic item may provide clues as to how an effect was created or stored. For it to serve as an actual learning source, its functioning must allow the underlying knowledge to be reconstructed; this is decided case by case.</p><h2>Access by Profession and Realm</h2><p>Discovering a teacher does not turn a foreign List into a Base List, and the existence of a book does not allow Realm restrictions to be ignored. RMU costs and categories continue to apply.</p><h2>Practice Can Be Dangerous</h2><p>Theoretical study does not trigger the Risk of Attracting the Shadow; actual practice does. A Magician learning a spectacular elemental spell must therefore choose carefully where to practise.</p><h2>Havens and Places of Learning</h2><p>Havens and other protected places become important because they bring together teachers, libraries, and a safer context for practice. This helps explain why certain traditions preserve more knowledge.</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>When a rank is purchased, the module may check Profession, Realm, List type, Race, Culture, Age, year, availability ceiling, and the existence of a recorded source. It can indicate whether the purchase is normal, exceptional, lacks a known source, or is normally unavailable.</p><h2>Learning Sources on the Actor</h2><p>It would be useful to record sources on the Actor such as “Teacher — Fire Law through level 15”, “Tome — Fire Law levels 1–12”, or “Fragment — Light Law levels 11–15”. A source may be owned, accessible, or lost without deleting ranks already learned.</p><h2>Informative Mode and Strict Mode</h2><p>By default, an informative mode will warn when a source is missing without automatically blocking the purchase. A strict mode may prevent acquisition until a source or GM exception is recorded.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Development Points represent the capacity to assimilate a List but do not create the necessary knowledge. A character knows a new spell because a teacher, text, tradition, discovery, or — in the particular case of the Eldar — their own nature made it possible to learn.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Learning Spell Lists","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Learning Spell Lists</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Spending Development Points is not enough to learn a List. The character must have access to a <strong>real source of knowledge</strong>: a teacher, text, tradition, inscription, or another appropriate source.</p><ul><li>First check Profession/Realm and RMU cost.</li><li>Then check availability in the relevant Age and Culture.</li><li>An exceptional source can allow a character to exceed a normal ceiling.</li><li>The <strong>Eldar</strong> can learn certain Mentalism Lists through practice and experimentation; during this self-teaching, the List is treated as Restricted.</li></ul><p>MERP-RMU learning is an external condition added to RMU development; it does not alter the native <code>listType</code> of Items.</p></section><h2>Learning a List Is Not Just Buying Ranks</h2><p>In RMU, Spell Lists are Skills developed with Development Points. MERP-RMU keeps this mechanic but adds one condition: the character must have access to the corresponding knowledge.</p><p>The additional question is simple: <strong>where does the character learn what they are buying?</strong> The answer must make sense within the world.</p><h2>The Teacher</h2><p>The most direct method is instruction by a teacher. The teacher must know enough of the List to transmit the levels sought. A character who knows a List only through level 10 normally cannot teach level 15.</p><h2>Teacher and Tradition</h2><p>A teacher generally belongs to a tradition, Culture, institution, or lineage of knowledge. Two characters may therefore learn the same List through very different methods and cultural contexts without changing the RMU mechanics.</p><h2>Several Teachers and Partial Transmission</h2><p>A character may learn the first levels from their original tradition and later continue with a more ancient teacher. A tradition may know levels 1 through 8 and have lost the rest; the character is not arbitrarily blocked, but simply has no one nearby who knows how to continue.</p><h2>Magical Texts</h2><p>A book, scroll, notes, inscription, or another written source can serve as a source of knowledge. The text may be complete, partial, damaged, encoded, or written in an ancient language. A source never grants ranks automatically: it makes learning possible.</p><h2>Ancient Texts</h2><p>A text from an earlier Age can preserve levels beyond the ceiling normally available in the campaign. It then becomes an exceptional source and one of the main ways to overcome the general decline of knowledge.</p><h2>Language of the Text</h2><p>A text is useful only if the character can understand it. Language, script, and magical vocabulary therefore matter. A translation can help but may lose technical nuances.</p><h2>Runes and Inscriptions</h2><p>A rune can store a spell according to RMU or serve as a medium for knowledge. These two functions are not the same. The Runes Skill may be relevant without turning every inscription into a learning manual.</p><h2>Personal Research</h2><p>A character may reconstruct part of a body of knowledge from fragments, objects, or several texts. This does not mean that any List can be freely invented from nothing; some source of knowledge must exist.</p><h2>The Eldar Exception</h2><p>The Eldar are the major exception: magic comes naturally to them and they can learn certain Mentalism Lists through practice and experimentation without a teacher or tome. This ability is explicitly theirs.</p><h3>List Temporarily Treated as Restricted</h3><p>During this independent learning, the List is considered Restricted. This status describes the difficulty of acquisition and must not lead to changing the native <code>listType</code> of the Item. Automation should apply a temporary treatment.</p><h3>An Exception Reserved for the Eldar</h3><p>An isolated human Magician cannot simply decide to experiment until the next levels of a List are discovered. Nor should every Quendi automatically be treated as Eldar; the exact Elven group must be checked.</p><h2>Profession Lists</h2><p>Profession Lists are learned as part of the corresponding training: Magician for elemental Lists, Alchemist for enchantments, Lay Healer for Mentalism healing Lists, and so on. The Profession implies a tradition but does not create unlimited infrastructure.</p><h2>Allowed Profession and Available Source</h2><p>A Culture may allow the Magician Profession without possessing a teacher capable of teaching all its Lists to high levels. A character may therefore begin training and later have to travel in order to continue development.</p><h2>Open, Restricted, and Evil Lists</h2><p>Open describes a mechanical classification, not automatic geographical availability. Restricted represents more difficult access. Evil Lists may depend on forbidden texts or teachers of the Shadow; some are also Sorcery.</p><h2>Knowing Is Not Casting</h2><p>Studying a Sorcery List does not automatically grant Corruption Points: the Corruption rule concerns use. Likewise, study does not trigger the Risk of Attracting the Shadow as long as no spell is actually cast.</p><h2>Learning Time</h2><p>The pages examined do not provide a universal fixed duration per rank. An arbitrary number of days should therefore not be invented. Learning requires a real period of study or practice according to RMU mechanisms and the situation.</p><h2>Between Adventures and While Travelling</h2><p>Much training can occur between adventures, with a teacher or in a library. It can also continue during travel if the character genuinely has the means to learn: a teacher travelling with the group, a book, Elven practice, and so on.</p><h2>Exceptional Sources</h2><p>An ancient teacher, a text from the First or Second Age, a community that has preserved a lost tradition, or a unique discovery can permit progress beyond normal limits. The source should explain precisely why the character can advance.</p><h2>Libraries</h2><p>A library can serve as a collective source. Its quality and condition determine how far study can proceed. A great Elven or Khazâd collection may preserve knowledge far beyond that of a local archive.</p><h2>Workshop and Alchemist</h2><p>For an Alchemist, some learning may also require a workshop: the teacher or text provides the knowledge, while actual manipulation of materials makes it possible to internalise that knowledge. Exact rules depend on the RMU Lists concerned.</p><h2>Observing a Spell</h2><p>Seeing someone cast a spell is not normally enough to learn the List. Observation may reveal that an effect exists, but not the complete technique. Eldarin self-teaching shows precisely that such independent deduction is exceptional.</p><h2>Objects as Sources</h2><p>A magic item may provide clues as to how an effect was created or stored. For it to serve as an actual learning source, its functioning must allow the underlying knowledge to be reconstructed; this is decided case by case.</p><h2>Access by Profession and Realm</h2><p>Discovering a teacher does not turn a foreign List into a Base List, and the existence of a book does not allow Realm restrictions to be ignored. RMU costs and categories continue to apply.</p><h2>Practice Can Be Dangerous</h2><p>Theoretical study does not trigger the Risk of Attracting the Shadow; actual practice does. A Magician learning a spectacular elemental spell must therefore choose carefully where to practise.</p><h2>Havens and Places of Learning</h2><p>Havens and other protected places become important because they bring together teachers, libraries, and a safer context for practice. This helps explain why certain traditions preserve more knowledge.</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>When a rank is purchased, the module may check Profession, Realm, List type, Race, Culture, Age, year, availability ceiling, and the existence of a recorded source. It can indicate whether the purchase is normal, exceptional, lacks a known source, or is normally unavailable.</p><h2>Learning Sources on the Actor</h2><p>It would be useful to record sources on the Actor such as “Teacher — Fire Law through level 15”, “Tome — Fire Law levels 1–12”, or “Fragment — Light Law levels 11–15”. A source may be owned, accessible, or lost without deleting ranks already learned.</p><h2>Informative Mode and Strict Mode</h2><p>By default, an informative mode will warn when a source is missing without automatically blocking the purchase. A strict mode may prevent acquisition until a source or GM exception is recorded.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Development Points represent the capacity to assimilate a List but do not create the necessary knowledge. A character knows a new spell because a teacher, text, tradition, discovery, or — in the particular case of the Eldar — their own nature made it possible to learn.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"GFyTblBHRxaHYvR7"}
{"name":"Decline and Availability of Spell Lists","sort":70000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"declin-listes","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"declin-listes","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Déclin et disponibilité des Listes","pages":[{"name":"Déclin et disponibilité des Listes","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Déclin et disponibilité des Listes</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>La connaissance magique décline avec les Âges. Le plafond de connaissance générale passe du niveau 50 au Premier Âge à environ 5 à la fin du Troisième Âge.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Période</th><th>Plafond général</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Premier Âge</td><td>jusqu’à 50</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr><tr><td>Quatrième Âge</td><td>magie presque disparue ; pas de table chiffrée ici</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ces plafonds sont des tendances. À T.A. 1640 : <strong>Elfes 30, Khazâd 25, Hommes 12</strong>. Les individus anciens ou les sources exceptionnelles peuvent dépasser les limites normales.</p><p>Le Domaine Arcane n’est pas utilisé en Terre du Milieu. Les JSON RMU doivent toutefois rester intacts : la restriction porte sur l’accès au savoir, pas sur la suppression des sorts.</p></section><h2>Le retrait progressif de la magie</h2><p>La Terre du Milieu n’est pas un monde dans lequel la connaissance magique demeure stable. Des maîtres meurent sans transmettre tout leur savoir, des peuples quittent le monde, des royaumes et bibliothèques disparaissent, et certaines formes de pouvoir deviennent de plus en plus rares.</p><h2>Premier Âge</h2><p>Au Premier Âge, les Listes de Sorts peuvent être connues jusqu’au niveau 50. Cette valeur signifie que le savoir existe encore dans le monde ; elle ne donne évidemment pas des sorts de niveau 50 à tous les lanceurs.</p><h2>Savoir disponible et capacité personnelle</h2><p>La disponibilité maximale d’une Liste ne correspond pas au niveau du personnage. Elle définit jusqu’où une tradition peut théoriquement être apprise. Le personnage doit toujours acheter ses rangs et satisfaire toutes les autres conditions RMU.</p><h2>Deuxième Âge</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Période</th><th>Niveau maximal général</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr></tbody></table><p>La notation en intervalle représente un déclin progressif ; elle ne signifie pas que le monde entier change brutalement de plafond à une date unique.</p><h2>Troisième Âge</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Période</th><th>Niveau maximal général</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr></tbody></table><p>À la fin du Troisième Âge, les connaissances permettant d’employer les niveaux élevés deviennent extrêmement rares. Des individus anciens, objets ou textes peuvent cependant encore les conserver.</p><h2>Quatrième Âge</h2><p>Le Quatrième Âge voit la magie presque disparaître. La source ne fournit pas ici de table détaillée comparable aux périodes précédentes : aucune suite numérique précise ne doit être inventée sans autre source.</p><h2>Une tendance générale, pas une loi absolue</h2><p>Le rythme de déclin ne constitue pas la norme uniforme de toutes les Cultures. Les peuples disposant de traditions anciennes, de maîtres durables ou d’une relation profonde à la magie conservent davantage de connaissances ; les communautés dépourvues d’institutions magiques structurées en conservent généralement moins.</p><h2>Peuples elfiques</h2><p>Les peuples elfiques conservent probablement la plus grande quantité de savoir. Leur longévité et leur rapport au Nolwë permettent la transmission directe de connaissances très anciennes.</p><h2>Limites proposées pour T.A. 1640</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Groupe</th><th>Plafond conseillé</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Elfes</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Khazâd</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Hommes</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ces valeurs montrent comment la tendance historique doit être combinée aux différences raciales et culturelles. Des PNJ puissants comme Elrond ou Galadriel peuvent dépasser le plafond elfique normal.</p><h2>Hommes des Collines</h2><p>La source fournit un exemple plus restrictif pour les Hommes des Collines : Listes de base jusqu’au niveau 8 et autres Listes jusqu’au niveau 5. Cet exemple montre que la disponibilité peut varier selon la Culture <em>et</em> le type de Liste.</p><h2>Listes de base et traditions propres</h2><p>Une Culture possédant une tradition magique locale peut conserver ses Listes de Profession mieux que les Listes générales du Royaume. La disponibilité ne doit donc pas être réduite à un unique plafond racial.</p><h2>Individus héritiers des Âges anciens</h2><p>Les plafonds ne s’appliquent pas nécessairement aux êtres ayant conservé personnellement des connaissances des Âges antérieurs, comme Elrond, Galadriel ou Sauron. Ils peuvent connaître des sorts au-delà de la disponibilité générale et éventuellement transmettre une partie de ce savoir.</p><h2>Transmission par un maître ancien</h2><p>Un personnage vivant à une époque où une Liste n’est normalement connue que jusqu’au niveau 10 peut rencontrer un maître capable d’enseigner davantage. Le déclin signifie que le savoir est perdu par la connaissance générale, pas qu’il est cosmologiquement impossible.</p><h2>Textes anciens, ruines et redécouverte</h2><p>Un tome d’un Âge antérieur, une bibliothèque perdue, une inscription ou un objet ancien peuvent préserver une connaissance dépassant le plafond local. Le personnage doit encore investir les rangs nécessaires ; la découverte rend simplement l’apprentissage possible.</p><h2>Disponibilité et rangs RMU</h2><p>RMU utilise les rangs d’une Liste pour déterminer le niveau maximal de sort accessible. MERP-RMU ajoute que le personnage ne peut normalement développer la connaissance au-delà du niveau disponible pour son époque et sa tradition sans source exceptionnelle.</p><h2>Ne pas tronquer les JSON</h2><p>Les Items <code>spell-list</code> RMU doivent conserver tous leurs sorts, y compris ceux de haut niveau. Ils peuvent être nécessaires pour d’autres Âges, des PNJ exceptionnels, des objets anciens ou une découverte extraordinaire. La restriction porte sur l’accès du personnage, jamais sur le contenu technique de la Liste.</p><h2>Disponibilité comme métadonnée</h2><p>La disponibilité pourra être représentée par une couche MERP séparée : limite générale par Âge, exceptions raciales et culturelles, plafond spécifique des Listes de base et sources personnelles exceptionnelles.</p><h2>Âge et année de campagne</h2><p>Une année de campagne précise permettrait de déterminer la période appropriée. Une éventuelle interpolation annuelle entre les valeurs de la table serait un outil MERP-RMU, pas une formule explicitement fournie par la source, et devrait être présentée comme telle.</p><h2>Professions autorisées</h2><p>Les Listes de base disponibles dépendent des Professions autorisées à la Race ou à la Culture. Cette chaîne peut être représentée simplement : Race/Culture → Profession autorisée → Listes de base accessibles.</p><h2>Listes Open, Restricted et Maléfiques</h2><p>La classification RMU décrit le coût et le statut technique, pas la présence automatique d’un maître dans chaque région. Une Liste Open peut être absente localement ; une Liste Restreinte nécessite un accès plus rare ; une Liste Maléfique peut exister sans être accessible aux Peuples Libres et peut en outre être Sorcellerie.</p><h2>Domaine Arcane</h2><p><strong>Le Domaine Arcane n’est pas utilisé en Terre du Milieu et aucune Liste Arcanique ne peut être choisie pour la création normale.</strong> Les données RMU natives doivent néanmoins rester présentes ; MERP-RMU applique une restriction de contexte plutôt que de modifier le Core.</p><h2>Professions hybrides</h2><p>Les Professions Hybrides utilisent leurs Listes de Profession dans l’organisation retenue. Leur disponibilité suit la Profession, les Royaumes concernés et les traditions capables de transmettre les Listes correspondantes.</p><h2>Connaissance partielle</h2><p>Une Liste peut être connue seulement jusqu’à un certain niveau. Une tradition peut conserver Fire Law jusqu’au niveau 8 et avoir perdu les niveaux supérieurs. Cette représentation partielle est beaucoup plus naturelle que de supprimer complètement la Liste.</p><h2>Objets comme survivance du passé</h2><p>Une arme du Premier Âge peut produire un effet qu’aucun artisan du Troisième ne sait reproduire. L’existence d’un objet ne prouve donc pas que le savoir permettant sa fabrication est encore disponible.</p><h2>Disponibilité historique, culturelle, locale et personnelle</h2><p>Il est utile de distinguer quatre couches : historique — niveau général de l’époque ; culturelle — savoir conservé par la Race ou Culture ; locale — source effectivement accessible dans le lieu ; personnelle — maître, livre, héritage ou autre exception propre au personnage.</p><h2>Une mécanique de rareté plutôt qu’une interdiction</h2><p>Le déclin doit créer de la rareté et des objectifs d’aventure. Lorsqu’un personnage veut progresser au-delà de ce que sa tradition connaît, la réponse devrait être « il faut trouver une source plus ancienne » plutôt qu’un simple « interdit ».</p><h2>Automatisation future</h2><p>Lors de l’achat d’un rang de Liste, le module pourra vérifier l’Âge, l’année, la Race, la Culture, la Profession, le type de Liste et les exceptions personnelles. Par défaut, il est préférable d’afficher un avertissement plutôt qu’un blocage absolu ; un mode strict pourra être proposé.</p><h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>Le déclin fonctionne comme une règle de transmission, rareté et accès à la connaissance. Il permet à la magie des Âges anciens de rester réellement supérieure sans supprimer des données RMU ni empêcher les personnages de rechercher et restaurer une partie du savoir perdu.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Decline and Availability of Spell Lists","pages":[{"name":"Decline and Availability of Spell Lists","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Decline and Availability of Spell Lists</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magical knowledge declines as the Ages pass. The general knowledge ceiling falls from level 50 in the First Age to about 5 by the end of the Third Age.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First Age</td><td>up to 50</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth Age</td><td>magic almost gone; no numerical table here</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These ceilings are trends. At T.A. 1640: <strong>Elves 30, Khazâd 25, Men 12</strong>. Ancient individuals or exceptional sources can exceed normal limits.</p><p>The Arcane Realm is not used in Middle-earth. RMU JSON data must nevertheless remain intact: the restriction applies to access to knowledge, not to deleting spells.</p></section><h2>The Gradual Withdrawal of Magic</h2><p>Middle-earth is not a world in which magical knowledge remains stable. Masters die without passing on all they know, peoples depart from the world, kingdoms and libraries vanish, and some forms of power become increasingly rare.</p><h2>First Age</h2><p>In the First Age, Spell Lists can be known up to level 50. This value means that the knowledge still exists in the world; it obviously does not grant level-50 spells to every caster.</p><h2>Available Knowledge and Personal Capability</h2><p>The maximum availability of a List is not the character's level. It defines how far a tradition can theoretically be learned. The character must still purchase ranks and satisfy all other RMU requirements.</p><h2>Second Age</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Maximum Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The range notation represents a gradual decline; it does not mean that the whole world suddenly changes its ceiling on a single date.</p><h2>Third Age</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Maximum Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr></tbody></table><p>By the end of the Third Age, knowledge allowing the use of high-level spells becomes extremely rare. Ancient individuals, objects, or texts may nevertheless still preserve it.</p><h2>Fourth Age</h2><p>In the Fourth Age, magic almost disappears. The source does not provide a detailed table comparable to those of earlier periods: no precise numerical progression should be invented without another source.</p><h2>A General Trend, Not an Absolute Law</h2><p>The rate of decline is not a uniform standard for every Culture. Peoples with ancient traditions, long-lived masters, or a deep relationship with magic preserve more knowledge; communities without structured magical institutions generally preserve less.</p><h2>Elven Peoples</h2><p>Elven peoples probably retain the greatest amount of knowledge. Their longevity and relationship with Nolwë allow very ancient knowledge to be transmitted directly.</p><h2>Suggested Limits for T.A. 1640</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Suggested Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Elves</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Khazâd</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Men</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These values show how the historical trend should be combined with racial and cultural differences. Powerful NPCs such as Elrond or Galadriel may exceed the normal Elven ceiling.</p><h2>Hillmen</h2><p>The source provides a more restrictive example for the Hillmen: Base Lists up to level 8 and other Lists up to level 5. This example shows that availability can vary according to both Culture <em>and</em> List type.</p><h2>Base Lists and Local Traditions</h2><p>A Culture with a local magical tradition may preserve its Profession Lists better than the Realm's general Lists. Availability should therefore not be reduced to a single racial ceiling.</p><h2>Individuals Who Inherit Earlier Ages</h2><p>The ceilings do not necessarily apply to beings who personally preserved knowledge from earlier Ages, such as Elrond, Galadriel, or Sauron. They may know spells beyond general availability and may potentially transmit some of that knowledge.</p><h2>Teaching by an Ancient Master</h2><p>A character living in a period when a List is normally known only up to level 10 may encounter a master able to teach beyond that limit. Decline means that the knowledge has been lost from general awareness, not that it has become cosmologically impossible.</p><h2>Ancient Texts, Ruins, and Rediscovery</h2><p>A tome from an earlier Age, a lost library, an inscription, or an ancient object can preserve knowledge beyond the local ceiling. The character must still invest the necessary ranks; the discovery merely makes learning possible.</p><h2>Availability and RMU Ranks</h2><p>RMU uses ranks in a List to determine the maximum spell level accessible. MERP-RMU adds that the character normally cannot develop knowledge beyond the level available to their Age and tradition without an exceptional source.</p><h2>Do Not Truncate the JSON</h2><p>RMU <code>spell-list</code> Items must retain all their spells, including high-level ones. They may be needed for other Ages, exceptional NPCs, ancient objects, or extraordinary discoveries. The restriction applies to character access, never to the technical content of the List.</p><h2>Availability as Metadata</h2><p>Availability can be represented through a separate MERP layer: general limit by Age, racial and cultural exceptions, a specific ceiling for Base Lists, and exceptional personal sources.</p><h2>Age and Campaign Year</h2><p>A precise campaign year can determine the appropriate period. Any annual interpolation between the values in the table would be a MERP-RMU tool, not a formula explicitly provided by the source, and should be presented as such.</p><h2>Allowed Professions</h2><p>The Base Lists available depend on the Professions allowed to the Race or Culture. This chain can be represented simply: Race/Culture → allowed Profession → accessible Base Lists.</p><h2>Open, Restricted, and Evil Lists</h2><p>The RMU classification describes cost and technical status, not the automatic presence of a teacher in every region. An Open List may be locally absent; a Restricted List requires rarer access; an Evil List may exist without being accessible to the Free Peoples and may also be Sorcery.</p><h2>Arcane Realm</h2><p><strong>The Arcane Realm is not used in Middle-earth and no Arcane List may be selected during normal character creation.</strong> Native RMU data must nevertheless remain present; MERP-RMU applies a contextual restriction rather than modifying the Core.</p><h2>Hybrid Professions</h2><p>Hybrid Professions use their Profession Lists within the adopted organisation. Their availability follows the Profession, the Realms involved, and the traditions capable of transmitting the corresponding Lists.</p><h2>Partial Knowledge</h2><p>A List may be known only up to a certain level. A tradition may preserve Fire Law up to level 8 while having lost the higher levels. This partial representation is much more natural than removing the List entirely.</p><h2>Objects as Survivals from the Past</h2><p>A weapon from the First Age may produce an effect that no Third Age craftsman knows how to reproduce. The existence of an object therefore does not prove that the knowledge required to make it is still available.</p><h2>Historical, Cultural, Local, and Personal Availability</h2><p>It is useful to distinguish four layers: historical — the general level of the period; cultural — knowledge preserved by the Race or Culture; local — a source actually accessible in the location; personal — a master, book, inheritance, or other exception specific to the character.</p><h2>A Rarity Mechanic Rather Than a Prohibition</h2><p>Decline should create rarity and adventure objectives. When a character wants to advance beyond what their tradition knows, the answer should be “you need to find an older source” rather than a simple “forbidden.”</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>When purchasing a List rank, the module may check the Age, year, Race, Culture, Profession, List type, and personal exceptions. By default, displaying a warning is preferable to an absolute block; a strict mode may be offered.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Decline functions as a rule of transmission, rarity, and access to knowledge. It allows the magic of ancient Ages to remain genuinely superior without deleting RMU data or preventing characters from seeking and restoring some of the lost knowledge.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Decline and Availability of Spell Lists","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Decline and Availability of Spell Lists</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magical knowledge declines as the Ages pass. The general knowledge ceiling falls from level 50 in the First Age to about 5 by the end of the Third Age.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First Age</td><td>up to 50</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth Age</td><td>magic almost gone; no numerical table here</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These ceilings are trends. At T.A. 1640: <strong>Elves 30, Khazâd 25, Men 12</strong>. Ancient individuals or exceptional sources can exceed normal limits.</p><p>The Arcane Realm is not used in Middle-earth. RMU JSON data must nevertheless remain intact: the restriction applies to access to knowledge, not to deleting spells.</p></section><h2>The Gradual Withdrawal of Magic</h2><p>Middle-earth is not a world in which magical knowledge remains stable. Masters die without passing on all they know, peoples depart from the world, kingdoms and libraries vanish, and some forms of power become increasingly rare.</p><h2>First Age</h2><p>In the First Age, Spell Lists can be known up to level 50. This value means that the knowledge still exists in the world; it obviously does not grant level-50 spells to every caster.</p><h2>Available Knowledge and Personal Capability</h2><p>The maximum availability of a List is not the character's level. It defines how far a tradition can theoretically be learned. The character must still purchase ranks and satisfy all other RMU requirements.</p><h2>Second Age</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Maximum Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S.A. 1–1147</td><td>50 → 30</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 1147–2294</td><td>30 → 25</td></tr><tr><td>S.A. 2294–3441</td><td>25 → 20</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The range notation represents a gradual decline; it does not mean that the whole world suddenly changes its ceiling on a single date.</p><h2>Third Age</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>General Maximum Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T.A. 1–1007</td><td>20 → 15</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 1007–2014</td><td>15 → 10</td></tr><tr><td>T.A. 2014–3021</td><td>10 → 5</td></tr></tbody></table><p>By the end of the Third Age, knowledge allowing the use of high-level spells becomes extremely rare. Ancient individuals, objects, or texts may nevertheless still preserve it.</p><h2>Fourth Age</h2><p>In the Fourth Age, magic almost disappears. The source does not provide a detailed table comparable to those of earlier periods: no precise numerical progression should be invented without another source.</p><h2>A General Trend, Not an Absolute Law</h2><p>The rate of decline is not a uniform standard for every Culture. Peoples with ancient traditions, long-lived masters, or a deep relationship with magic preserve more knowledge; communities without structured magical institutions generally preserve less.</p><h2>Elven Peoples</h2><p>Elven peoples probably retain the greatest amount of knowledge. Their longevity and relationship with Nolwë allow very ancient knowledge to be transmitted directly.</p><h2>Suggested Limits for T.A. 1640</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Suggested Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Elves</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Khazâd</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Men</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These values show how the historical trend should be combined with racial and cultural differences. Powerful NPCs such as Elrond or Galadriel may exceed the normal Elven ceiling.</p><h2>Hillmen</h2><p>The source provides a more restrictive example for the Hillmen: Base Lists up to level 8 and other Lists up to level 5. This example shows that availability can vary according to both Culture <em>and</em> List type.</p><h2>Base Lists and Local Traditions</h2><p>A Culture with a local magical tradition may preserve its Profession Lists better than the Realm's general Lists. Availability should therefore not be reduced to a single racial ceiling.</p><h2>Individuals Who Inherit Earlier Ages</h2><p>The ceilings do not necessarily apply to beings who personally preserved knowledge from earlier Ages, such as Elrond, Galadriel, or Sauron. They may know spells beyond general availability and may potentially transmit some of that knowledge.</p><h2>Teaching by an Ancient Master</h2><p>A character living in a period when a List is normally known only up to level 10 may encounter a master able to teach beyond that limit. Decline means that the knowledge has been lost from general awareness, not that it has become cosmologically impossible.</p><h2>Ancient Texts, Ruins, and Rediscovery</h2><p>A tome from an earlier Age, a lost library, an inscription, or an ancient object can preserve knowledge beyond the local ceiling. The character must still invest the necessary ranks; the discovery merely makes learning possible.</p><h2>Availability and RMU Ranks</h2><p>RMU uses ranks in a List to determine the maximum spell level accessible. MERP-RMU adds that the character normally cannot develop knowledge beyond the level available to their Age and tradition without an exceptional source.</p><h2>Do Not Truncate the JSON</h2><p>RMU <code>spell-list</code> Items must retain all their spells, including high-level ones. They may be needed for other Ages, exceptional NPCs, ancient objects, or extraordinary discoveries. The restriction applies to character access, never to the technical content of the List.</p><h2>Availability as Metadata</h2><p>Availability can be represented through a separate MERP layer: general limit by Age, racial and cultural exceptions, a specific ceiling for Base Lists, and exceptional personal sources.</p><h2>Age and Campaign Year</h2><p>A precise campaign year can determine the appropriate period. Any annual interpolation between the values in the table would be a MERP-RMU tool, not a formula explicitly provided by the source, and should be presented as such.</p><h2>Allowed Professions</h2><p>The Base Lists available depend on the Professions allowed to the Race or Culture. This chain can be represented simply: Race/Culture → allowed Profession → accessible Base Lists.</p><h2>Open, Restricted, and Evil Lists</h2><p>The RMU classification describes cost and technical status, not the automatic presence of a teacher in every region. An Open List may be locally absent; a Restricted List requires rarer access; an Evil List may exist without being accessible to the Free Peoples and may also be Sorcery.</p><h2>Arcane Realm</h2><p><strong>The Arcane Realm is not used in Middle-earth and no Arcane List may be selected during normal character creation.</strong> Native RMU data must nevertheless remain present; MERP-RMU applies a contextual restriction rather than modifying the Core.</p><h2>Hybrid Professions</h2><p>Hybrid Professions use their Profession Lists within the adopted organisation. Their availability follows the Profession, the Realms involved, and the traditions capable of transmitting the corresponding Lists.</p><h2>Partial Knowledge</h2><p>A List may be known only up to a certain level. A tradition may preserve Fire Law up to level 8 while having lost the higher levels. This partial representation is much more natural than removing the List entirely.</p><h2>Objects as Survivals from the Past</h2><p>A weapon from the First Age may produce an effect that no Third Age craftsman knows how to reproduce. The existence of an object therefore does not prove that the knowledge required to make it is still available.</p><h2>Historical, Cultural, Local, and Personal Availability</h2><p>It is useful to distinguish four layers: historical — the general level of the period; cultural — knowledge preserved by the Race or Culture; local — a source actually accessible in the location; personal — a master, book, inheritance, or other exception specific to the character.</p><h2>A Rarity Mechanic Rather Than a Prohibition</h2><p>Decline should create rarity and adventure objectives. When a character wants to advance beyond what their tradition knows, the answer should be “you need to find an older source” rather than a simple “forbidden.”</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>When purchasing a List rank, the module may check the Age, year, Race, Culture, Profession, List type, and personal exceptions. By default, displaying a warning is preferable to an absolute block; a strict mode may be offered.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Decline functions as a rule of transmission, rarity, and access to knowledge. It allows the magic of ancient Ages to remain genuinely superior without deleting RMU data or preventing characters from seeking and restoring some of the lost knowledge.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"9J9AY3Wu8PAIcSPf"}
{"name":"Economy","sort":2000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"economie","section":"regles","compendiumSourceKey":"economie","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-economie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Économie","pages":[{"name":"Économie","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Économie</h1>\n<section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>L’économie de la Terre du Milieu repose sur <strong>trois grands systèmes d’échange et de redistribution</strong> qui se combinent différemment selon les peuples et les régions : le <strong>troc</strong>, le <strong>marché monétaire</strong> et l’<strong>échange hiérarchique</strong> fondé sur la parenté, la fidélité, la dépendance et l’obligation.</p><p>La monnaie est surtout courante dans les grandes villes, les régions commerçantes et les sociétés disposant de réseaux d’échanges importants. Dans de nombreuses campagnes et chez plusieurs Cultures, le <strong>troc demeure le mode d’échange ordinaire</strong>, y compris à l’intérieur de royaumes utilisant officiellement des pièces.</p><p>Une pièce possède principalement la valeur du <strong>métal précieux qu’elle contient</strong>. Une monnaie provenant d’un royaume disparu peut donc rester valable si son poids et sa pureté sont reconnus. La présence de pièces ne garantit jamais qu’un vendeur les accepte : dans une société qui mesure la richesse en troupeaux, nourriture, armes, terres ou relations de fidélité, une bourse d’or peut être beaucoup moins utile qu’un cheval, une couverture ou plusieurs jours de vivres.</p></section>\n<h2>Une économie différente selon les peuples</h2><p>La Terre du Milieu ne possède pas un système économique uniforme. Les grandes cités, les royaumes ruraux, les communautés forestières, les tribus nomades et les refuges elfiques organisent la richesse selon des principes souvent très différents.</p><p>Dans les plus grandes villes, comme <strong>Minas Tirith, Minas Ithil, Tharbad, Annúminas ou Fornost</strong>, payer des biens avec des pièces constitue une pratique ordinaire. Dès que l’on quitte ces grands centres, les échanges deviennent beaucoup plus variés. Les campagnes d’Arnor ou de Gondor utilisent volontiers le troc, tandis que certains peuples n’accordent qu’une importance limitée à la monnaie.</p><p>Le prix inscrit sur une fiche d’équipement doit être compris comme une valeur de référence et non comme la garantie qu’un marchand acceptera cette somme sous n’importe quelle forme. Une épée peut valoir plusieurs pièces d’argent dans une ville du Gondor, tandis qu’un village éloigné peut préférer recevoir du grain, du bétail, des outils ou une obligation de service.</p>\n<h2>Les trois grands systèmes économiques</h2><p>Les sociétés de la Terre du Milieu utilisent principalement trois formes d’organisation économique : <strong>le troc</strong>, <strong>le marché monétaire</strong> et <strong>l’échange hiérarchique fondé sur l’obligation et le don</strong>. La plupart des Cultures combinent ces trois formes dans des proportions différentes. Les Eldar utilisent presque exclusivement le système hiérarchique, les Dúnedain combinent obligations féodales, monnaie et troc, tandis que les Woses reposent essentiellement sur le troc.</p>\n<h2>Le troc</h2><p>Le <strong>troc</strong> constitue la forme d’échange la plus répandue en Terre du Milieu. Deux personnes échangent directement ce qu’elles possèdent ou ce qu’elles peuvent produire. Une herboriste des Hommes des Bois peut échanger plusieurs préparations médicinales contre un manteau de laine, un berger d’Eriador céder une balle de laine contre un sac de céréales et un chasseur Lossoth proposer une carcasse contre un renne dressé.</p><p>La valeur n’est donc pas nécessairement exprimée sous la forme d’un prix fixe. Elle dépend de ce que chacun possède et surtout de ce dont chacun a besoin.</p>\n<h2>La valeur dépend du besoin</h2><p>Dans une économie de troc, un objet possède une valeur fortement liée aux circonstances. Un sac de grain peut être banal après une bonne récolte et extrêmement précieux pendant un hiver difficile. Une arme peut représenter une richesse importante dans une communauté menacée par des raids. Un cheval dressé possède peu d’utilité pour une communauté vivant dans des marais ou des montagnes, tandis qu’il peut représenter plusieurs années de richesse pour une tribu de cavaliers.</p><p>Les valeurs monétaires RMU doivent donc servir d’indication générale plutôt que de taux de conversion universel. La valeur réelle dépend de l’endroit, de la saison, de la rareté et des besoins locaux.</p>\n<h2>Le troc dans les régions monétaires</h2><p>L’usage de la monnaie ne fait pas disparaître le troc. Même dans les régions où les pièces sont courantes, de nombreux habitants continuent à échanger directement des biens. Un paysan peut payer le forgeron avec du grain, un aubergiste accepter du gibier en échange d’un repas et un propriétaire de chevaux recevoir du fourrage ou des services plutôt que des pièces.</p>\n<h2>La monnaie n’est pas toujours acceptée</h2><p>Une personne issue d’une société qui pratique exclusivement le troc peut n’avoir aucune raison pratique d’accepter de l’or ou de l’argent. Un guerrier Easterling possédant un excellent cheval peut considérer qu’un autre cheval, une tente, des armes ou du bétail possèdent une valeur claire, tandis qu’une poignée de pièces peut lui sembler inutile si personne dans son circuit nomade ne les accepte.</p><p>La question économique centrale devient alors : <strong>que pourra faire le vendeur avec ce que les personnages lui proposent ?</strong> Une pièce n’a de valeur réelle que si elle peut ensuite être échangée.</p>\n<h2>Isolement et acceptation de la monnaie</h2><p>Le degré d’<strong>isolement</strong> joue un rôle majeur dans l’acceptation des pièces. Une communauté en contact régulier avec des marchands étrangers peut accepter des monnaies qu’elle ne frappe pas elle-même. Un village situé sur une route commerciale peut connaître les monnaies du Gondor ou du Rhovanion, tandis qu’une tribu rencontrant un marchand étranger une fois tous les dix ans peut n’y voir aucun intérêt.</p>\n<h2>Nomades et monnaie</h2><p>Le mode de vie nomade ne produit pas une attitude unique envers la monnaie. Certaines tribus de cavaliers mesurent principalement leur richesse en <strong>chevaux, troupeaux, armes et dépendants</strong> et utilisent très peu les pièces. D’autres peuples nomades sont eux-mêmes commerçants et trouvent au contraire dans les <strong>pièces, bijoux et pierres précieuses</strong> une manière pratique de transporter une grande richesse sous un faible volume.</p>\n<h2>La monnaie comme métal précieux</h2><p>Une pièce de monnaie de Terre du Milieu représente principalement une <strong>quantité déterminée de métal précieux</strong>. Sa valeur repose sur son poids, sa matière et sa pureté plutôt que sur la garantie abstraite de l’État qui l’a émise. Une pièce ancienne peut rester valable, une pièce étrangère peut être acceptée et une pièce endommagée peut conserver une partie de sa valeur.</p><p>Les changeurs, marchands et artisans capables d’évaluer la qualité des métaux occupent donc une place importante dans les économies développées.</p>\n<h2>Les monnaies anciennes</h2><p>La disparition d’un royaume n’annule pas nécessairement la valeur de sa monnaie. Une pièce de Númenor ou d’un ancien royaume dúnadan peut rester un morceau parfaitement valable d’or ou d’argent plusieurs siècles après la chute de l’autorité qui l’avait frappée. Sa valeur peut même dépasser celle de son métal si elle possède une qualité exceptionnelle, une origine prestigieuse, une valeur historique ou une rareté particulière.</p>\n<h2>Poids et pureté</h2><p>L’utilisation de monnaies étrangères encourage la vérification du <strong>poids</strong> et de la <strong>pureté</strong>. Un marchand expérimenté peut utiliser une petite balance, un changeur peut connaître les poids habituels des différentes monnaies et un artisan peut tester le métal. Une pièce douteuse peut être acceptée seulement pour une fraction de sa valeur déclarée.</p>\n<h2>Le marché monétaire</h2><p>Le marché monétaire apparaît surtout dans les régions où les échanges sont suffisamment nombreux et réguliers pour rendre la monnaie utile. Les grandes villes disposent de nombreux artisans spécialisés et de marchandises provenant de régions différentes. Un acheteur peut y vendre un objet à une personne puis utiliser l’argent obtenu auprès d’une autre.</p><p>La monnaie facilite ainsi les économies urbaines, les marchés importants et le commerce à longue distance.</p>\n<h2>Les grandes villes</h2><p>Dans les grandes cités, l’usage des pièces est suffisamment courant pour que les personnages puissent généralement acheter nourriture, logement, vêtements, équipement et services directement. Les prix RMU prennent alors tout leur sens comme référence sans devenir fixes. La rareté, la qualité, les taxes, les événements politiques et l’approvisionnement peuvent toujours les faire varier.</p>\n<h2>Marchés et foires</h2><p>Les marchés temporaires et les grandes foires jouent un rôle essentiel entre économie rurale et économie urbaine. Des paysans peuvent y vendre leur surplus, des artisans acheter des matières premières et des marchands itinérants échanger des biens provenant de régions éloignées. La monnaie y circule davantage que dans les villages environnants, mais le troc reste fréquent.</p>\n<h2>Bijoux et pierres précieuses</h2><p>Les bijoux et pierres précieuses constituent une forme de richesse particulièrement utile lorsque les pièces sont peu pratiques. Ils concentrent une grande valeur dans un faible volume. Un bracelet d’or peut fonctionner simultanément comme ornement, réserve de métal précieux, symbole social, cadeau diplomatique et moyen d’échange.</p>\n<h2>L’échange hiérarchique</h2><p>Le troisième grand système économique repose sur les <strong>liens de parenté, de fidélité et d’obligation</strong>. Un seigneur doit fournir certaines ressources à ses vassaux, qui doivent à leur tour soutenir leurs propres dépendants. Cette organisation distribue la richesse sans nécessairement recourir à l’achat.</p><p>Un souverain fournit nourriture, logement, vêtements, outils, armes, terres et protection ; ses vassaux lui doivent service, loyauté, présence militaire, tribut, travail ou soutien politique.</p>\n<h2>Obligation du seigneur et devoir du vassal</h2><p>Un personnage lié à un seigneur ne doit pas nécessairement acheter personnellement tout son équipement. Un guerrier appartenant à la maison d’un noble peut recevoir son armure et ses armes, un serviteur être nourri et logé, un officier obtenir une monture et un administrateur recevoir une maison et des terres attachées à sa fonction.</p><p>Ces biens ne constituent pas toujours une propriété personnelle librement revendable. Ils représentent souvent les moyens que le seigneur fournit afin que son dépendant puisse accomplir ses devoirs. Le bénéficiaire doit en retour respecter les obligations attachées à son statut.</p>\n<h2>Don et récompense</h2><p>Le <strong>don</strong> joue une place majeure dans ce système. Un seigneur peut récompenser un acte remarquable avec des pierres précieuses, des bijoux, des vêtements somptueux, du mobilier, des terres, un domaine, un cheval exceptionnel ou le droit de recevoir lui-même les serments d’autres dépendants.</p><p>La valeur de ces récompenses dépasse souvent leur prix marchand puisqu’elles représentent également l’honneur, la réputation, la confiance du souverain et le rang social.</p>\n<h2>Richesse et prestige</h2><p>Dans de nombreuses Cultures, la richesse est étroitement liée au <strong>prestige</strong>. Posséder beaucoup de pièces peut être moins important que posséder un vaste troupeau, avoir de nombreux dépendants, porter un bijou offert par un roi, disposer d’une grande maison, posséder une terre fertile ou être reconnu comme le chef d’une famille importante.</p>\n<h2>Les Eldar</h2><p>Les <strong>Eldar</strong> constituent l’exemple le plus pur d’une économie reposant sur les liens hiérarchiques et communautaires. Les invités étrangers doivent normalement être entretenus par des <strong>dons et l’hospitalité</strong> pendant leur séjour. Un visiteur ne doit donc pas s’attendre à trouver une auberge elfique où chaque repas possède un prix affiché.</p><p>Recevoir quelqu’un signifie prendre temporairement la responsabilité de ses besoins. Essayer de payer immédiatement chaque service dans un refuge eldar peut paraître étrange ; un cadeau approprié ou un service rendu ultérieurement peut avoir beaucoup plus de sens.</p>\n<h2>Les Khazâd</h2><p>Les <strong>Khazâd</strong> combinent une économie monétaire très développée avec certains échanges hiérarchiques. Le roi et les grands seigneurs peuvent distribuer des ressources ou des récompenses à leurs dépendants, tandis que les transactions ordinaires reposent largement sur un marché utilisant les pièces. Le troc y reste relativement rare.</p><p>Les Khazâd comprennent particulièrement bien le poids, la pureté, la valeur métallique et la qualité artisanale, ce qui les rend naturellement à l’aise avec les monnaies étrangères et les métaux précieux.</p>\n<h2>Les Umli</h2><p>Les <strong>Umli</strong> utilisent certaines formes limitées d’échange hiérarchique, tandis que leurs transactions ordinaires reposent principalement sur le <strong>troc</strong>. Les pièces étrangères peuvent donc posséder beaucoup moins d’intérêt que des biens immédiatement utiles.</p>\n<h2>Les Avari</h2><p>Chez les <strong>Avari</strong>, les formes économiques varient selon les communautés. Certaines se rapprochent du système hiérarchique eldar, tandis que d’autres utilisent principalement le troc. Les marchés fondés sur la monnaie restent rares et chaque Culture avarine peut recevoir sa propre interprétation économique.</p>\n<h2>Les Hobbits</h2><p>Les <strong>Hobbits</strong> utilisent largement les marchés fondés sur les pièces tout en conservant certaines pratiques de troc et quelques relations hiérarchiques liées historiquement au Roi d’Arnor et au Thain. La monnaie convient particulièrement bien à une société composée de fermiers, artisans, aubergistes et petits propriétaires qui échangent régulièrement entre eux.</p>\n<h2>Les Beornides</h2><p>L’économie des <strong>Beornides</strong> repose largement sur les relations hiérarchiques et communautaires, accompagnées de troc. Après la Quête d’Erebor, les seigneurs beornides acceptent davantage la monnaie des voyageurs, montrant comment une Culture peut modifier progressivement ses pratiques économiques lorsque les routes commerciales se développent.</p>\n<h2>Les Dúnedain et les Gondoriens</h2><p>Les <strong>Dúnedain</strong> combinent fortement les trois systèmes. Les obligations entre souverain, vassaux et dépendants restent importantes, tandis que les grands marchés utilisent largement la monnaie et que le troc demeure fréquent. Un chevalier dúnadan peut recevoir ses terres de son seigneur, acheter une épée avec des pièces et régler une dette locale en donnant plusieurs sacs de grain.</p><p>Chez les <strong>Gondoriens</strong>, le marché monétaire est particulièrement répandu. Les liens hiérarchiques continuent à organiser certaines relations entre nobles, vassaux et dépendants, tandis que le troc reste présent dans les campagnes.</p>\n<h2>Les Eriadoriens et les Dunlendings</h2><p>Les <strong>Eriadoriens</strong> reposent largement sur le troc. Certaines relations hiérarchiques subsistent dans les territoires liés aux traditions arnoriques, tandis que les villages utilisent parfois des pièces.</p><p>Les <strong>Dunlendings</strong> utilisent principalement le troc. Les échanges hiérarchiques existent surtout pour assurer nourriture et logement aux membres du groupe, tandis que certaines tribus en contact avec l’extérieur acceptent des pièces. Les communautés les plus isolées peuvent les refuser entièrement.</p>\n<h2>Les Dorwinrim</h2><p>Les <strong>Dorwinrim</strong> combinent un marché monétaire actif et un troc important. Cette combinaison convient à une Culture fortement tournée vers le commerce, la production spécialisée et les échanges avec des voisins différents. Les marchands dorwinrim peuvent donc être particulièrement à l’aise avec les monnaies étrangères, le change, les bijoux, les contrats et les cargaisons.</p>\n<h2>Les Haradrim</h2><p>Chez les <strong>Haradrim</strong>, l’économie combine plusieurs pratiques. Les richesses issues des raids peuvent être redistribuées selon des principes hiérarchiques, tandis que les échanges commerciaux utilisent volontiers bijoux d’or, métaux précieux et monnaies. Pour des commerçants voyageant sur de longues distances, les formes compactes de richesse sont particulièrement pratiques.</p>\n<h2>Les Easterlings</h2><p>Les pratiques des <strong>Easterlings</strong> varient selon les tribus, mais beaucoup utilisent principalement le troc et refusent les pièces. La richesse peut être mesurée en chevaux, bétail, armes, tentes, bijoux, dépendants ou capacités à entretenir un groupe. Les traditions commerciales et les routes peuvent cependant produire des comportements différents selon les peuples.</p>\n<h2>Les Lossoth</h2><p>Les <strong>Lossoth</strong> utilisent essentiellement le <strong>troc</strong>. Leur environnement rend particulièrement importantes les ressources immédiatement utiles : nourriture, fourrures, outils, armes, animaux et matériaux adaptés au froid. Une bourse pleine de pièces peut donc constituer une richesse théorique dont le personnage aura beaucoup de mal à faire usage.</p>\n<h2>Les Rohirrim</h2><p>Les <strong>Rohirrim</strong> combinent plusieurs systèmes. La famille et les relations hiérarchiques prennent en charge certains besoins, le Roi garantit notamment l’équipement militaire de certains officiers et de leurs forces, les marchés utilisent largement les pièces d’argent et le troc reste répandu. Les chevaux possèdent naturellement une importance économique et symbolique considérable.</p>\n<h2>Les Hommes des Bois et les Woses</h2><p>Les <strong>Hommes des Bois</strong> utilisent essentiellement le troc, bien qu’ils puissent accepter certaines pièces lorsqu’ils sont en contact avec des voyageurs. Les biens utiles à la vie forestière possèdent naturellement une grande valeur : outils, armes, miel, fourrures, herbes, produits du bois et nourriture conservable.</p><p>Les <strong>Woses</strong> reposent exclusivement sur le troc et n’acceptent normalement pas la monnaie. Proposer davantage d’or ne rend pas automatiquement une transaction plus attractive ; il faut comprendre ce dont la communauté a réellement besoin.</p>\n<h2>Les Variags, Orcs et Trolls</h2><p>Les <strong>Variags</strong> utilisent principalement le troc et n’accordent généralement pas de valeur aux pièces ; les biens issus de raids peuvent être répartis selon des principes hiérarchiques entre chefs et guerriers.</p><p>Chez les <strong>Orcs</strong>, la richesse circule principalement par la domination. Un chef fournit ressources et équipement à ses subordonnés, tandis que le vol, le pillage et la violence complètent ce système. Les Orcs acceptent volontiers les pièces surtout parce qu’ils comprennent qu’elles permettent d’obtenir des biens auprès des autres peuples.</p><p>Les <strong>Trolls sauvages</strong> ne disposent pas réellement d’un système d’échange volontaire organisé et obtiennent principalement leurs possessions par la force ou le vol. Les Trolls liés directement à l’organisation de Sauron utilisent davantage la distribution hiérarchique, tandis que les <strong>Demi-Trolls</strong> peuvent comprendre et valoriser les monnaies selon des principes plus proches des sociétés humaines.</p>\n<h2>Commerce à longue distance</h2><p>Le commerce à longue distance transforme profondément la valeur des biens. Les marchandises qui supportent bien le transport deviennent particulièrement importantes : épices, vin, métaux, bijoux, pierres précieuses, tissus fins, armes, herbes rares et objets artisanaux de haute qualité.</p><p>Transporter des céréales sur plusieurs centaines de kilomètres peut coûter davantage que leur valeur, tandis qu’une petite pierre précieuse peut représenter plusieurs mois de transactions. Les routes commerciales privilégient donc les marchandises possédant un fort rapport entre <strong>valeur et poids</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Transport, risque et routes</h2><p>Le coût économique d’un objet comprend également le risque nécessaire pour l’acheminer. Une route menacée par des Orcs augmente le prix des marchandises, un col fermé par la neige réduit l’approvisionnement et une guerre peut interrompre totalement un commerce régional.</p><p>Une route sûre produit progressivement davantage de marchands, davantage de monnaie, davantage de spécialisation artisanale et davantage de biens étrangers. Une route dangereuse favorise au contraire la production locale, le troc, les stocks et les prix élevés pour les produits importés.</p>\n<h2>Artisanat et nourriture</h2><p>La valeur des objets dépend fortement du <strong>temps et de la compétence nécessaires à leur fabrication</strong>. Une épée exige du minerai, du charbon, une forge, des outils et un artisan compétent. Une armure de qualité représente donc une concentration considérable de ressources et de savoir-faire.</p><p>La nourriture possède une importance économique fondamentale. Une armée consomme chaque jour d’énormes quantités de grain, viande, bière, fourrage et eau ; une grande ville dépend de son arrière-pays et une mauvaise récolte affecte les prix, les déplacements, les réserves et les capacités militaires.</p>\n<h2>Terres, services et obligations</h2><p>Dans les sociétés hiérarchiques, la <strong>terre</strong> représente souvent une forme de richesse beaucoup plus importante que l’argent liquide. Posséder une terre donne accès aux récoltes, aux loyers, aux troupeaux, au bois, aux mines, aux droits de passage et aux personnes qui y vivent.</p><p>Le travail et les compétences peuvent eux-mêmes servir de moyen d’échange. Un guérisseur peut recevoir logement et nourriture contre ses soins, un forgeron réparer une arme en échange d’un service et un guide accepter une part du butin. Toutes les dettes ne sont donc pas monétaires : un personnage peut devoir un service, une protection, un cadeau, une faveur ou une hospitalité future.</p>\n<h2>Le marchandage</h2><p>Le <strong>marchandage</strong> n’a pas le même rôle dans toutes les Cultures. Dans certains marchés, négocier le prix fait partie de la transaction. Dans d’autres communautés, insister sur le prix peut être perçu comme de l’avarice ou une insulte. Les compétences sociales doivent donc être utilisées en fonction des normes de la Culture concernée.</p>\n<h2>Estimation et information</h2><p>La compétence d’<strong>Estimation</strong> devient particulièrement importante dans une économie où les personnages rencontrent monnaies étrangères, bijoux, pierres précieuses, armes anciennes, objets artisanaux et marchandises inconnues. Une estimation réussie permet de comprendre approximativement ce que le personnage possède sans garantir qu’un acheteur acceptera automatiquement cette valeur.</p><p>Le marchand dispose également d’une ressource aussi importante que son argent : <strong>l’information</strong>. Il sait où une marchandise est rare ou abondante, quelles routes sont sûres, quelles monnaies sont acceptées, quels seigneurs prélèvent des taxes et quelles guerres vont perturber les échanges.</p>\n<h2>Impôts, péages et pillage</h2><p>Les royaumes et seigneurs peuvent prélever une partie de la richesse circulant sur leur territoire sous forme de pièces, produits agricoles, services, journées de travail, droits de passage ou parts de cargaison. Un péage n’a donc pas forcément besoin d’être payé en monnaie.</p><p>Le <strong>pillage</strong> constitue une forme importante de transfert de richesse dans les sociétés guerrières et parmi les forces de l’Ombre. Armes, bétail, nourriture, bijoux et captifs peuvent représenter un butin plus utile que les pièces, et le partage suit souvent des règles hiérarchiques précises.</p>\n<h2>Richesse des aventuriers et disponibilité</h2><p>Les aventuriers peuvent porter sur eux davantage de métal précieux que plusieurs familles rurales n’en voient pendant toute leur vie. Cette richesse ne garantit pourtant pas qu’ils puissent obtenir immédiatement tout ce qu’ils désirent. Un village peut ne pas posséder de cheval à vendre, d’armure disponible, de chambre supplémentaire ou de nourriture excédentaire.</p><p>La <strong>disponibilité</strong> doit donc rester distincte de la valeur : proposer dix fois le prix ne crée pas un objet qui n’existe pas.</p>\n<h2>Les trésors anciens</h2><p>Les trésors provenant d’anciens royaumes posent une situation particulière. Les pièces conservent leur valeur métallique, tandis que certains objets peuvent posséder une valeur historique ou artistique beaucoup plus élevée. Une coupe númenóréenne peut être simultanément de l’argent, une antiquité, un symbole dynastique et un objet recherché par des érudits.</p>\n<h2>Application aux prix RMU</h2><p>Les prix indiqués dans RMU doivent servir de <strong>base de référence commune</strong>. Dans une grande ville, le prix RMU peut correspondre approximativement au prix normal. Dans un marché rural, le même prix peut être exprimé en marchandises équivalentes. Dans une communauté isolée, l’objet peut être indisponible ou la monnaie refusée. En période de pénurie, le prix peut augmenter considérablement, tandis qu’il peut diminuer dans une région productrice.</p><p>Cette approche conserve l’utilité des tables RMU sans imposer une économie monétaire uniforme à toute la Terre du Milieu.</p>\n<h2>Application aux Cultures MERP-RMU</h2><p>Chaque Culture MERP-RMU pourra recevoir progressivement une métadonnée <strong>Économie</strong> comprenant au minimum le système dominant, l’usage de la monnaie, l’importance du troc, l’importance de l’échange hiérarchique, les formes de richesse privilégiées et l’attitude envers les monnaies étrangères.</p><p>Les Cultures déjà intégrées au module pourront ainsi exploiter directement ces informations : <strong>Woses</strong> — troc exclusif ; <strong>Khazâd</strong> — marché monétaire développé et troc rare ; <strong>Dúnedain</strong> — système mixte ; <strong>Eldar</strong> — économie du don et de l’obligation ; <strong>Lossoth</strong> — troc exclusif.</p>\n<h2>Application à Foundry</h2><p>Cette entrée peut servir de base à une adaptation légère du commerce dans MERP-UI sans construire une simulation économique lourde. Quelques informations simples suffisent : monnaie normalement acceptée, acceptée avec réserve ou non utilisée ; troc courant ; disponibilité locale ; coefficient contextuel éventuel sur les prix ; forme de richesse privilégiée par la Culture.</p>\n<h2>Économie et Culture</h2><p>L’économie doit être considérée comme une partie de la <strong>Culture</strong>, au même titre que les langues, les pratiques religieuses ou l’organisation sociale. Elle influence la manière dont un personnage comprend la richesse, la dette, la propriété, le don, le commerce et la récompense.</p><p>Un Noldo, un marchand dorwinrim et un chasseur Lossoth peuvent tous comprendre parfaitement la notion de valeur tout en ayant des conceptions radicalement différentes de la manière dont cette valeur doit circuler.</p>\n<h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>L’économie de la Terre du Milieu repose sur une combinaison variable de <strong>troc, marché monétaire et obligations hiérarchiques</strong>. La monnaie est particulièrement courante dans les grandes villes et les sociétés commerçantes, tandis que le troc demeure extrêmement répandu dans les campagnes et chez de nombreuses Cultures.</p><p>Les pièces valent principalement pour le métal précieux qu’elles contiennent, ce qui permet aux monnaies anciennes et étrangères de conserver une valeur longtemps après la disparition de leurs royaumes d’origine. Les relations de fidélité et de dépendance constituent une autre forme essentielle de circulation des richesses : un seigneur fournit terres, équipement, nourriture ou protection à ses dépendants, tandis que ceux-ci lui rendent service, loyauté ou soutien militaire.</p><p>MERP-RMU utilise donc les prix RMU comme des <strong>valeurs de référence</strong>, tout en laissant la Culture, le lieu, la rareté et les besoins déterminer la forme réelle de chaque transaction. Une bourse d’or peut ouvrir toutes les portes d’un grand marché et rester presque inutile quelques centaines de kilomètres plus loin dans une communauté qui mesure la richesse en chevaux, fourrures, nourriture, terres ou obligations.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Economy","pages":[{"name":"Economy","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Economy</h1>\n<section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>The economy of Middle-earth rests on <strong>three major systems of exchange and redistribution</strong>, combined differently according to people and region: <strong>barter</strong>, the <strong>monetary market</strong>, and <strong>hierarchical exchange</strong> based on kinship, loyalty, dependence, and obligation.</p><p>Coinage is most common in great cities, trading regions, and societies with substantial exchange networks. In many rural areas and among several Cultures, <strong>barter remains the ordinary form of exchange</strong>, even within kingdoms that officially use coinage.</p><p>A coin is worth primarily the <strong>precious metal it contains</strong>. Coinage from a vanished kingdom can therefore remain valuable if its weight and purity are recognised. The existence of coins never guarantees that a seller will accept them: in a society that measures wealth in herds, food, weapons, land, or relationships of loyalty, a purse of gold may be far less useful than a horse, a blanket, or several days of provisions.</p></section>\n<h2>Different Economies for Different Peoples</h2><p>Middle-earth does not possess a uniform economic system. Great cities, rural kingdoms, woodland communities, nomadic tribes, and Elven refuges organise wealth according to principles that are often very different.</p><p>In the largest cities, such as <strong>Minas Tirith, Minas Ithil, Tharbad, Annúminas, or Fornost</strong>, paying for goods with coins is ordinary practice. Once one leaves these great centres, exchanges become much more varied. The countryside of Arnor or Gondor readily uses barter, while some peoples attach only limited importance to coinage.</p><p>The price listed on an equipment sheet should be understood as a reference value, not as a guarantee that a merchant will accept that amount in any form. A sword may be worth several silver coins in a Gondorian city, while a remote village may prefer grain, livestock, tools, or an obligation of service.</p>\n<h2>The Three Major Economic Systems</h2><p>Societies in Middle-earth chiefly use three forms of economic organisation: <strong>barter</strong>, the <strong>monetary market</strong>, and <strong>hierarchical exchange based on obligation and gift-giving</strong>. Most Cultures combine these three forms in different proportions. The Eldar use the hierarchical system almost exclusively, the Dúnedain combine feudal obligations, coinage, and barter, while the Woses rely essentially on barter.</p>\n<h2>Barter</h2><p><strong>Barter</strong> is the most widespread form of exchange in Middle-earth. Two people directly exchange what they possess or can produce. A Woodman herbalist may trade several medicinal preparations for a wool cloak, an Eriadorian shepherd a bale of wool for a sack of grain, and a Lossoth hunter a carcass for a trained reindeer.</p><p>Value is therefore not necessarily expressed as a fixed price. It depends on what each person possesses and, above all, on what each person needs.</p>\n<h2>Value Depends on Need</h2><p>In a barter economy, the value of an object is strongly tied to circumstances. A sack of grain may be commonplace after a good harvest and extremely valuable during a hard winter. A weapon may represent considerable wealth in a community threatened by raids. A trained horse has little use to a people living in marshes or mountains, while it may represent several years of wealth to a tribe of riders.</p><p>RMU monetary values should therefore serve as general guidance rather than a universal conversion rate. Actual value depends on location, season, rarity, and local needs.</p>\n<h2>Barter in Monetary Regions</h2><p>The use of money does not eliminate barter. Even in regions where coins are common, many inhabitants continue to exchange goods directly. A farmer may pay the smith with grain, an innkeeper accept game in exchange for a meal, and a horse owner receive fodder or services rather than coin.</p>\n<h2>Coinage Is Not Always Accepted</h2><p>A person from a society that relies exclusively on barter may have no practical reason to accept gold or silver. An Easterling warrior who owns an excellent horse may clearly value another horse, a tent, weapons, or livestock, while a handful of coins may seem useless if nobody within that nomadic circuit accepts them.</p><p>The central economic question then becomes: <strong>what will the seller be able to do with what the characters offer?</strong> A coin has real value only if it can later be exchanged.</p>\n<h2>Isolation and Acceptance of Coinage</h2><p>The degree of <strong>isolation</strong> plays a major role in whether coins are accepted. A community in regular contact with foreign merchants may accept coinage it does not mint itself. A village on a trade road may recognise Gondorian or Rhovanion coins, while a tribe that meets a foreign merchant once every ten years may see no use in them.</p>\n<h2>Nomads and Coinage</h2><p>A nomadic way of life does not produce a single attitude toward money. Some riding tribes measure wealth primarily in <strong>horses, herds, weapons, and dependants</strong> and use coins very little. Other nomadic peoples are themselves traders and, on the contrary, find <strong>coins, jewellery, and gemstones</strong> a practical way of carrying great wealth in little space.</p>\n<h2>Coinage as Precious Metal</h2><p>A coin of Middle-earth primarily represents a <strong>defined quantity of precious metal</strong>. Its value rests on weight, material, and purity rather than on the abstract guarantee of the state that issued it. An ancient coin may remain valid, a foreign coin may be accepted, and a damaged coin may retain part of its value.</p><p>Moneychangers, merchants, and craftspeople capable of judging the quality of metals therefore occupy an important place in developed economies.</p>\n<h2>Ancient Coinage</h2><p>The disappearance of a kingdom does not necessarily cancel the value of its currency. A coin of Númenor or of an ancient Dúnedain realm can remain a perfectly valid piece of gold or silver centuries after the authority that struck it has fallen. Its value may even exceed that of its metal if it possesses exceptional workmanship, prestigious origin, historical significance, or unusual rarity.</p>\n<h2>Weight and Purity</h2><p>The use of foreign coins encourages the checking of <strong>weight</strong> and <strong>purity</strong>. An experienced merchant may use a small scale, a moneychanger may know the customary weights of different currencies, and a craftsman may test the metal. A doubtful coin may be accepted only for a fraction of its stated value.</p>\n<h2>The Monetary Market</h2><p>The monetary market appears chiefly in regions where exchanges are numerous and regular enough to make money useful. Great cities contain many specialised craftspeople and goods from different regions. A buyer can sell an item to one person and then spend the money obtained with another.</p><p>Money therefore facilitates urban economies, major markets, and long-distance trade.</p>\n<h2>Great Cities</h2><p>In great cities, coinage is common enough that characters can generally purchase food, lodging, clothing, equipment, and services directly. RMU prices then make full sense as references without becoming fixed. Rarity, quality, taxes, political events, and supply can still change them.</p>\n<h2>Markets and Fairs</h2><p>Temporary markets and great fairs play an essential role between rural and urban economies. Farmers can sell surpluses there, craftspeople buy raw materials, and travelling merchants exchange goods from distant regions. Coinage circulates more freely than in surrounding villages, but barter remains common.</p>\n<h2>Jewellery and Gemstones</h2><p>Jewellery and gemstones are a particularly useful form of wealth when coinage is impractical. They concentrate great value in little space. A gold bracelet can simultaneously function as ornament, reserve of precious metal, social symbol, diplomatic gift, and medium of exchange.</p>\n<h2>Hierarchical Exchange</h2><p>The third major economic system rests on <strong>ties of kinship, loyalty, and obligation</strong>. A lord must provide certain resources to vassals, who in turn must support their own dependants. This organisation distributes wealth without necessarily requiring purchase.</p><p>A ruler provides food, lodging, clothing, tools, weapons, land, and protection; vassals owe service, loyalty, military presence, tribute, labour, or political support in return.</p>\n<h2>The Lord's Obligation and the Vassal's Duty</h2><p>A character bound to a lord does not necessarily need to buy all personal equipment. A warrior belonging to a noble household may receive armour and weapons, a servant be fed and housed, an officer receive a mount, and an administrator be granted a house and land attached to the office.</p><p>Such goods are not always personal property that can be freely resold. They often represent the means provided by the lord so that a dependant can fulfil their duties. In return, the beneficiary must respect the obligations attached to that status.</p>\n<h2>Gift and Reward</h2><p><strong>Gift-giving</strong> plays a major role in this system. A lord may reward a remarkable deed with gemstones, jewellery, splendid clothing, furnishings, land, an estate, an exceptional horse, or the right to receive the oaths of dependants in turn.</p><p>The value of such rewards often exceeds their market price because they also represent honour, reputation, the ruler's trust, and social rank.</p>\n<h2>Wealth and Prestige</h2><p>In many Cultures, wealth is closely tied to <strong>prestige</strong>. Possessing many coins may be less important than owning a large herd, having numerous dependants, wearing jewellery given by a king, maintaining a great household, holding fertile land, or being recognised as head of an important family.</p>\n<h2>The Eldar</h2><p>The <strong>Eldar</strong> provide the clearest example of an economy based on hierarchical and communal ties. Foreign guests should normally be maintained through <strong>gifts and hospitality</strong> during their stay. A visitor should therefore not expect to find an Elven inn where every meal has a posted price.</p><p>Receiving someone means temporarily taking responsibility for their needs. Trying to pay immediately for every service in an Eldarin refuge may seem strange; an appropriate gift or a service rendered later may make far more sense.</p>\n<h2>The Khazâd</h2><p>The <strong>Khazâd</strong> combine a highly developed monetary economy with certain hierarchical exchanges. The king and great lords may distribute resources or rewards to their dependants, while ordinary transactions rely heavily on a market using coinage. Barter remains comparatively rare.</p><p>The Khazâd understand weight, purity, metal value, and quality of workmanship especially well, making them naturally comfortable with foreign currencies and precious metals.</p>\n<h2>The Umli</h2><p>The <strong>Umli</strong> use some limited forms of hierarchical exchange, while their ordinary transactions rely primarily on <strong>barter</strong>. Foreign coins may therefore be of far less interest than immediately useful goods.</p>\n<h2>The Avari</h2><p>Among the <strong>Avari</strong>, economic forms vary by community. Some resemble the Eldarin hierarchical system, while others rely mainly on barter. Coin-based markets remain rare, and each Avarin Culture may receive its own economic interpretation.</p>\n<h2>The Hobbits</h2><p>The <strong>Hobbits</strong> make broad use of markets based on coinage while preserving some barter practices and a few hierarchical relationships historically linked to the King of Arnor and the Thain. Money suits a society of farmers, craftspeople, innkeepers, and small landholders who trade with one another regularly.</p>\n<h2>The Beornings</h2><p>The economy of the <strong>Beornings</strong> rests largely on hierarchical and communal relationships accompanied by barter. After the Quest of Erebor, Beorning lords accept travellers' coinage more readily, showing how a Culture can gradually change its economic practices as trade routes develop.</p>\n<h2>The Dúnedain and Gondorians</h2><p>The <strong>Dúnedain</strong> strongly combine all three systems. Obligations between ruler, vassals, and dependants remain important, while great markets use coinage extensively and barter remains common. A Dúnedain knight may receive land from a lord, buy a sword with coins, and settle a local debt by giving several sacks of grain.</p><p>Among the <strong>Gondorians</strong>, the monetary market is especially widespread. Hierarchical ties continue to organise some relationships among nobles, vassals, and dependants, while barter remains present in the countryside.</p>\n<h2>Eriadorians and Dunlendings</h2><p>The <strong>Eriadorians</strong> rely heavily on barter. Some hierarchical relationships survive in territories linked to Arnorian traditions, while villages sometimes use coinage.</p><p>The <strong>Dunlendings</strong> rely mainly on barter. Hierarchical exchanges exist chiefly to provide food and shelter to members of the group, while some tribes in contact with outsiders accept coinage. The most isolated communities may reject it entirely.</p>\n<h2>The Dorwinrim</h2><p>The <strong>Dorwinrim</strong> combine an active monetary market with extensive barter. This combination suits a Culture strongly oriented toward trade, specialised production, and exchange with different neighbours. Dorwinrim merchants may therefore be especially comfortable with foreign currencies, moneychanging, jewellery, contracts, and cargoes.</p>\n<h2>The Haradrim</h2><p>Among the <strong>Haradrim</strong>, the economy combines several practices. Wealth gained through raids may be redistributed according to hierarchical principles, while commercial exchanges readily use gold jewellery, precious metals, and coinage. For merchants travelling long distances, compact forms of wealth are especially practical.</p>\n<h2>The Easterlings</h2><p>Practices among the <strong>Easterlings</strong> vary by tribe, but many rely primarily on barter and refuse coins. Wealth may be measured in horses, livestock, weapons, tents, jewellery, dependants, or the ability to support a group. Trading traditions and routes can nevertheless produce different behaviours among different peoples.</p>\n<h2>The Lossoth</h2><p>The <strong>Lossoth</strong> rely essentially on <strong>barter</strong>. Their environment makes immediately useful resources especially important: food, furs, tools, weapons, animals, and materials suited to the cold. A purse full of coins can therefore represent theoretical wealth that the character may find very difficult to use.</p>\n<h2>The Rohirrim</h2><p>The <strong>Rohirrim</strong> combine several systems. Family and hierarchical ties provide for some needs, the King notably guarantees military equipment for certain officers and their forces, markets use silver coinage widely, and barter remains common. Horses naturally possess enormous economic and symbolic importance.</p>\n<h2>Woodmen and Woses</h2><p>The <strong>Woodmen</strong> rely essentially on barter, although they may accept some coinage when dealing with travellers. Goods useful to forest life naturally have great value: tools, weapons, honey, furs, herbs, wood products, and food that keeps well.</p><p>The <strong>Woses</strong> rely exclusively on barter and normally do not accept money. Offering more gold does not automatically make a transaction more attractive; one must understand what the community actually needs.</p>\n<h2>Variags, Orcs, and Trolls</h2><p>The <strong>Variags</strong> rely mainly on barter and generally attach no value to coins; goods taken in raids may be distributed according to hierarchical principles among chiefs and warriors.</p><p>Among <strong>Orcs</strong>, wealth circulates mainly through domination. A chief provides resources and equipment to subordinates, while theft, plunder, and violence complete the system. Orcs readily accept coins chiefly because they understand that coins can obtain goods from other peoples.</p><p><strong>Wild Trolls</strong> do not really possess an organised system of voluntary exchange and obtain most possessions through force or theft. Trolls directly tied to Sauron's organisation make greater use of hierarchical distribution, while <strong>Half-trolls</strong> may understand and value coinage according to principles closer to human societies.</p>\n<h2>Long-Distance Trade</h2><p>Long-distance trade profoundly alters the value of goods. Commodities that withstand transport well become especially important: spices, wine, metals, jewellery, gemstones, fine cloth, weapons, rare herbs, and high-quality crafted objects.</p><p>Transporting grain several hundred kilometres may cost more than the grain itself, while a small gemstone can represent several months of transactions. Trade routes therefore favour goods with a high ratio of <strong>value to weight</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Transport, Risk, and Roads</h2><p>The economic cost of an object also includes the risk required to bring it to market. A road threatened by Orcs raises the price of goods, a pass closed by snow reduces supply, and war may interrupt regional trade entirely.</p><p>A safe road gradually produces more merchants, more money, greater craft specialisation, and more foreign goods. A dangerous road instead favours local production, barter, stockpiling, and high prices for imported goods.</p>\n<h2>Craft and Food</h2><p>The value of objects depends heavily on the <strong>time and skill required to make them</strong>. A sword requires ore, charcoal, a forge, tools, and a skilled craftsman. Quality armour therefore represents a considerable concentration of resources and expertise.</p><p>Food has fundamental economic importance. An army consumes enormous quantities of grain, meat, ale, fodder, and water each day; a great city depends on its hinterland, and a poor harvest affects prices, movement, reserves, and military capability.</p>\n<h2>Land, Services, and Obligations</h2><p>In hierarchical societies, <strong>land</strong> often represents a form of wealth far more important than liquid money. Owning land gives access to crops, rents, herds, timber, mines, passage rights, and the people who live upon it.</p><p>Labour and skills can themselves serve as media of exchange. A healer may receive food and lodging for treatment, a smith repair a weapon in exchange for a service, and a guide accept a share of treasure. Not all debts are monetary: a character may owe service, protection, a gift, a favour, or future hospitality.</p>\n<h2>Haggling</h2><p><strong>Haggling</strong> does not have the same role in every Culture. In some markets, negotiating the price is part of the transaction. In other communities, insisting over price may be seen as greed or an insult. Social Skills should therefore be used according to the norms of the Culture involved.</p>\n<h2>Appraisal and Information</h2><p>The <strong>Appraisal</strong> Skill becomes especially important in an economy where characters encounter foreign coins, jewellery, gemstones, ancient weapons, crafted objects, and unfamiliar goods. A successful appraisal provides an approximate understanding of what the character possesses without guaranteeing that a buyer will automatically accept that value.</p><p>A merchant also possesses a resource as important as money: <strong>information</strong>. Merchants know where a commodity is scarce or abundant, which roads are safe, which currencies are accepted, which lords levy taxes, and which wars will disrupt exchange.</p>\n<h2>Taxes, Tolls, and Plunder</h2><p>Kingdoms and lords may take part of the wealth moving through their territory in the form of coins, agricultural produce, services, days of labour, passage rights, or shares of cargo. A toll therefore does not necessarily have to be paid in money.</p><p><strong>Plunder</strong> is an important form of wealth transfer in warrior societies and among the forces of the Shadow. Weapons, livestock, food, jewellery, and captives may represent more useful booty than coin, and the division of spoils often follows precise hierarchical rules.</p>\n<h2>Adventurer Wealth and Availability</h2><p>Adventurers may carry more precious metal than several rural families see in their entire lives. Yet this wealth does not guarantee that they can immediately obtain whatever they desire. A village may have no horse for sale, no armour available, no extra room, or no surplus food.</p><p><strong>Availability</strong> must therefore remain distinct from value: offering ten times the price does not create an object that does not exist.</p>\n<h2>Ancient Treasures</h2><p>Treasures from ancient kingdoms present a special situation. Coins retain their metal value, while some objects may have much greater historical or artistic value. A Númenórean cup may simultaneously be silver, an antiquity, a dynastic symbol, and an object sought by scholars.</p>\n<h2>Application to RMU Prices</h2><p>Prices listed in RMU should serve as a <strong>common reference baseline</strong>. In a great city, the RMU price may approximately match the normal price. In a rural market, the same price may be expressed in equivalent goods. In an isolated community, the item may be unavailable or coin refused. In a time of shortage, the price may rise considerably, while in a producing region it may fall.</p><p>This approach preserves the usefulness of RMU tables without imposing a uniform monetary economy on all of Middle-earth.</p>\n<h2>Application to MERP-RMU Cultures</h2><p>Each MERP-RMU Culture may gradually receive <strong>Economy</strong> metadata containing at minimum the dominant system, use of coinage, importance of barter, importance of hierarchical exchange, preferred forms of wealth, and attitude toward foreign currencies.</p><p>Cultures already integrated into the module can thus use this information directly: <strong>Woses</strong> — barter only; <strong>Khazâd</strong> — developed monetary market and rare barter; <strong>Dúnedain</strong> — mixed system; <strong>Eldar</strong> — economy of gift and obligation; <strong>Lossoth</strong> — barter only.</p>\n<h2>Application to Foundry</h2><p>This entry can serve as the basis for a light adaptation of commerce in MERP-UI without building a heavy economic simulation. A few simple pieces of information are enough: coinage normally accepted, accepted with reservations, or not used; barter common; local availability; possible contextual price coefficient; preferred form of wealth for the Culture.</p>\n<h2>Economy and Culture</h2><p>Economy should be regarded as part of <strong>Culture</strong>, alongside languages, religious practices, or social organisation. It affects the way a character understands wealth, debt, property, gift-giving, trade, and reward.</p><p>A Noldo, a Dorwinrim merchant, and a Lossoth hunter can all understand the concept of value perfectly while holding radically different ideas about how that value should circulate.</p>\n<h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>The economy of Middle-earth rests on a variable combination of <strong>barter, monetary markets, and hierarchical obligations</strong>. Coinage is especially common in great cities and trading societies, while barter remains extremely widespread in the countryside and among many Cultures.</p><p>Coins are valued primarily for the precious metal they contain, allowing ancient and foreign currencies to retain value long after their kingdoms of origin have disappeared. Relationships of loyalty and dependence form another essential channel for the circulation of wealth: a lord provides land, equipment, food, or protection to dependants, while they return service, loyalty, or military support.</p><p>MERP-RMU therefore uses RMU prices as <strong>reference values</strong>, while allowing Culture, location, rarity, and need to determine the real form of each transaction. A purse of gold can open every door in a great market and remain almost useless a few hundred kilometres away in a community that measures wealth in horses, furs, food, land, or obligations.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["2 - Economie"],"en":["2 - Economy"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-02-economy"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Economy","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Economy</h1>\n<section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>The economy of Middle-earth rests on <strong>three major systems of exchange and redistribution</strong>, combined differently according to people and region: <strong>barter</strong>, the <strong>monetary market</strong>, and <strong>hierarchical exchange</strong> based on kinship, loyalty, dependence, and obligation.</p><p>Coinage is most common in great cities, trading regions, and societies with substantial exchange networks. In many rural areas and among several Cultures, <strong>barter remains the ordinary form of exchange</strong>, even within kingdoms that officially use coinage.</p><p>A coin is worth primarily the <strong>precious metal it contains</strong>. Coinage from a vanished kingdom can therefore remain valuable if its weight and purity are recognised. The existence of coins never guarantees that a seller will accept them: in a society that measures wealth in herds, food, weapons, land, or relationships of loyalty, a purse of gold may be far less useful than a horse, a blanket, or several days of provisions.</p></section>\n<h2>Different Economies for Different Peoples</h2><p>Middle-earth does not possess a uniform economic system. Great cities, rural kingdoms, woodland communities, nomadic tribes, and Elven refuges organise wealth according to principles that are often very different.</p><p>In the largest cities, such as <strong>Minas Tirith, Minas Ithil, Tharbad, Annúminas, or Fornost</strong>, paying for goods with coins is ordinary practice. Once one leaves these great centres, exchanges become much more varied. The countryside of Arnor or Gondor readily uses barter, while some peoples attach only limited importance to coinage.</p><p>The price listed on an equipment sheet should be understood as a reference value, not as a guarantee that a merchant will accept that amount in any form. A sword may be worth several silver coins in a Gondorian city, while a remote village may prefer grain, livestock, tools, or an obligation of service.</p>\n<h2>The Three Major Economic Systems</h2><p>Societies in Middle-earth chiefly use three forms of economic organisation: <strong>barter</strong>, the <strong>monetary market</strong>, and <strong>hierarchical exchange based on obligation and gift-giving</strong>. Most Cultures combine these three forms in different proportions. The Eldar use the hierarchical system almost exclusively, the Dúnedain combine feudal obligations, coinage, and barter, while the Woses rely essentially on barter.</p>\n<h2>Barter</h2><p><strong>Barter</strong> is the most widespread form of exchange in Middle-earth. Two people directly exchange what they possess or can produce. A Woodman herbalist may trade several medicinal preparations for a wool cloak, an Eriadorian shepherd a bale of wool for a sack of grain, and a Lossoth hunter a carcass for a trained reindeer.</p><p>Value is therefore not necessarily expressed as a fixed price. It depends on what each person possesses and, above all, on what each person needs.</p>\n<h2>Value Depends on Need</h2><p>In a barter economy, the value of an object is strongly tied to circumstances. A sack of grain may be commonplace after a good harvest and extremely valuable during a hard winter. A weapon may represent considerable wealth in a community threatened by raids. A trained horse has little use to a people living in marshes or mountains, while it may represent several years of wealth to a tribe of riders.</p><p>RMU monetary values should therefore serve as general guidance rather than a universal conversion rate. Actual value depends on location, season, rarity, and local needs.</p>\n<h2>Barter in Monetary Regions</h2><p>The use of money does not eliminate barter. Even in regions where coins are common, many inhabitants continue to exchange goods directly. A farmer may pay the smith with grain, an innkeeper accept game in exchange for a meal, and a horse owner receive fodder or services rather than coin.</p>\n<h2>Coinage Is Not Always Accepted</h2><p>A person from a society that relies exclusively on barter may have no practical reason to accept gold or silver. An Easterling warrior who owns an excellent horse may clearly value another horse, a tent, weapons, or livestock, while a handful of coins may seem useless if nobody within that nomadic circuit accepts them.</p><p>The central economic question then becomes: <strong>what will the seller be able to do with what the characters offer?</strong> A coin has real value only if it can later be exchanged.</p>\n<h2>Isolation and Acceptance of Coinage</h2><p>The degree of <strong>isolation</strong> plays a major role in whether coins are accepted. A community in regular contact with foreign merchants may accept coinage it does not mint itself. A village on a trade road may recognise Gondorian or Rhovanion coins, while a tribe that meets a foreign merchant once every ten years may see no use in them.</p>\n<h2>Nomads and Coinage</h2><p>A nomadic way of life does not produce a single attitude toward money. Some riding tribes measure wealth primarily in <strong>horses, herds, weapons, and dependants</strong> and use coins very little. Other nomadic peoples are themselves traders and, on the contrary, find <strong>coins, jewellery, and gemstones</strong> a practical way of carrying great wealth in little space.</p>\n<h2>Coinage as Precious Metal</h2><p>A coin of Middle-earth primarily represents a <strong>defined quantity of precious metal</strong>. Its value rests on weight, material, and purity rather than on the abstract guarantee of the state that issued it. An ancient coin may remain valid, a foreign coin may be accepted, and a damaged coin may retain part of its value.</p><p>Moneychangers, merchants, and craftspeople capable of judging the quality of metals therefore occupy an important place in developed economies.</p>\n<h2>Ancient Coinage</h2><p>The disappearance of a kingdom does not necessarily cancel the value of its currency. A coin of Númenor or of an ancient Dúnedain realm can remain a perfectly valid piece of gold or silver centuries after the authority that struck it has fallen. Its value may even exceed that of its metal if it possesses exceptional workmanship, prestigious origin, historical significance, or unusual rarity.</p>\n<h2>Weight and Purity</h2><p>The use of foreign coins encourages the checking of <strong>weight</strong> and <strong>purity</strong>. An experienced merchant may use a small scale, a moneychanger may know the customary weights of different currencies, and a craftsman may test the metal. A doubtful coin may be accepted only for a fraction of its stated value.</p>\n<h2>The Monetary Market</h2><p>The monetary market appears chiefly in regions where exchanges are numerous and regular enough to make money useful. Great cities contain many specialised craftspeople and goods from different regions. A buyer can sell an item to one person and then spend the money obtained with another.</p><p>Money therefore facilitates urban economies, major markets, and long-distance trade.</p>\n<h2>Great Cities</h2><p>In great cities, coinage is common enough that characters can generally purchase food, lodging, clothing, equipment, and services directly. RMU prices then make full sense as references without becoming fixed. Rarity, quality, taxes, political events, and supply can still change them.</p>\n<h2>Markets and Fairs</h2><p>Temporary markets and great fairs play an essential role between rural and urban economies. Farmers can sell surpluses there, craftspeople buy raw materials, and travelling merchants exchange goods from distant regions. Coinage circulates more freely than in surrounding villages, but barter remains common.</p>\n<h2>Jewellery and Gemstones</h2><p>Jewellery and gemstones are a particularly useful form of wealth when coinage is impractical. They concentrate great value in little space. A gold bracelet can simultaneously function as ornament, reserve of precious metal, social symbol, diplomatic gift, and medium of exchange.</p>\n<h2>Hierarchical Exchange</h2><p>The third major economic system rests on <strong>ties of kinship, loyalty, and obligation</strong>. A lord must provide certain resources to vassals, who in turn must support their own dependants. This organisation distributes wealth without necessarily requiring purchase.</p><p>A ruler provides food, lodging, clothing, tools, weapons, land, and protection; vassals owe service, loyalty, military presence, tribute, labour, or political support in return.</p>\n<h2>The Lord's Obligation and the Vassal's Duty</h2><p>A character bound to a lord does not necessarily need to buy all personal equipment. A warrior belonging to a noble household may receive armour and weapons, a servant be fed and housed, an officer receive a mount, and an administrator be granted a house and land attached to the office.</p><p>Such goods are not always personal property that can be freely resold. They often represent the means provided by the lord so that a dependant can fulfil their duties. In return, the beneficiary must respect the obligations attached to that status.</p>\n<h2>Gift and Reward</h2><p><strong>Gift-giving</strong> plays a major role in this system. A lord may reward a remarkable deed with gemstones, jewellery, splendid clothing, furnishings, land, an estate, an exceptional horse, or the right to receive the oaths of dependants in turn.</p><p>The value of such rewards often exceeds their market price because they also represent honour, reputation, the ruler's trust, and social rank.</p>\n<h2>Wealth and Prestige</h2><p>In many Cultures, wealth is closely tied to <strong>prestige</strong>. Possessing many coins may be less important than owning a large herd, having numerous dependants, wearing jewellery given by a king, maintaining a great household, holding fertile land, or being recognised as head of an important family.</p>\n<h2>The Eldar</h2><p>The <strong>Eldar</strong> provide the clearest example of an economy based on hierarchical and communal ties. Foreign guests should normally be maintained through <strong>gifts and hospitality</strong> during their stay. A visitor should therefore not expect to find an Elven inn where every meal has a posted price.</p><p>Receiving someone means temporarily taking responsibility for their needs. Trying to pay immediately for every service in an Eldarin refuge may seem strange; an appropriate gift or a service rendered later may make far more sense.</p>\n<h2>The Khazâd</h2><p>The <strong>Khazâd</strong> combine a highly developed monetary economy with certain hierarchical exchanges. The king and great lords may distribute resources or rewards to their dependants, while ordinary transactions rely heavily on a market using coinage. Barter remains comparatively rare.</p><p>The Khazâd understand weight, purity, metal value, and quality of workmanship especially well, making them naturally comfortable with foreign currencies and precious metals.</p>\n<h2>The Umli</h2><p>The <strong>Umli</strong> use some limited forms of hierarchical exchange, while their ordinary transactions rely primarily on <strong>barter</strong>. Foreign coins may therefore be of far less interest than immediately useful goods.</p>\n<h2>The Avari</h2><p>Among the <strong>Avari</strong>, economic forms vary by community. Some resemble the Eldarin hierarchical system, while others rely mainly on barter. Coin-based markets remain rare, and each Avarin Culture may receive its own economic interpretation.</p>\n<h2>The Hobbits</h2><p>The <strong>Hobbits</strong> make broad use of markets based on coinage while preserving some barter practices and a few hierarchical relationships historically linked to the King of Arnor and the Thain. Money suits a society of farmers, craftspeople, innkeepers, and small landholders who trade with one another regularly.</p>\n<h2>The Beornings</h2><p>The economy of the <strong>Beornings</strong> rests largely on hierarchical and communal relationships accompanied by barter. After the Quest of Erebor, Beorning lords accept travellers' coinage more readily, showing how a Culture can gradually change its economic practices as trade routes develop.</p>\n<h2>The Dúnedain and Gondorians</h2><p>The <strong>Dúnedain</strong> strongly combine all three systems. Obligations between ruler, vassals, and dependants remain important, while great markets use coinage extensively and barter remains common. A Dúnedain knight may receive land from a lord, buy a sword with coins, and settle a local debt by giving several sacks of grain.</p><p>Among the <strong>Gondorians</strong>, the monetary market is especially widespread. Hierarchical ties continue to organise some relationships among nobles, vassals, and dependants, while barter remains present in the countryside.</p>\n<h2>Eriadorians and Dunlendings</h2><p>The <strong>Eriadorians</strong> rely heavily on barter. Some hierarchical relationships survive in territories linked to Arnorian traditions, while villages sometimes use coinage.</p><p>The <strong>Dunlendings</strong> rely mainly on barter. Hierarchical exchanges exist chiefly to provide food and shelter to members of the group, while some tribes in contact with outsiders accept coinage. The most isolated communities may reject it entirely.</p>\n<h2>The Dorwinrim</h2><p>The <strong>Dorwinrim</strong> combine an active monetary market with extensive barter. This combination suits a Culture strongly oriented toward trade, specialised production, and exchange with different neighbours. Dorwinrim merchants may therefore be especially comfortable with foreign currencies, moneychanging, jewellery, contracts, and cargoes.</p>\n<h2>The Haradrim</h2><p>Among the <strong>Haradrim</strong>, the economy combines several practices. Wealth gained through raids may be redistributed according to hierarchical principles, while commercial exchanges readily use gold jewellery, precious metals, and coinage. For merchants travelling long distances, compact forms of wealth are especially practical.</p>\n<h2>The Easterlings</h2><p>Practices among the <strong>Easterlings</strong> vary by tribe, but many rely primarily on barter and refuse coins. Wealth may be measured in horses, livestock, weapons, tents, jewellery, dependants, or the ability to support a group. Trading traditions and routes can nevertheless produce different behaviours among different peoples.</p>\n<h2>The Lossoth</h2><p>The <strong>Lossoth</strong> rely essentially on <strong>barter</strong>. Their environment makes immediately useful resources especially important: food, furs, tools, weapons, animals, and materials suited to the cold. A purse full of coins can therefore represent theoretical wealth that the character may find very difficult to use.</p>\n<h2>The Rohirrim</h2><p>The <strong>Rohirrim</strong> combine several systems. Family and hierarchical ties provide for some needs, the King notably guarantees military equipment for certain officers and their forces, markets use silver coinage widely, and barter remains common. Horses naturally possess enormous economic and symbolic importance.</p>\n<h2>Woodmen and Woses</h2><p>The <strong>Woodmen</strong> rely essentially on barter, although they may accept some coinage when dealing with travellers. Goods useful to forest life naturally have great value: tools, weapons, honey, furs, herbs, wood products, and food that keeps well.</p><p>The <strong>Woses</strong> rely exclusively on barter and normally do not accept money. Offering more gold does not automatically make a transaction more attractive; one must understand what the community actually needs.</p>\n<h2>Variags, Orcs, and Trolls</h2><p>The <strong>Variags</strong> rely mainly on barter and generally attach no value to coins; goods taken in raids may be distributed according to hierarchical principles among chiefs and warriors.</p><p>Among <strong>Orcs</strong>, wealth circulates mainly through domination. A chief provides resources and equipment to subordinates, while theft, plunder, and violence complete the system. Orcs readily accept coins chiefly because they understand that coins can obtain goods from other peoples.</p><p><strong>Wild Trolls</strong> do not really possess an organised system of voluntary exchange and obtain most possessions through force or theft. Trolls directly tied to Sauron's organisation make greater use of hierarchical distribution, while <strong>Half-trolls</strong> may understand and value coinage according to principles closer to human societies.</p>\n<h2>Long-Distance Trade</h2><p>Long-distance trade profoundly alters the value of goods. Commodities that withstand transport well become especially important: spices, wine, metals, jewellery, gemstones, fine cloth, weapons, rare herbs, and high-quality crafted objects.</p><p>Transporting grain several hundred kilometres may cost more than the grain itself, while a small gemstone can represent several months of transactions. Trade routes therefore favour goods with a high ratio of <strong>value to weight</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Transport, Risk, and Roads</h2><p>The economic cost of an object also includes the risk required to bring it to market. A road threatened by Orcs raises the price of goods, a pass closed by snow reduces supply, and war may interrupt regional trade entirely.</p><p>A safe road gradually produces more merchants, more money, greater craft specialisation, and more foreign goods. A dangerous road instead favours local production, barter, stockpiling, and high prices for imported goods.</p>\n<h2>Craft and Food</h2><p>The value of objects depends heavily on the <strong>time and skill required to make them</strong>. A sword requires ore, charcoal, a forge, tools, and a skilled craftsman. Quality armour therefore represents a considerable concentration of resources and expertise.</p><p>Food has fundamental economic importance. An army consumes enormous quantities of grain, meat, ale, fodder, and water each day; a great city depends on its hinterland, and a poor harvest affects prices, movement, reserves, and military capability.</p>\n<h2>Land, Services, and Obligations</h2><p>In hierarchical societies, <strong>land</strong> often represents a form of wealth far more important than liquid money. Owning land gives access to crops, rents, herds, timber, mines, passage rights, and the people who live upon it.</p><p>Labour and skills can themselves serve as media of exchange. A healer may receive food and lodging for treatment, a smith repair a weapon in exchange for a service, and a guide accept a share of treasure. Not all debts are monetary: a character may owe service, protection, a gift, a favour, or future hospitality.</p>\n<h2>Haggling</h2><p><strong>Haggling</strong> does not have the same role in every Culture. In some markets, negotiating the price is part of the transaction. In other communities, insisting over price may be seen as greed or an insult. Social Skills should therefore be used according to the norms of the Culture involved.</p>\n<h2>Appraisal and Information</h2><p>The <strong>Appraisal</strong> Skill becomes especially important in an economy where characters encounter foreign coins, jewellery, gemstones, ancient weapons, crafted objects, and unfamiliar goods. A successful appraisal provides an approximate understanding of what the character possesses without guaranteeing that a buyer will automatically accept that value.</p><p>A merchant also possesses a resource as important as money: <strong>information</strong>. Merchants know where a commodity is scarce or abundant, which roads are safe, which currencies are accepted, which lords levy taxes, and which wars will disrupt exchange.</p>\n<h2>Taxes, Tolls, and Plunder</h2><p>Kingdoms and lords may take part of the wealth moving through their territory in the form of coins, agricultural produce, services, days of labour, passage rights, or shares of cargo. A toll therefore does not necessarily have to be paid in money.</p><p><strong>Plunder</strong> is an important form of wealth transfer in warrior societies and among the forces of the Shadow. Weapons, livestock, food, jewellery, and captives may represent more useful booty than coin, and the division of spoils often follows precise hierarchical rules.</p>\n<h2>Adventurer Wealth and Availability</h2><p>Adventurers may carry more precious metal than several rural families see in their entire lives. Yet this wealth does not guarantee that they can immediately obtain whatever they desire. A village may have no horse for sale, no armour available, no extra room, or no surplus food.</p><p><strong>Availability</strong> must therefore remain distinct from value: offering ten times the price does not create an object that does not exist.</p>\n<h2>Ancient Treasures</h2><p>Treasures from ancient kingdoms present a special situation. Coins retain their metal value, while some objects may have much greater historical or artistic value. A Númenórean cup may simultaneously be silver, an antiquity, a dynastic symbol, and an object sought by scholars.</p>\n<h2>Application to RMU Prices</h2><p>Prices listed in RMU should serve as a <strong>common reference baseline</strong>. In a great city, the RMU price may approximately match the normal price. In a rural market, the same price may be expressed in equivalent goods. In an isolated community, the item may be unavailable or coin refused. In a time of shortage, the price may rise considerably, while in a producing region it may fall.</p><p>This approach preserves the usefulness of RMU tables without imposing a uniform monetary economy on all of Middle-earth.</p>\n<h2>Application to MERP-RMU Cultures</h2><p>Each MERP-RMU Culture may gradually receive <strong>Economy</strong> metadata containing at minimum the dominant system, use of coinage, importance of barter, importance of hierarchical exchange, preferred forms of wealth, and attitude toward foreign currencies.</p><p>Cultures already integrated into the module can thus use this information directly: <strong>Woses</strong> — barter only; <strong>Khazâd</strong> — developed monetary market and rare barter; <strong>Dúnedain</strong> — mixed system; <strong>Eldar</strong> — economy of gift and obligation; <strong>Lossoth</strong> — barter only.</p>\n<h2>Application to Foundry</h2><p>This entry can serve as the basis for a light adaptation of commerce in MERP-UI without building a heavy economic simulation. A few simple pieces of information are enough: coinage normally accepted, accepted with reservations, or not used; barter common; local availability; possible contextual price coefficient; preferred form of wealth for the Culture.</p>\n<h2>Economy and Culture</h2><p>Economy should be regarded as part of <strong>Culture</strong>, alongside languages, religious practices, or social organisation. It affects the way a character understands wealth, debt, property, gift-giving, trade, and reward.</p><p>A Noldo, a Dorwinrim merchant, and a Lossoth hunter can all understand the concept of value perfectly while holding radically different ideas about how that value should circulate.</p>\n<h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>The economy of Middle-earth rests on a variable combination of <strong>barter, monetary markets, and hierarchical obligations</strong>. Coinage is especially common in great cities and trading societies, while barter remains extremely widespread in the countryside and among many Cultures.</p><p>Coins are valued primarily for the precious metal they contain, allowing ancient and foreign currencies to retain value long after their kingdoms of origin have disappeared. Relationships of loyalty and dependence form another essential channel for the circulation of wealth: a lord provides land, equipment, food, or protection to dependants, while they return service, loyalty, or military support.</p><p>MERP-RMU therefore uses RMU prices as <strong>reference values</strong>, while allowing Culture, location, rarity, and need to determine the real form of each transaction. A purse of gold can open every door in a great market and remain almost useless a few hundred kilometres away in a community that measures wealth in horses, furs, food, land, or obligations.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"TSB3Q5ZVZ5jNt1Ow"}
{"name":"Incantation and Words of Command","sort":90000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"incantation-commandement","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"incantation-commandement","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Incantation et Paroles de Commandement","pages":[{"name":"Incantation et Paroles de Commandement","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Incantation et Paroles de Commandement</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>La magie est normalement invoquée par <strong>paroles et gestes</strong>. Même le Mentalisme emploie généralement ces formes, avec une exception particulière pour les Elfes.</p><ul><li>Un sort demande normalement <strong>au moins 1 round de préparation</strong>.</li><li>Une <strong>Parole de Commandement</strong> permet de supprimer ce minimum.</li><li>Une phrase de <strong>cinq mots maximum</strong> permet d’accélérer un sort ordinaire.</li><li>Un <strong>mot unique</strong> est associé aux sorts marqués instantanés.</li></ul><p>La Parole de Commandement ne supprime ni la résolution RMU, ni le coût, ni le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre, ni les éventuels tests de Corruption. Son automatisation devra être alignée sur l’économie d’actions RMU afin d’éviter tout double comptage.</p></section><h2>Paroles et gestes</h2><p>La magie en Terre du Milieu est normalement invoquée par l’emploi conjoint de paroles et de gestes. Ils constituent la manière ordinaire dont le lanceur organise sa volonté, donne une forme précise au pouvoir et conduit cette puissance vers l’effet recherché.</p><p>Le sort est donc généralement un acte perceptible. Un observateur suffisamment proche peut voir les gestes, entendre les paroles ou constater que le personnage accomplit quelque chose d’inhabituel.</p><h2>L’incantation comme technique</h2><p>Paroles et gestes peuvent dépendre de la Liste, de la tradition magique, de la Race, de la Culture, du maître et éventuellement de la langue utilisée. La mécanique reste identique : le personnage emploie une forme apprise afin de modeler correctement le fairë.</p><h2>Pas de langue magique universelle</h2><p>Les pages étudiées ne définissent pas une langue universelle de la magie. Les mots peuvent être des termes traditionnels, des formules ou des expressions anciennes propres à une école. Il serait donc incorrect d’imposer systématiquement le quenya, le sindarin, le Parler Noir ou toute autre langue.</p><h2>Gestes</h2><p>Les gestes peuvent accompagner l’orientation du pouvoir, désigner une cible, structurer une séquence ou reproduire une technique transmise par le maître. La source ne donne pas de catalogue universel de gestes : le principe est simplement que le lancement normal en implique.</p><h2>Mentalisme</h2><p>Même les utilisateurs du Mentalisme emploient normalement paroles et gestes, bien que ces formes ne leur apportent aucun avantage particulier. Elles constituent surtout la méthode normalement apprise et employée.</p><h2>Exception elfique</h2><p>Les Elfes constituent une exception notable : le texte précise que les utilisateurs du Mentalisme emploient paroles et gestes sauf les Elfes, « qui savent mieux ». Cette exception renvoie à leur compréhension plus naturelle du fairë et devra être confrontée aux règles RMU avant automatisation.</p><h2>Un round minimum de préparation</h2><p>Un sort demande normalement <strong>au moins un round de préparation</strong> avant de pouvoir être lancé. Cette préparation représente le temps nécessaire pour rassembler la volonté, accomplir les paroles et gestes requis et préparer la manifestation du pouvoir.</p><h2>Anticipation et combat</h2><p>Cette règle crée un coût tactique réel. Le lanceur doit anticiper, commencer à préparer avant que l’ennemi soit à portée ou choisir une autre solution si la situation ne lui laisse pas le temps nécessaire.</p><h2>Relation avec RMU</h2><p>La règle MERP doit être comparée précisément à l’économie d’actions et aux règles de Spell Casting RMU. Il ne faut pas ajouter aveuglément un round si RMU représente déjà la préparation. L’intention à conserver est qu’un sort exige normalement une préparation réelle.</p><h2>Paroles de Commandement</h2><p>Pour lancer un sort sans ce minimum d’un round, le personnage doit utiliser une <strong>Parole de Commandement</strong>. Il s’agit d’une forme d’incantation particulièrement condensée, dans laquelle le lanceur impose immédiatement par sa volonté une structure magique normalement préparée plus longuement.</p><h2>Deux formes</h2><p>Les Paroles de Commandement prennent deux formes : <strong>un mot unique</strong>, associé aux sorts instantanés ; ou une <strong>courte phrase de cinq mots maximum</strong>, permettant de lancer un sort sans round de préparation.</p><h3>La courte phrase</h3><p>Une courte phrase de cinq mots maximum permet d’accélérer un sort ordinaire. Elle concentre l’incantation dans une formulation courte, immédiate et chargée de volonté.</p><h3>Le mot unique</h3><p>La forme la plus brève est un mot unique, utilisé avec les sorts marqués comme instantanés. La puissance vient de la capacité du lanceur à investir entièrement ce mot de la volonté nécessaire.</p><h2>Formulation et langue</h2><p>La source ne donne pas de formule obligatoire pour chaque sort ni de langue imposée. Deux Magiciens utilisant le même sort peuvent employer des paroles différentes selon leur tradition. Comprendre les mots entendus ne suffit pas à reproduire le sort : la connaissance de la Liste et toutes les conditions RMU restent nécessaires.</p><h2>Pas de lancement gratuit</h2><p>La Parole de Commandement ne rend pas le sort automatiquement réussi et ne supprime pas son coût en Points de Pouvoir, sa cible, sa durée, ses résistances ou ses autres conditions. Elle modifie uniquement le temps de préparation.</p><h2>Instantanéité</h2><p>Le mot unique est réservé aux sorts marqués comme instantanés. Lors de l’intégration, il faudra identifier précisément la propriété RMU correspondant à cette notion dans les JSON plutôt que la déduire du nom du sort.</p><h2>Concentration et cible</h2><p>La suppression du round de préparation ne supprime pas les exigences ultérieures de concentration et ne modifie ni portée ni cible. Le sort continue à fonctionner selon sa fiche RMU.</p><h2>Usage en combat et hors combat</h2><p>La Parole de Commandement devient particulièrement importante lorsqu’une menace surgit, qu’une porte cède, qu’un compagnon tombe ou qu’un événement soudain ne laisse pas le temps de préparer. Hors combat, elle peut répondre à une catastrophe immédiate ou à une urgence similaire.</p><h2>Usage discret</h2><p>Une phrase courte peut sembler plus discrète qu’une incantation longue, mais elle reste prononcée et peut attirer l’attention. Rapidité et discrétion sont deux notions différentes.</p><h2>Subterfuge magique</h2><p>Le Subterfuge magique peut servir à masquer les manifestations verbales ou gestuelles. Il ne remplace pas la Parole de Commandement : celle-ci réduit le temps de préparation, tandis que le Subterfuge cherche à dissimuler l’incantation.</p><h2>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</h2><p>Une Parole de Commandement ne supprime pas le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre. Le sort est toujours lancé et manipule toujours du fairë. Le Jet de Risque doit donc être effectué normalement.</p><h2>Corruption</h2><p>La Parole de Commandement ne modifie pas non plus la nature morale du sort. Sorcellerie et usages visant à dominer ou terrifier continuent à déclencher les règles de Corruption appropriées.</p><h2>Silence, immobilisation et blessures</h2><p>La source confirme que les paroles et gestes font normalement partie de l’incantation mais ne fournit pas ici les pénalités chiffrées pour silence, immobilisation ou blessures. Ces situations doivent être résolues avec les mécanismes RMU existants plutôt que par une sous-règle inventée.</p><h2>Tradition et style</h2><p>La forme d’une Parole de Commandement peut être très caractéristique : formule technique, mot ancien, injonction issue d’une tradition de Canalisation ou expression plus sombre. Ces différences sont descriptives tant qu’aucune règle ne leur attribue un effet supplémentaire.</p><h2>Ne pas confondre avec domination</h2><p>Le terme « commandement » ne rend pas la méthode corruptrice par nature. Une Parole de Commandement peut produire lumière, protection ou guérison. La Corruption dépend du sort et de son usage.</p><h2>Exemples</h2><p>Les exemples peuvent être très simples : « Lumière ! », « Écarte-toi ! », « Que le feu s’élève ! », « Cette porte restera close ! ». Ils sont illustratifs ; la seule contrainte textuelle certaine est la limite de cinq mots pour la phrase courte.</p><h2>Lien avec la fiche du sort</h2><p>Une fiche de sort pourrait à terme afficher une section Incantation MERP-RMU : préparation minimale, possibilité de Parole de Commandement, propriété instantanée, manifestations et éventuelle exception de Mentalisme elfique.</p><h2>Automatisation future</h2><p>Cette mécanique ne devra être codée qu’après comparaison avec les règles natives RMU. Le module pourra déterminer si la préparation a été effectuée, si une Parole de Commandement est utilisée, si le sort est instantané et si une exception raciale s’applique.</p><h2>Séquence de lancement</h2><p>La séquence cible est : 1) préparation ou Parole de Commandement ; 2) résolution RMU normale ; 3) Jet de Risque d’attirer l’Ombre ; 4) vérification de la Corruption. Cette séparation évite de confondre les responsabilités de RMU et de MERP-RMU.</p><h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>L’Incantation exprime normalement la volonté par paroles et gestes. La Parole de Commandement concentre cette volonté afin d’accélérer le lancement, sans changer la nature, le coût ni les conséquences du sort.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Incantation and Words of Command","pages":[{"name":"Incantation and Words of Command","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Incantation and Words of Command</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magic is normally invoked through <strong>words and gestures</strong>. Even Mentalism generally uses these forms, with a particular exception for Elves.</p><ul><li>A spell normally requires <strong>at least 1 round of preparation</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>Word of Command</strong> allows this minimum to be removed.</li><li>A phrase of <strong>no more than five words</strong> allows an ordinary spell to be accelerated.</li><li>A <strong>single word</strong> is associated with spells marked as instantaneous.</li></ul><p>A Word of Command does not remove RMU resolution, cost, the Risk of Attracting the Shadow, or any applicable Corruption Tests. Its automation must be aligned with RMU's action economy so as to avoid double counting.</p></section><h2>Words and Gestures</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth is normally invoked through the combined use of words and gestures. They are the ordinary means by which the caster organises will, gives precise form to power, and directs that power toward the desired effect.</p><p>Casting is therefore generally a perceptible act. An observer close enough may see the gestures, hear the words, or notice that the character is doing something unusual.</p><h2>Incantation as a Technique</h2><p>Words and gestures may depend on the List, magical tradition, Race, Culture, teacher, and possibly the language being used. The mechanic remains the same: the character employs a learned form in order to shape fairë correctly.</p><h2>No Universal Magical Language</h2><p>The pages examined do not define a universal language of magic. The words may be traditional terms, formulae, or ancient expressions particular to a school. It would therefore be incorrect to impose Quenya, Sindarin, the Black Speech, or any other language systematically.</p><h2>Gestures</h2><p>Gestures may accompany the directing of power, designate a target, structure a sequence, or reproduce a technique passed on by a teacher. The source provides no universal catalogue of gestures: the principle is simply that normal casting involves them.</p><h2>Mentalism</h2><p>Even Mentalism users normally employ words and gestures, although these forms provide them with no particular advantage. They are primarily the method normally learned and used.</p><h2>Elven Exception</h2><p>Elves are a notable exception: the text states that Mentalism users employ words and gestures except for Elves, “who know better.” This exception points to their more natural understanding of fairë and must be compared with RMU rules before automation.</p><h2>A Minimum of One Round of Preparation</h2><p>A spell normally requires <strong>at least one round of preparation</strong> before it can be cast. This preparation represents the time needed to gather the will, perform the required words and gestures, and prepare the manifestation of power.</p><h2>Anticipation and Combat</h2><p>This rule creates a real tactical cost. The caster must anticipate, begin preparing before the enemy is within reach, or choose another solution if the situation does not allow the necessary time.</p><h2>Relationship with RMU</h2><p>The MERP rule must be compared precisely with RMU's action economy and Spell Casting rules. A round must not be added blindly if RMU already represents preparation. The intent to preserve is that a spell normally requires genuine preparation.</p><h2>Words of Command</h2><p>To cast a spell without this minimum of one round, the character must use a <strong>Word of Command</strong>. This is an especially condensed form of incantation in which the caster immediately imposes through will a magical structure that would normally be prepared over a longer period.</p><h2>Two Forms</h2><p>Words of Command take two forms: <strong>a single word</strong>, associated with instantaneous spells; or a <strong>short phrase of no more than five words</strong>, allowing a spell to be cast without a preparation round.</p><h3>The Short Phrase</h3><p>A short phrase of no more than five words allows an ordinary spell to be accelerated. It concentrates the incantation into a brief, immediate formulation charged with will.</p><h3>The Single Word</h3><p>The shortest form is a single word, used with spells marked as instantaneous. Its power comes from the caster's ability to invest that word fully with the necessary will.</p><h2>Wording and Language</h2><p>The source gives no mandatory formula for each spell and imposes no language. Two Magicians using the same spell may employ different words according to their traditions. Understanding words that are overheard is not enough to reproduce the spell: knowledge of the List and all RMU requirements remain necessary.</p><h2>No Free Casting</h2><p>A Word of Command does not make the spell automatically successful and does not remove its Power Point cost, target, duration, resistances, or other requirements. It changes only the preparation time.</p><h2>Instantaneous Casting</h2><p>The single word is reserved for spells marked as instantaneous. During integration, the RMU property corresponding to this concept must be identified precisely in the JSON rather than inferred from the spell's name.</p><h2>Concentration and Target</h2><p>Removing the preparation round does not remove later concentration requirements and does not alter range or target. The spell continues to function according to its RMU sheet.</p><h2>Use in and out of Combat</h2><p>A Word of Command becomes particularly important when a threat suddenly appears, a door gives way, a companion falls, or an unexpected event leaves no time to prepare. Outside combat, it may answer an immediate disaster or a similar emergency.</p><h2>Discreet Use</h2><p>A short phrase may seem more discreet than a long incantation, but it is still spoken and can attract attention. Speed and discretion are two different concepts.</p><h2>Spell Trickery</h2><p>Spell Trickery can be used to conceal verbal or gestural manifestations. It does not replace a Word of Command: the latter reduces preparation time, while Spell Trickery seeks to conceal the casting.</p><h2>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</h2><p>A Word of Command does not remove the Risk of Attracting the Shadow. The spell is still cast and still manipulates fairë. The Risk Roll must therefore be made normally.</p><h2>Corruption</h2><p>A Word of Command does not change the spell's moral nature either. Sorcery and uses intended to dominate or terrify continue to trigger the appropriate Corruption rules.</p><h2>Silence, Restraint, and Injuries</h2><p>The source confirms that words and gestures are normally part of casting but does not provide numerical penalties here for silence, restraint, or injuries. Such situations should be resolved with existing RMU mechanisms rather than through an invented sub-rule.</p><h2>Tradition and Style</h2><p>The form of a Word of Command can be highly characteristic: a technical formula, an ancient word, an injunction from a Channeling tradition, or a darker expression. These differences are descriptive unless a rule assigns them an additional effect.</p><h2>Do Not Confuse It with Domination</h2><p>The word “command” does not make the method corrupting by nature. A Word of Command may produce light, protection, or healing. Corruption depends on the spell and its use.</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>Examples can be very simple: “Light!”, “Stand aside!”, “Let the fire rise!”, “This door will remain shut!” They are illustrative; the only certain textual constraint is the five-word limit for the short phrase.</p><h2>Link with the Spell Sheet</h2><p>A spell sheet could eventually display a MERP-RMU Incantation section: minimum preparation, availability of a Word of Command, instantaneous property, manifestations, and any Elven Mentalism exception.</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>This mechanic should only be coded after comparison with native RMU rules. The module may determine whether preparation has been completed, whether a Word of Command is being used, whether the spell is instantaneous, and whether a racial exception applies.</p><h2>Casting Sequence</h2><p>The target sequence is: 1) preparation or Word of Command; 2) normal RMU resolution; 3) Risk Roll for Attracting the Shadow; 4) Corruption check. This separation avoids confusing RMU and MERP-RMU responsibilities.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Incantation normally expresses will through words and gestures. A Word of Command concentrates that will in order to accelerate casting without changing the spell's nature, cost, or consequences.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Incantation and Words of Command","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Incantation and Words of Command</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magic is normally invoked through <strong>words and gestures</strong>. Even Mentalism generally uses these forms, with a particular exception for Elves.</p><ul><li>A spell normally requires <strong>at least 1 round of preparation</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>Word of Command</strong> allows this minimum to be removed.</li><li>A phrase of <strong>no more than five words</strong> allows an ordinary spell to be accelerated.</li><li>A <strong>single word</strong> is associated with spells marked as instantaneous.</li></ul><p>A Word of Command does not remove RMU resolution, cost, the Risk of Attracting the Shadow, or any applicable Corruption Tests. Its automation must be aligned with RMU's action economy so as to avoid double counting.</p></section><h2>Words and Gestures</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth is normally invoked through the combined use of words and gestures. They are the ordinary means by which the caster organises will, gives precise form to power, and directs that power toward the desired effect.</p><p>Casting is therefore generally a perceptible act. An observer close enough may see the gestures, hear the words, or notice that the character is doing something unusual.</p><h2>Incantation as a Technique</h2><p>Words and gestures may depend on the List, magical tradition, Race, Culture, teacher, and possibly the language being used. The mechanic remains the same: the character employs a learned form in order to shape fairë correctly.</p><h2>No Universal Magical Language</h2><p>The pages examined do not define a universal language of magic. The words may be traditional terms, formulae, or ancient expressions particular to a school. It would therefore be incorrect to impose Quenya, Sindarin, the Black Speech, or any other language systematically.</p><h2>Gestures</h2><p>Gestures may accompany the directing of power, designate a target, structure a sequence, or reproduce a technique passed on by a teacher. The source provides no universal catalogue of gestures: the principle is simply that normal casting involves them.</p><h2>Mentalism</h2><p>Even Mentalism users normally employ words and gestures, although these forms provide them with no particular advantage. They are primarily the method normally learned and used.</p><h2>Elven Exception</h2><p>Elves are a notable exception: the text states that Mentalism users employ words and gestures except for Elves, “who know better.” This exception points to their more natural understanding of fairë and must be compared with RMU rules before automation.</p><h2>A Minimum of One Round of Preparation</h2><p>A spell normally requires <strong>at least one round of preparation</strong> before it can be cast. This preparation represents the time needed to gather the will, perform the required words and gestures, and prepare the manifestation of power.</p><h2>Anticipation and Combat</h2><p>This rule creates a real tactical cost. The caster must anticipate, begin preparing before the enemy is within reach, or choose another solution if the situation does not allow the necessary time.</p><h2>Relationship with RMU</h2><p>The MERP rule must be compared precisely with RMU's action economy and Spell Casting rules. A round must not be added blindly if RMU already represents preparation. The intent to preserve is that a spell normally requires genuine preparation.</p><h2>Words of Command</h2><p>To cast a spell without this minimum of one round, the character must use a <strong>Word of Command</strong>. This is an especially condensed form of incantation in which the caster immediately imposes through will a magical structure that would normally be prepared over a longer period.</p><h2>Two Forms</h2><p>Words of Command take two forms: <strong>a single word</strong>, associated with instantaneous spells; or a <strong>short phrase of no more than five words</strong>, allowing a spell to be cast without a preparation round.</p><h3>The Short Phrase</h3><p>A short phrase of no more than five words allows an ordinary spell to be accelerated. It concentrates the incantation into a brief, immediate formulation charged with will.</p><h3>The Single Word</h3><p>The shortest form is a single word, used with spells marked as instantaneous. Its power comes from the caster's ability to invest that word fully with the necessary will.</p><h2>Wording and Language</h2><p>The source gives no mandatory formula for each spell and imposes no language. Two Magicians using the same spell may employ different words according to their traditions. Understanding words that are overheard is not enough to reproduce the spell: knowledge of the List and all RMU requirements remain necessary.</p><h2>No Free Casting</h2><p>A Word of Command does not make the spell automatically successful and does not remove its Power Point cost, target, duration, resistances, or other requirements. It changes only the preparation time.</p><h2>Instantaneous Casting</h2><p>The single word is reserved for spells marked as instantaneous. During integration, the RMU property corresponding to this concept must be identified precisely in the JSON rather than inferred from the spell's name.</p><h2>Concentration and Target</h2><p>Removing the preparation round does not remove later concentration requirements and does not alter range or target. The spell continues to function according to its RMU sheet.</p><h2>Use in and out of Combat</h2><p>A Word of Command becomes particularly important when a threat suddenly appears, a door gives way, a companion falls, or an unexpected event leaves no time to prepare. Outside combat, it may answer an immediate disaster or a similar emergency.</p><h2>Discreet Use</h2><p>A short phrase may seem more discreet than a long incantation, but it is still spoken and can attract attention. Speed and discretion are two different concepts.</p><h2>Spell Trickery</h2><p>Spell Trickery can be used to conceal verbal or gestural manifestations. It does not replace a Word of Command: the latter reduces preparation time, while Spell Trickery seeks to conceal the casting.</p><h2>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</h2><p>A Word of Command does not remove the Risk of Attracting the Shadow. The spell is still cast and still manipulates fairë. The Risk Roll must therefore be made normally.</p><h2>Corruption</h2><p>A Word of Command does not change the spell's moral nature either. Sorcery and uses intended to dominate or terrify continue to trigger the appropriate Corruption rules.</p><h2>Silence, Restraint, and Injuries</h2><p>The source confirms that words and gestures are normally part of casting but does not provide numerical penalties here for silence, restraint, or injuries. Such situations should be resolved with existing RMU mechanisms rather than through an invented sub-rule.</p><h2>Tradition and Style</h2><p>The form of a Word of Command can be highly characteristic: a technical formula, an ancient word, an injunction from a Channeling tradition, or a darker expression. These differences are descriptive unless a rule assigns them an additional effect.</p><h2>Do Not Confuse It with Domination</h2><p>The word “command” does not make the method corrupting by nature. A Word of Command may produce light, protection, or healing. Corruption depends on the spell and its use.</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>Examples can be very simple: “Light!”, “Stand aside!”, “Let the fire rise!”, “This door will remain shut!” They are illustrative; the only certain textual constraint is the five-word limit for the short phrase.</p><h2>Link with the Spell Sheet</h2><p>A spell sheet could eventually display a MERP-RMU Incantation section: minimum preparation, availability of a Word of Command, instantaneous property, manifestations, and any Elven Mentalism exception.</p><h2>Future Automation</h2><p>This mechanic should only be coded after comparison with native RMU rules. The module may determine whether preparation has been completed, whether a Word of Command is being used, whether the spell is instantaneous, and whether a racial exception applies.</p><h2>Casting Sequence</h2><p>The target sequence is: 1) preparation or Word of Command; 2) normal RMU resolution; 3) Risk Roll for Attracting the Shadow; 4) Corruption check. This separation avoids confusing RMU and MERP-RMU responsibilities.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Incantation normally expresses will through words and gestures. A Word of Command concentrates that will in order to accelerate casting without changing the spell's nature, cost, or consequences.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"WKl5JCM53a7GuVuX"}
{"name":"MERP-RMU Presentation","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"merp-rmu-introduction","collection":"merp-rmu-introduction","contentVersion":1,"compendiumSourceKey":"merp-rmu-introduction","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/introduction-v1.json","compendiumFolderKey":null,"compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Présentation de MERP-RMU","pages":[{"name":"Bienvenue dans MERP-RMU","text":{"content":"<h1>Bienvenue dans MERP-RMU</h1><p><strong>MERP-RMU</strong> est une adaptation de <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> destinée à jouer dans la Terre du Milieu en retrouvant l'esprit, la richesse et la diversité qui ont longtemps caractérisé <strong>MERP</strong> et les différentes générations de <strong>Rolemaster</strong>.</p><p>Notre objectif n'est pas de reconstituer à l'identique un ancien système de règles. RMU apporte une architecture moderne, cohérente et beaucoup plus homogène. MERP-RMU s'appuie sur cette base et y réintroduit les peuples, les cultures, les professions, les traditions magiques, les compétences, les talents, les défauts, les herbes, les objets et les particularités qui donnent à la Terre du Milieu sa personnalité propre.</p><p>Le projet s'est construit progressivement autour de parties réellement jouées. Chaque règle ajoutée cherche donc à répondre à une situation de table : créer un personnage du monde de Tolkien, rendre la magie plus discrète et plus dangereuse, différencier les cultures, retrouver certaines traditions de MERP, ou permettre à Foundry VTT d'accompagner le jeu sans le remplacer.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Notre démarche","text":{"content":"<h1>Notre démarche</h1><p>MERP-RMU repose sur un principe simple : <strong>conserver RMU lorsqu'il répond déjà correctement au besoin, adapter lorsqu'une mécanique ancienne mérite d'être transposée, et créer seulement lorsque la Terre du Milieu exige quelque chose que RMU ne propose pas</strong>.</p><p>Cette méthode évite d'empiler deux systèmes de jeu. Lorsqu'un Talent, une Compétence, une Profession ou une règle possède déjà un équivalent fonctionnel dans RMU, nous utilisons le mécanisme du Core. Lorsque les anciennes règles reposent sur des concepts disparus — anciens types d'actions, coûts de développement, catégories Everyman, multiplicateurs ou bonus devenus excessifs — elles sont réécrites dans le langage mécanique de RMU.</p><p>Les adaptations sont également replacées dans le contexte de la Terre du Milieu. La magie y reste rare, enracinée dans les peuples et les traditions, et son usage peut avoir des conséquences. Les Cultures ont une importance réelle dans la création des personnages. Les Listes de sorts ne sont pas de simples catalogues abstraits : leur disponibilité dépend de l'époque, de l'enseignement et parfois de l'influence de l'Ombre.</p><p>Certaines parties du projet sont explicitement proposées en <strong>bêta</strong>. Elles sont destinées à être éprouvées par les joueurs avant d'être considérées comme définitives. L'économie, certaines Professions reconstruites ou des mécanismes propres à la Terre du Milieu pourront donc évoluer en fonction des retours de jeu.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Ce que contient le projet","text":{"content":"<h1>Ce que contient le projet</h1><p>MERP-RMU ne se limite plus à une conversion de création de personnage. Le travail couvre aujourd'hui plusieurs niveaux du jeu.</p><h2>Peuples et Cultures</h2><p>Les Races et Cultures de la Terre du Milieu sont adaptées à la structure RMU, avec leurs caractéristiques, langues, compétences culturelles, restrictions et disponibilités selon les Âges.</p><h2>Professions</h2><p>Les Professions issues des traditions MERP et Rolemaster sont reconstruites sur des châssis RMU stables. Leurs descriptions détaillent leur rôle dans la Terre du Milieu, leurs caractéristiques primordiales, leurs Compétences Professionnelles, leurs Listes de sorts et les choix d'adaptation effectués.</p><h2>Magie</h2><p>Le projet adapte l'Essence, la Théurgie et le Mentalisme à la Terre du Milieu, avec des règles sur l'usage discret de la magie, le déclin des traditions, la Sorcellerie, l'Élément de Morgoth, la Corruption et le <strong>Risque d'attirer l'Ombre</strong>. Des Listes et Compétences propres à certaines cultures sont également intégrées lorsque les sources le justifient.</p><h2>Compétences, Talents et Défauts</h2><p>Les éléments déjà couverts par le Core RMU restent dans le Core. Les capacités réellement spécifiques à MERP ou à la Terre du Milieu sont converties en équivalents RMU, avec des coûts et des Tiers adaptés à l'échelle moderne du système.</p><h2>Herbes, substances et objets</h2><p>Les herbes, remèdes, poisons, aliments et substances des anciennes sources sont convertis en Items RMU et classés selon leur utilité. Leur environnement, leur difficulté de recherche, leur préparation et leurs effets sont présentés sous une forme directement utilisable en jeu.</p><h2>Foundry VTT et MERP-UI</h2><p>MERP-UI assure l'intégration technique : contenus de Compendium, création de personnage, traductions, automatisations, règles de magie, Journaux, aides de jeu et outils destinés au meneur. L'objectif est que l'interface accompagne les règles sans exiger du joueur qu'il connaisse les détails techniques internes de RMU.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Sources et crédits","text":{"content":"<h1>Sources et crédits</h1><p>MERP-RMU s'inspire très largement de l'excellent <em>MERP™ Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP</em>, version 2.5 du 13 septembre 2005, conçu par <strong>Whitford Bates</strong>. Ce travail a constitué une source essentielle pour les Cultures, les Professions, les Background Options et de nombreuses règles spécifiques à la Terre du Milieu.</p><p>Nous souhaitons également créditer <strong>Jonathon Dale</strong> ainsi que les contributeurs de la communauté <strong>merp-fan-modules</strong>, dont les travaux ont participé à préserver et enrichir pendant de nombreuses années le matériel de jeu consacré à la Terre du Milieu.</p><p>MERP-RMU s'appuie parallèlement sur <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> et sur son implémentation Foundry VTT. Lorsque RMU fournit déjà une solution fonctionnelle, celle-ci reste la référence mécanique de l'adaptation.</p><p>Enfin, les nombreux suppléments MERP et Rolemaster consacrés à la Terre du Milieu demeurent une source d'inspiration essentielle pour le contexte, les peuples, les lieux, les traditions et les aventures.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Un projet communautaire gratuit","text":{"content":"<h1>Un projet communautaire gratuit</h1><p>MERP-RMU est un <strong>projet communautaire non commercial</strong>. Il est conçu pour être partagé gratuitement entre joueurs et meneurs qui souhaitent utiliser Rolemaster Unified dans la Terre du Milieu.</p><p>Les adaptations, traductions, Journaux, données Foundry, scripts, présentations et autres éléments créés spécifiquement pour MERP-RMU sont mis à disposition dans cet esprit. Ils ne doivent pas être revendus, reconditionnés ou intégrés à un module payant par un tiers.</p><p><strong>Projet communautaire gratuit — revente interdite.</strong></p><p>Cette démarche n'emporte évidemment aucun droit sur les œuvres, marques ou textes appartenant à leurs ayants droit respectifs. MERP-RMU vise à rendre hommage à ces jeux et à permettre à une communauté de passionnés de continuer à jouer dans cet univers avec les outils actuels.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"À propos de l'adaptation","text":{"content":"<h1>À propos de l'adaptation</h1><p>Je m'appelle <strong>Jean-Brice Gayet</strong>. Je joue aux jeux de rôle depuis de nombreuses années, avec un attachement particulier à la <strong>Terre du Milieu, MERP et Rolemaster</strong>, et je consacre également beaucoup de temps à la création et à l'adaptation de contenus pour <strong>Foundry VTT</strong>.</p><p>Ce travail a commencé avec une idée très simple : pouvoir faire jouer des aventures de la Terre du Milieu avec Rolemaster Unified dans Foundry. En avançant, le projet s'est élargi. Une simple conversion de règles est devenue progressivement un ensemble cohérent de contenus : personnages, cultures, professions, magie, compétences, objets, Journaux, traductions, automatisations et outils de jeu.</p><p>MERP-RMU reste un projet vivant. Son objectif n'est pas de prétendre proposer une version définitive de la Terre du Milieu pour RMU, mais de fournir une base solide, jouable et documentée, puis de l'améliorer grâce aux parties, aux retours des joueurs et à l'expérience acquise en campagne.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Guide d’installation et de réglages MERP-UI","text":{"content":"<h1>Guide d’installation et de réglages MERP-UI</h1><p>Cette page résume les principaux réglages à vérifier avant la création des personnages.</p><p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"modules/merp-ui/assets/guides/merp-ui-settings-guide-fr.webp\" alt=\"Guide des réglages MERP-UI / MERP-RMU\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;border:0;\"></p>"},"title":{"show":false},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"settingsGuidePage":true,"guideLanguage":"fr"}}}]},"en":{"name":"MERP-RMU Presentation","pages":[{"name":"Welcome to MERP-RMU","text":{"content":"<h1>Welcome to MERP-RMU</h1><p><strong>MERP-RMU</strong> is an adaptation of <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> designed for play in Middle-earth while recapturing the spirit, richness, and diversity that have long characterised <strong>MERP</strong> and the different generations of <strong>Rolemaster</strong>.</p><p>Our goal is not to recreate an older rules system exactly. RMU provides a modern, coherent, and much more unified architecture. MERP-RMU builds on that foundation and reintroduces the peoples, cultures, professions, magical traditions, skills, talents, flaws, herbs, items, and distinctive features that give Middle-earth its unique character.</p><p>The project has developed gradually around games that were actually played. Every rule we add therefore seeks to answer a situation that arises at the table: creating a character from Tolkien's world, making magic more discreet and more dangerous, differentiating cultures, recovering certain MERP traditions, or allowing Foundry VTT to support the game without replacing it.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Our Approach","text":{"content":"<h1>Our Approach</h1><p>MERP-RMU follows a simple principle: <strong>keep RMU when it already meets the need correctly, adapt when an older mechanic deserves to be carried over, and create only when Middle-earth requires something that RMU does not provide</strong>.</p><p>This method avoids stacking two game systems on top of one another. When a Talent, Skill, Profession, or rule already has a functional equivalent in RMU, we use the Core mechanism. When older rules rely on concepts that no longer exist — former action types, development costs, Everyman categories, multipliers, or bonuses that have become excessive — they are rewritten in RMU's mechanical language.</p><p>The adaptations are also placed back into the context of Middle-earth. Magic remains rare, rooted in peoples and traditions, and its use can have consequences. Cultures have real importance during character creation. Spell Lists are not merely abstract catalogues: their availability depends on the Age, teaching, and sometimes the influence of the Shadow.</p><p>Some parts of the project are explicitly presented as <strong>beta</strong>. They are intended to be tested by players before being considered final. The economy, certain reconstructed Professions, or mechanics specific to Middle-earth may therefore evolve in response to actual play.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"What the Project Contains","text":{"content":"<h1>What the Project Contains</h1><p>MERP-RMU is no longer limited to converting character creation. The work now covers several levels of the game.</p><h2>Peoples and Cultures</h2><p>The Races and Cultures of Middle-earth are adapted to the RMU structure, including their characteristics, languages, cultural skills, restrictions, and availability by Age.</p><h2>Professions</h2><p>Professions drawn from the MERP and Rolemaster traditions are rebuilt on stable RMU chassis. Their descriptions detail their role in Middle-earth, their Prime Stats, Professional Skills, Spell Lists, and the adaptation choices that were made.</p><h2>Magic</h2><p>The project adapts Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism to Middle-earth, with rules for discreet use of magic, the decline of magical traditions, Sorcery, the Morgoth Element, Corruption, and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>. Lists and Skills specific to certain cultures are also integrated when the sources justify them.</p><h2>Skills, Talents, and Flaws</h2><p>Elements already covered by the RMU Core remain in the Core. Abilities genuinely specific to MERP or Middle-earth are converted into RMU equivalents, with costs and Tiers adjusted to the modern scale of the system.</p><h2>Herbs, Substances, and Items</h2><p>Herbs, remedies, poisons, foods, and substances from older sources are converted into RMU Items and classified according to their use. Their environment, search difficulty, preparation, and effects are presented in a form ready for play.</p><h2>Foundry VTT and MERP-UI</h2><p>MERP-UI handles the technical integration: Compendium content, character creation, translations, automations, magic rules, Journals, play aids, and tools for the Game Master. The goal is for the interface to support the rules without requiring players to understand RMU's internal technical details.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Sources and Credits","text":{"content":"<h1>Sources and Credits</h1><p>MERP-RMU draws very heavily on the excellent <em>MERP™ Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP</em>, version 2.5 dated 13 September 2005, designed by <strong>Whitford Bates</strong>. This work has been an essential source for Cultures, Professions, Background Options, and many rules specific to Middle-earth.</p><p>We also wish to credit <strong>Jonathon Dale</strong> and the contributors to the <strong>merp-fan-modules</strong> community, whose work has helped preserve and enrich Middle-earth gaming material for many years.</p><p>MERP-RMU also builds on <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> and its Foundry VTT implementation. Whenever RMU already provides a functional solution, that solution remains the mechanical reference for the adaptation.</p><p>Finally, the many MERP and Rolemaster supplements devoted to Middle-earth remain an essential source of inspiration for the setting, peoples, places, traditions, and adventures.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"A Free Community Project","text":{"content":"<h1>A Free Community Project</h1><p>MERP-RMU is a <strong>non-commercial community project</strong>. It is intended to be shared freely among players and Game Masters who wish to use Rolemaster Unified in Middle-earth.</p><p>The adaptations, translations, Journals, Foundry data, scripts, presentations, and other elements created specifically for MERP-RMU are made available in that spirit. They must not be resold, repackaged, or incorporated into a paid module by a third party.</p><p><strong>Free community project — resale prohibited.</strong></p><p>This approach obviously grants no rights over works, trademarks, or texts belonging to their respective rights holders. MERP-RMU is intended as a tribute to these games and as a way for a community of enthusiasts to continue playing in this setting with current tools.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"About the Adaptation","text":{"content":"<h1>About the Adaptation</h1><p>My name is <strong>Jean-Brice Gayet</strong>. I have been playing roleplaying games for many years, with a particular attachment to <strong>Middle-earth, MERP, and Rolemaster</strong>, and I also devote a great deal of time to creating and adapting content for <strong>Foundry VTT</strong>.</p><p>This work began with a very simple idea: to be able to run Middle-earth adventures with Rolemaster Unified in Foundry. As the work progressed, the project expanded. A simple rules conversion gradually became a coherent body of content: characters, cultures, professions, magic, skills, items, Journals, translations, automations, and gaming tools.</p><p>MERP-RMU remains a living project. Its purpose is not to claim to provide a definitive version of Middle-earth for RMU, but to offer a solid, playable, and documented foundation, then improve it through actual games, player feedback, and experience gained in campaigns.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"MERP-UI Installation & Settings Guide","text":{"content":"<h1>MERP-UI Installation & Settings Guide</h1><p>This page summarises the main settings to review before starting character creation.</p><p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"modules/merp-ui/assets/guides/merp-ui-settings-guide-en.webp\" alt=\"MERP-UI / MERP-RMU Settings Guide\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;border:0;\"></p>"},"title":{"show":false},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"settingsGuidePage":true,"guideLanguage":"en"}}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["0 - Présentation de MERP - RMU"],"en":["0 - MERP-RMU Presentation"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-00-presentation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Welcome to MERP-RMU","text":{"content":"<h1>Welcome to MERP-RMU</h1><p><strong>MERP-RMU</strong> is an adaptation of <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> designed for play in Middle-earth while recapturing the spirit, richness, and diversity that have long characterised <strong>MERP</strong> and the different generations of <strong>Rolemaster</strong>.</p><p>Our goal is not to recreate an older rules system exactly. RMU provides a modern, coherent, and much more unified architecture. MERP-RMU builds on that foundation and reintroduces the peoples, cultures, professions, magical traditions, skills, talents, flaws, herbs, items, and distinctive features that give Middle-earth its unique character.</p><p>The project has developed gradually around games that were actually played. Every rule we add therefore seeks to answer a situation that arises at the table: creating a character from Tolkien's world, making magic more discreet and more dangerous, differentiating cultures, recovering certain MERP traditions, or allowing Foundry VTT to support the game without replacing it.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Our Approach","text":{"content":"<h1>Our Approach</h1><p>MERP-RMU follows a simple principle: <strong>keep RMU when it already meets the need correctly, adapt when an older mechanic deserves to be carried over, and create only when Middle-earth requires something that RMU does not provide</strong>.</p><p>This method avoids stacking two game systems on top of one another. When a Talent, Skill, Profession, or rule already has a functional equivalent in RMU, we use the Core mechanism. When older rules rely on concepts that no longer exist — former action types, development costs, Everyman categories, multipliers, or bonuses that have become excessive — they are rewritten in RMU's mechanical language.</p><p>The adaptations are also placed back into the context of Middle-earth. Magic remains rare, rooted in peoples and traditions, and its use can have consequences. Cultures have real importance during character creation. Spell Lists are not merely abstract catalogues: their availability depends on the Age, teaching, and sometimes the influence of the Shadow.</p><p>Some parts of the project are explicitly presented as <strong>beta</strong>. They are intended to be tested by players before being considered final. The economy, certain reconstructed Professions, or mechanics specific to Middle-earth may therefore evolve in response to actual play.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"What the Project Contains","text":{"content":"<h1>What the Project Contains</h1><p>MERP-RMU is no longer limited to converting character creation. The work now covers several levels of the game.</p><h2>Peoples and Cultures</h2><p>The Races and Cultures of Middle-earth are adapted to the RMU structure, including their characteristics, languages, cultural skills, restrictions, and availability by Age.</p><h2>Professions</h2><p>Professions drawn from the MERP and Rolemaster traditions are rebuilt on stable RMU chassis. Their descriptions detail their role in Middle-earth, their Prime Stats, Professional Skills, Spell Lists, and the adaptation choices that were made.</p><h2>Magic</h2><p>The project adapts Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism to Middle-earth, with rules for discreet use of magic, the decline of magical traditions, Sorcery, the Morgoth Element, Corruption, and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>. Lists and Skills specific to certain cultures are also integrated when the sources justify them.</p><h2>Skills, Talents, and Flaws</h2><p>Elements already covered by the RMU Core remain in the Core. Abilities genuinely specific to MERP or Middle-earth are converted into RMU equivalents, with costs and Tiers adjusted to the modern scale of the system.</p><h2>Herbs, Substances, and Items</h2><p>Herbs, remedies, poisons, foods, and substances from older sources are converted into RMU Items and classified according to their use. Their environment, search difficulty, preparation, and effects are presented in a form ready for play.</p><h2>Foundry VTT and MERP-UI</h2><p>MERP-UI handles the technical integration: Compendium content, character creation, translations, automations, magic rules, Journals, play aids, and tools for the Game Master. The goal is for the interface to support the rules without requiring players to understand RMU's internal technical details.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Sources and Credits","text":{"content":"<h1>Sources and Credits</h1><p>MERP-RMU draws very heavily on the excellent <em>MERP™ Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP</em>, version 2.5 dated 13 September 2005, designed by <strong>Whitford Bates</strong>. This work has been an essential source for Cultures, Professions, Background Options, and many rules specific to Middle-earth.</p><p>We also wish to credit <strong>Jonathon Dale</strong> and the contributors to the <strong>merp-fan-modules</strong> community, whose work has helped preserve and enrich Middle-earth gaming material for many years.</p><p>MERP-RMU also builds on <strong>Rolemaster Unified</strong> and its Foundry VTT implementation. Whenever RMU already provides a functional solution, that solution remains the mechanical reference for the adaptation.</p><p>Finally, the many MERP and Rolemaster supplements devoted to Middle-earth remain an essential source of inspiration for the setting, peoples, places, traditions, and adventures.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"A Free Community Project","text":{"content":"<h1>A Free Community Project</h1><p>MERP-RMU is a <strong>non-commercial community project</strong>. It is intended to be shared freely among players and Game Masters who wish to use Rolemaster Unified in Middle-earth.</p><p>The adaptations, translations, Journals, Foundry data, scripts, presentations, and other elements created specifically for MERP-RMU are made available in that spirit. They must not be resold, repackaged, or incorporated into a paid module by a third party.</p><p><strong>Free community project — resale prohibited.</strong></p><p>This approach obviously grants no rights over works, trademarks, or texts belonging to their respective rights holders. MERP-RMU is intended as a tribute to these games and as a way for a community of enthusiasts to continue playing in this setting with current tools.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"About the Adaptation","text":{"content":"<h1>About the Adaptation</h1><p>My name is <strong>Jean-Brice Gayet</strong>. I have been playing roleplaying games for many years, with a particular attachment to <strong>Middle-earth, MERP, and Rolemaster</strong>, and I also devote a great deal of time to creating and adapting content for <strong>Foundry VTT</strong>.</p><p>This work began with a very simple idea: to be able to run Middle-earth adventures with Rolemaster Unified in Foundry. As the work progressed, the project expanded. A simple rules conversion gradually became a coherent body of content: characters, cultures, professions, magic, skills, items, Journals, translations, automations, and gaming tools.</p><p>MERP-RMU remains a living project. Its purpose is not to claim to provide a definitive version of Middle-earth for RMU, but to offer a solid, playable, and documented foundation, then improve it through actual games, player feedback, and experience gained in campaigns.</p>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"MERP-UI Installation & Settings Guide","text":{"content":"<h1>MERP-UI Installation & Settings Guide</h1><p>This page summarises the main settings to review before starting character creation.</p><p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"modules/merp-ui/assets/guides/merp-ui-settings-guide-en.webp\" alt=\"MERP-UI / MERP-RMU Settings Guide\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;border:0;\"></p>"},"title":{"show":false},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"settingsGuidePage":true,"guideLanguage":"en"}}}],"_id":"aRDgzw0QbDWsJSBT"}
{"name":"Morgoth Element and Corruption","sort":50000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"morgoth-corruption","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"morgoth-corruption","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Élément de Morgoth et Corruption","pages":[{"name":"Élément de Morgoth et Corruption","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Élément de Morgoth et Corruption</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>Morgoth a diffusé une grande partie de sa puissance dans Arda afin de la dominer. Cette influence, appelée <strong>Élément de Morgoth</strong>, demeure présente à des degrés divers dans le monde.</p><ul><li>Test de Corruption pour la <strong>Sorcellerie</strong>.</li><li>Test possible lorsqu’un sort est employé pour terrifier, tromper, subjuguer, contraindre, contrôler ou dominer la libre volonté.</li><li>Le sort est résolu normalement, puis le lanceur effectue une <strong>RR contre l’Essence</strong>, niveau d’attaque = niveau du sort.</li><li>Sorcellerie : <strong>-15 à la RR</strong> et <strong>PC gagnés doublés</strong>.</li><li>La Corruption peut également être réduite par une <strong>purification volontaire</strong> : renoncement, réparation, rituel approprié, aide d’un lieu ou d’un guide spirituel exceptionnel.</li><li>Un personnage ne peut pas provoquer volontairement des tests de Corruption dans le seul but d’obtenir des réductions de PC sur une forte réussite.</li></ul><p>Le MJ peut exempter un sort approprié employé dans un but clairement pur. Les Points de Corruption commencent normalement à 0 et, à 100+, le personnage est considéré comme suffisamment mauvais pour être affecté par les sorts ciblant le Mal. La Corruption est plus facile à acquérir qu’à perdre, mais la rédemption doit rester possible par des choix significatifs.</p></section><h2>Morgoth et Arda</h2><p>Le désir de Morgoth de dominer Arda et de la remodeler selon sa propre vision l’amena à diffuser une grande partie de sa puissance naturelle dans le monde lui-même. En s’engageant toujours davantage dans la domination du monde matériel, il se lia profondément à Arda jusqu’à investir une part considérable de sa propre puissance dans sa substance.</p><h2>Une influence présente partout</h2><p>L’Élément de Morgoth n’est pas limité à un territoire, une forteresse ou un objet. Il est présent, en quantités variables, dans toutes les choses qui appartiennent à Arda ou qui en sont issues. Certaines matières, certains lieux et certaines créations peuvent porter cette influence avec une intensité bien plus importante que d’autres.</p><h2>Une corruption latente</h2><p>Cette influence reste souvent dormante. La simple existence dans Arda ne provoque pas automatiquement une accumulation constante de Points de Corruption. Ce qui devient dangereux est la manière dont certaines actions, certains objets et surtout certains usages de la magie ouvrent le personnage à cette influence.</p><h2>La magie n’est pas intrinsèquement corruptrice</h2><p>L’Essence, la Canalisation et le Mentalisme ne sont pas par nature des expressions de Morgoth. La Corruption intervient lorsque la manière dont le pouvoir est recherché ou utilisé entre en résonance avec la volonté de domination qui caractérise Morgoth.</p><h2>L’intention</h2><p>Un test de Corruption peut être requis lorsque le personnage utilise la magie pour <strong>terrifier, tromper, subjuguer, contraindre, contrôler ou dominer la libre volonté d’un autre être</strong>. Le critère fondamental n’est donc pas seulement le sort mais également l’intention avec laquelle il est employé.</p><h3>Dominer la libre volonté</h3><p>La domination constitue l’un des thèmes centraux de l’Élément de Morgoth. Un lanceur qui substitue sa volonté à celle d’un autre reproduit, à une échelle réduite, le même principe de domination.</p><h3>Terrifier et tromper</h3><p>La peur ordinaire n’est pas nécessairement corruptrice ; la règle concerne l’utilisation délibérée du pouvoir pour briser ou manipuler le comportement par la terreur. Toutes les illusions ne sont pas automatiquement corruptrices non plus : une illusion qui protège des réfugiés n’a pas le même sens qu’une illusion destinée à soumettre une personne.</p><h3>Contraindre et contrôler</h3><p>La contrainte devient dangereuse lorsque la magie force une autre volonté à suivre celle du lanceur. Les sorts de contrôle peuvent être extrêmement utiles, ce qui rend précisément leur facilité tentante et la règle de Corruption importante.</p><h2>Sorcellerie</h2><p>Les sorts classés comme Sorcellerie entretiennent une relation encore plus directe avec l’Élément de Morgoth. Ils ont été conçus pour dominer, contrôler ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda et tirent leur puissance de cet Élément. Ils sont toujours soumis au test de Corruption.</p><h2>Résolution normale du sort</h2><p>Le test de Corruption ne remplace pas le lancement. Le sort est d’abord résolu normalement selon RMU. Ensuite, MERP-RMU détermine si son usage déclenche un test de Corruption.</p><h2>Test de Résistance</h2><p>Lorsqu’un test est requis, le lanceur effectue une <strong>RR contre l’Essence</strong> avec un niveau d’attaque égal au niveau du sort. Le MJ peut préférer la variante consistant à effectuer la RR contre le Royaume du sort ; cette variante doit rester optionnelle.</p><h2>Points de Corruption</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Résultat de la RR</th><th>Points de Corruption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Échec de 101+</td><td>+5</td></tr><tr><td>Échec de 51–100</td><td>+4</td></tr><tr><td>Échec de 26–50</td><td>+3</td></tr><tr><td>Échec de 11–25</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><td>Échec de 1–10</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Réussite de 1–10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Réussite de 11–25</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Réussite de 26–50</td><td>-2</td></tr><tr><td>Réussite de 51–100</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>Réussite de 101+</td><td>-4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Pour un sort ou une Liste classée Sorcellerie, les Points de Corruption <strong>gagnés</strong> sont doublés. Les pertes de PC obtenues sur une forte réussite ne sont pas doublées.</p><h2>Modificateurs de la RR</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Modificateur</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sort DE / BE</td><td>-20</td></tr><tr><td>Sort E / F</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>Sort P</td><td>-5</td></tr><tr><td>Sort U</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>Sort I</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Sorcellerie</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>Total actuel de PC</td><td>0 à -100</td></tr><tr><td>Bonus racial</td><td>variable</td></tr><tr><td>3 × bonus d’Empathie</td><td>-30 à +30</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Les sorts agressifs imposent les plus fortes pénalités. La Sorcellerie ajoute encore -15. Le total actuel de PC intervient lui-même comme modificateur, de sorte qu’un personnage déjà corrompu résiste de moins en moins bien aux nouvelles expositions.</p><h2>Empathie et bonus racial</h2><p>Le test ajoute trois fois le bonus d’Empathie, dans une plage de -30 à +30, ainsi qu’un bonus racial variable. Lors de l’intégration, ces valeurs devront être récupérées depuis les données RMU et MERP plutôt que reconstruites approximativement.</p><h2>Intentions pures</h2><p>Le MJ peut ne pas attribuer de Corruption lorsqu’un sort <strong>approprié</strong> est utilisé dans un but clairement pur : combattre directement le Mal, protéger les faibles ou soigner les blessés. L’intention ne suffit pas seule ; le moyen doit être proportionné et approprié.</p><h3>Exemple de proportion</h3><p>Un Fire Bolt n’est pas une réponse appropriée contre un simple tyran local ou un voleur, alors qu’un sort de Sleep peut l’être. À l’inverse, une magie puissante contre une véritable force des Ténèbres peut être totalement appropriée.</p><h2>Points initiaux et seuil de 100</h2><p>Le total de Points de Corruption commence normalement à zéro. À <strong>100 PC ou plus</strong>, le personnage est considéré comme suffisamment mauvais pour être affecté par les sorts ciblant spécifiquement le Mal.</p><h2>Objets maléfiques</h2><p>La Corruption peut également venir d’objets maléfiques. Lorsqu’un objet est soumis à cette règle, son utilisation peut imposer une RR contre l’Essence avec un niveau d’attaque correspondant au niveau de l’objet ; à défaut de règle plus précise, la source propose un niveau 10.</p><h2>Actions non magiques</h2><p>Le MJ peut utiliser une règle optionnelle de tests de Corruption pour certaines actions non magiques ou expositions particulièrement corruptrices. Le test utilise alors une Manœuvre Statique appropriée modifiée par trois fois le bonus d’Autodiscipline. Cette option doit rester distincte de la règle normale.</p><h2>Purification et réduction de la Corruption</h2><p>Une forte réussite à une RR de Corruption peut faire perdre des Points de Corruption, mais elle ne doit pas constituer la seule voie permettant à un personnage de se purifier. La règle MERP prévoit explicitement que certains rituels accomplis dans certains lieux puissent retirer des Points de Corruption. Elle ne fournit cependant pas de procédure universelle pour ces purifications.</p><p>MERP-RMU développe cette possibilité sous la forme d’un processus de <strong>renoncement, de réparation et de purification</strong>. Cette extension est une règle d’adaptation destinée à éviter qu’un personnage ne doive rechercher de nouvelles activités corruptrices pour espérer diminuer son total de PC.</p><h3>Principe général</h3><p>La purification ne fonctionne pas comme une récompense automatique pour une bonne action. Elle devient possible lorsque le personnage choisit volontairement de s’opposer à ce qui l’a corrompu, renonce à l’avantage qu’il en retirait et entreprend, lorsque cela est possible, de réparer les conséquences de ses actes.</p><p>Un personnage corrompu par des pouvoirs de domination peut ainsi devoir cesser de les employer, libérer ceux qu’il contrôlait ou renoncer à une position acquise grâce à eux. Un personnage lié à un objet maléfique devra généralement rompre ce lien ou abandonner l’objet avant qu’une purification durable puisse être envisagée.</p><h3>Rituels et lieux de purification</h3><p>Certains lieux exceptionnellement préservés de l’influence de l’Ombre peuvent permettre ou faciliter une purification. Il peut s’agir d’un sanctuaire eldarin ancien, d’un lieu consacré à une Puissance fidèle, d’un refuge particulièrement protégé ou d’un site possédant une importance spirituelle réelle dans la campagne.</p><p>La présence dans un tel lieu ne retire jamais automatiquement des PC. Le lieu fournit les conditions nécessaires au processus ; la volonté du personnage, son renoncement et la nature du rituel restent déterminants.</p><h3>Guides spirituels</h3><p>Des individus exceptionnels peuvent également assister une purification : certains Eldar anciens, de grands guérisseurs, des sages ou des utilisateurs de Canalisation disposant d’une autorité spirituelle particulière. Leur intervention ne remplace pas le choix du personnage et ne doit pas fonctionner comme un simple sort de « suppression de Corruption ».</p><h3>Tentative de Purification</h3><p>Lorsqu’une période significative de renoncement, de repentance ou de réparation a été accomplie et que les circonstances le justifient, le MJ peut autoriser une <strong>Tentative de Purification</strong>. Par défaut, MERP-RMU la traite comme une <strong>Manœuvre Absolue d’Autodiscipline</strong>, éventuellement modifiée par la qualité du rituel, du lieu, de l’aide reçue et par le total actuel de PC.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Résultat de la Tentative de Purification</th><th>Effet conseillé</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Échec</td><td>Aucun PC retiré</td></tr><tr><td>Succès partiel</td><td>Aucun PC retiré, mais le processus progresse et peut justifier une nouvelle tentative ultérieure</td></tr><tr><td>Succès</td><td>-1 PC</td></tr><tr><td>Succès absolu</td><td>-2 PC</td></tr><tr><td>Succès exceptionnel</td><td>-3 PC</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Cette table est une règle MERP-RMU et non une table reproduite du texte MERP. Le MJ peut l’adapter à la gravité de la situation, mais la réduction doit rester lente : la Corruption est volontairement plus facile à acquérir qu’à effacer.</p><h3>Difficulté selon la Corruption actuelle</h3><p>Plus un personnage est corrompu, plus il est difficile de revenir en arrière. Le MJ doit donc augmenter progressivement la difficulté de la Tentative de Purification lorsque le total de PC devient important. Un personnage faiblement touché peut encore se détourner relativement facilement de cette influence, tandis qu’un personnage proche de 100 PC devrait avoir besoin d’un changement profond, d’une aide exceptionnelle ou d’un événement majeur.</p><p>À titre de guide narratif : <strong>1–10 PC</strong> représentent une influence encore limitée ; <strong>11–25 PC</strong> une habitude ou une compromission déjà réelle ; <strong>26–50 PC</strong> une corruption installée ; <strong>51–75 PC</strong> une dépendance spirituelle profonde ; <strong>76–99 PC</strong> une situation critique où la purification devient extrêmement difficile. Ces seuils servent à guider le MJ et ne remplacent pas les effets mécaniques déjà définis.</p><h3>Actes de renoncement et de sacrifice</h3><p>Un acte réellement désintéressé impliquant un coût personnel important peut exceptionnellement justifier une réduction directe de Corruption ou ouvrir immédiatement la possibilité d’une Tentative de Purification. Cela peut inclure l’abandon volontaire d’un objet de pouvoir, la destruction d’une source corruptrice dont le personnage aurait pu profiter, le refus d’une tentation majeure ou un sacrifice accompli pour protéger autrui sans bénéfice personnel.</p><p>Ces réductions doivent rester rares et décidées par le MJ. Elles ne constituent jamais une économie de « bonnes actions contre des PC » et ne doivent pas pouvoir être planifiées ou répétées mécaniquement.</p><h3>Rompre avec la source de Corruption</h3><p>Lorsqu’une source de Corruption demeure active, la purification peut être impossible ou seulement temporaire. Un personnage continuant à utiliser régulièrement un objet maléfique, une Liste de Sorcellerie ou une relation corruptrice ne peut normalement pas prétendre à une véritable purification sans commencer par rompre volontairement ce lien.</p><h3>Interdiction de provoquer des tests</h3><p>Un personnage ne peut jamais provoquer volontairement un test de Corruption dans le seul but d’obtenir une forte réussite et de perdre des PC. Le test représente une réaction à une exposition réelle à l’influence corruptrice, pas une procédure de purification. Lorsqu’une exposition est recherchée uniquement pour exploiter la table, le MJ n’accorde aucune réduction de PC.</p><h3>Rôle des fortes réussites de RR</h3><p>Les pertes de PC obtenues sur une forte réussite à une RR de Corruption restent valables. Elles représentent une victoire intérieure immédiate : confronté à une influence corruptrice réelle, le personnage la rejette avec une force suffisante pour affaiblir une part de la Corruption déjà présente. Cette possibilité complète la purification volontaire mais ne la remplace pas.</p><h2>Corruption progressive</h2><p>Un premier Point de Corruption ne transforme pas immédiatement un personnage en serviteur de Sauron. Les PC représentent une tendance progressive vers le Mal. Plus ils augmentent, plus la domination, l’égoïsme et la destruction de l’ordre naturel peuvent devenir faciles à justifier.</p><h2>Différence avec l’attention de Sauron</h2><p>Le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre répond à « les forces de Sauron ont-elles détecté ce sort ? ». La Corruption répond à « cet usage du pouvoir expose-t-il le lanceur à l’influence de Morgoth ? ». Un même sort peut déclencher les deux, un seul ou aucun des mécanismes.</p><h2>Exemples</h2><p>Un Magicien utilisant un sort de Feu contre des Orcs attaquant des villageois peut déclencher un Jet de Risque sans test de Corruption si l’usage est approprié. Le même Magicien employant une magie spectaculaire pour terrifier un paysan afin d’obtenir des informations déclenche logiquement les deux mécanismes.</p><p>Un utilisateur de Mentalisme imposant un ordre à une cible peut déclencher un test de Corruption même si le sort est visuellement discret. Une Liste explicitement classée Sorcellerie déclenche le test automatiquement, indépendamment du spectacle produit.</p><h2>Automatisation MERP-RMU</h2><p>Au lancement, le module pourra vérifier la Liste et le sort, leur flag Sorcellerie, le type RMU, le niveau, les PC actuels, le bonus racial et l’Empathie. Les cas objectifs seront calculés automatiquement ; pour les sorts ordinaires, le MJ conservera la décision sur l’intention.</p><p>Les Points de Corruption devront être stockés sur l’Actor avec un historique des gains et pertes. Une option de campagne pourra choisir entre RR contre l’Essence et RR contre le Royaume du sort.</p><h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>La manière dont un personnage choisit d’exercer son pouvoir finit par agir sur celui qui l’exerce. La Corruption n’est pas une punition pour toute magie ; elle représente la proximité progressive avec la logique fondamentale de Morgoth : domination, contrôle et altération de l’ordre naturel.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Morgoth Element and Corruption","pages":[{"name":"Morgoth Element and Corruption","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Morgoth Element and Corruption</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Morgoth dispersed much of his power into Arda in order to dominate it. This influence, called the <strong>Morgoth Element</strong>, remains present in varying degrees throughout the world.</p><ul><li>Corruption Test for <strong>Sorcery</strong>.</li><li>Possible test when a spell is used to terrify, deceive, subjugate, compel, control, or dominate free will.</li><li>The spell is resolved normally, then the caster makes an <strong>RR against Essence</strong>, with attack level equal to the spell level.</li><li>Sorcery: <strong>-15 to the RR</strong> and <strong>Corruption Points gained are doubled</strong>.</li><li>Corruption can also be reduced through <strong>voluntary purification</strong>: renunciation, reparation, an appropriate ritual, or aid from an exceptional place or spiritual guide.</li><li>A character may not deliberately provoke Corruption Tests solely in the hope of reducing CP through a high success.</li></ul><p>The GM may exempt an appropriate spell used for a clearly pure purpose. Corruption Points normally begin at 0 and, at 100+, the character is considered evil enough to be affected by spells that specifically target Evil. Corruption is easier to acquire than to lose, but redemption must remain possible through meaningful choices.</p></section><h2>Morgoth and Arda</h2><p>Morgoth's desire to dominate Arda and reshape it according to his own vision led him to disperse a great part of his natural power into the world itself. By committing himself ever more deeply to domination of the material world, he became profoundly bound to Arda and invested a considerable portion of his own power into its substance.</p><h2>An Influence Present Everywhere</h2><p>The Morgoth Element is not confined to a territory, fortress, or object. It is present, in varying quantities, in everything that belongs to Arda or derives from it. Some substances, places, and creations can bear this influence far more intensely than others.</p><h2>Latent Corruption</h2><p>This influence often remains dormant. Merely existing in Arda does not automatically cause a constant accumulation of Corruption Points. What becomes dangerous is the way certain actions, certain objects, and above all certain uses of magic open the character to that influence.</p><h2>Magic Is Not Inherently Corrupting</h2><p>Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism are not by their nature expressions of Morgoth. Corruption enters when the way power is sought or used resonates with the will to dominate that characterises Morgoth.</p><h2>Intent</h2><p>A Corruption Test may be required when the character uses magic to <strong>terrify, deceive, subjugate, compel, control, or dominate the free will of another being</strong>. The fundamental criterion is therefore not only the spell, but also the intent with which it is used.</p><h3>Dominating Free Will</h3><p>Domination is one of the central themes of the Morgoth Element. A caster who replaces another being's will with their own reproduces, on a smaller scale, the same principle of domination.</p><h3>Terrifying and Deceiving</h3><p>Ordinary fear is not necessarily corrupting; the rule concerns the deliberate use of power to break or manipulate behaviour through terror. Not every illusion is automatically corrupting either: an illusion that protects refugees does not carry the same meaning as one intended to subjugate a person.</p><h3>Compelling and Controlling</h3><p>Compulsion becomes dangerous when magic forces another will to follow the caster's. Control spells can be extremely useful, which is precisely what makes their convenience tempting and the Corruption rule important.</p><h2>Sorcery</h2><p>Spells classified as Sorcery have an even more direct relationship with the Morgoth Element. They were designed to dominate, control, or destroy the natural order of Arda and draw their power from that Element. They are always subject to a Corruption Test.</p><h2>Normal Resolution of the Spell</h2><p>The Corruption Test does not replace casting. The spell is first resolved normally according to RMU. MERP-RMU then determines whether its use triggers a Corruption Test.</p><h2>Resistance Roll</h2><p>When a test is required, the caster makes an <strong>RR against Essence</strong> with an attack level equal to the spell's level. The GM may prefer the variant of making the RR against the spell's Realm; that variant should remain optional.</p><h2>Corruption Points</h2><table><thead><tr><th>RR Result</th><th>Corruption Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Failure by 101+</td><td>+5</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 51–100</td><td>+4</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 26–50</td><td>+3</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 11–25</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 1–10</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 1–10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 11–25</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 26–50</td><td>-2</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 51–100</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 101+</td><td>-4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For a spell or List classified as Sorcery, Corruption Points <strong>gained</strong> are doubled. CP lost through a high successful roll are not doubled.</p><h2>RR Modifiers</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Modifier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DE / BE Spell</td><td>-20</td></tr><tr><td>E / F Spell</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>P Spell</td><td>-5</td></tr><tr><td>U Spell</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>I Spell</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Sorcery</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>Current CP Total</td><td>0 to -100</td></tr><tr><td>Racial Bonus</td><td>variable</td></tr><tr><td>3 × Empathy Bonus</td><td>-30 to +30</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Aggressive spells impose the strongest penalties. Sorcery adds a further -15. The current CP total itself is also applied as a modifier, so a character already corrupted becomes progressively less able to resist further exposure.</p><h2>Empathy and Racial Bonus</h2><p>The test adds three times the Empathy bonus, within a range of -30 to +30, as well as a variable racial bonus. During integration, these values should be retrieved from RMU and MERP data rather than reconstructed approximately.</p><h2>Pure Intentions</h2><p>The GM may choose not to assign Corruption when an <strong>appropriate</strong> spell is used for a clearly pure purpose: directly fighting Evil, protecting the weak, or healing the wounded. Intent alone is not enough; the means must also be proportionate and appropriate.</p><h3>Example of Proportion</h3><p>A Fire Bolt is not an appropriate response to a mere local tyrant or thief, whereas a Sleep spell may be. Conversely, powerful magic used against a genuine force of Darkness can be entirely appropriate.</p><h2>Starting Points and the 100 Threshold</h2><p>The Corruption Point total normally begins at zero. At <strong>100 CP or more</strong>, the character is considered evil enough to be affected by spells that specifically target Evil.</p><h2>Evil Items</h2><p>Corruption can also come from evil items. When an item is subject to this rule, using it may require an RR against Essence with an attack level corresponding to the item's level; if no more precise rule applies, the source suggests level 10.</p><h2>Non-Magical Actions</h2><p>The GM may use an optional Corruption Test rule for certain non-magical actions or particularly corrupting exposures. The test then uses an appropriate Static Maneuver modified by three times the Self Discipline bonus. This option must remain distinct from the normal rule.</p><h2>Purification and Reducing Corruption</h2><p>A high success on a Corruption RR can remove Corruption Points, but it should not be the only route by which a character can purify themselves. The MERP rule explicitly provides that certain rituals performed in certain places can remove Corruption Points. It does not, however, provide a universal procedure for such purifications.</p><p>MERP-RMU develops this possibility as a process of <strong>renunciation, reparation, and purification</strong>. This extension is an adaptation rule intended to prevent a character from having to seek out further corrupting activity merely in the hope of lowering their CP total.</p><h3>General Principle</h3><p>Purification does not function as an automatic reward for a good deed. It becomes possible when the character voluntarily chooses to oppose what has corrupted them, gives up the advantage gained from it, and undertakes, where possible, to repair the consequences of their actions.</p><p>A character corrupted through powers of domination may therefore have to stop using them, free those they controlled, or give up a position acquired through them. A character bound to an evil item will generally need to break that bond or abandon the item before lasting purification can be considered.</p><h3>Rituals and Places of Purification</h3><p>Certain places exceptionally preserved from the influence of the Shadow may permit or facilitate purification. These might be an ancient Eldarin sanctuary, a place consecrated to a faithful Power, an especially protected refuge, or a site possessing genuine spiritual significance in the campaign.</p><p>Simply being in such a place never removes CP automatically. The place provides the necessary conditions for the process; the character's will, renunciation, and the nature of the ritual remain decisive.</p><h3>Spiritual Guides</h3><p>Exceptional individuals may also assist with purification: certain ancient Eldar, great healers, sages, or Channeling users possessing particular spiritual authority. Their intervention does not replace the character's choice and should not function as a simple “remove Corruption” spell.</p><h3>Purification Attempt</h3><p>Once a meaningful period of renunciation, repentance, or reparation has been completed and circumstances justify it, the GM may allow a <strong>Purification Attempt</strong>. By default, MERP-RMU treats this as an <strong>Absolute Self Discipline Maneuver</strong>, potentially modified by the quality of the ritual, the place, the assistance received, and the current CP total.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Purification Attempt Result</th><th>Suggested Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Failure</td><td>No CP removed</td></tr><tr><td>Partial Success</td><td>No CP removed, but the process progresses and may justify another attempt later</td></tr><tr><td>Success</td><td>-1 CP</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute Success</td><td>-2 CP</td></tr><tr><td>Exceptional Success</td><td>-3 CP</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This table is a MERP-RMU rule, not a table reproduced from the MERP text. The GM may adapt it to the severity of the situation, but reduction should remain slow: Corruption is deliberately easier to acquire than to erase.</p><h3>Difficulty According to Current Corruption</h3><p>The more corrupted a character is, the harder it becomes to turn back. The GM should therefore increase the difficulty of the Purification Attempt progressively as the CP total rises. A lightly affected character may still turn away from the influence relatively easily, while a character close to 100 CP should require a profound change, exceptional aid, or a major event.</p><p>As a narrative guide: <strong>1–10 CP</strong> represent still-limited influence; <strong>11–25 CP</strong> a habit or compromise that has become real; <strong>26–50 CP</strong> established corruption; <strong>51–75 CP</strong> deep spiritual dependence; <strong>76–99 CP</strong> a critical situation in which purification becomes extremely difficult. These thresholds guide the GM and do not replace already-defined mechanical effects.</p><h3>Acts of Renunciation and Sacrifice</h3><p>A genuinely selfless act involving substantial personal cost may exceptionally justify a direct reduction in Corruption or immediately open the possibility of a Purification Attempt. This may include voluntarily abandoning an item of power, destroying a corrupting source the character could have exploited, refusing a major temptation, or making a sacrifice to protect others without personal benefit.</p><p>Such reductions should remain rare and be decided by the GM. They never constitute an economy of “good deeds in exchange for CP” and must not be something that can be planned or repeated mechanically.</p><h3>Breaking with the Source of Corruption</h3><p>When a source of Corruption remains active, purification may be impossible or only temporary. A character who continues to use an evil item, a Sorcery List, or a corrupting relationship regularly cannot normally claim genuine purification without first voluntarily breaking that bond.</p><h3>Prohibition on Provoking Tests</h3><p>A character may never deliberately provoke a Corruption Test solely to obtain a high success and lose CP. The test represents a reaction to genuine exposure to corrupting influence, not a purification procedure. When exposure is sought only to exploit the table, the GM grants no reduction in CP.</p><h3>The Role of High RR Successes</h3><p>CP losses resulting from a high success on a Corruption RR remain valid. They represent an immediate inner victory: confronted with a real corrupting influence, the character rejects it strongly enough to weaken some of the Corruption already present. This possibility complements voluntary purification but does not replace it.</p><h2>Progressive Corruption</h2><p>A first Corruption Point does not immediately turn a character into a servant of Sauron. CP represent a progressive tendency toward Evil. As they increase, domination, selfishness, and the destruction of the natural order may become easier to justify.</p><h2>Difference from Sauron's Attention</h2><p>The Risk of Attracting the Shadow answers “have Sauron's forces detected this spell?” Corruption answers “does this use of power expose the caster to Morgoth's influence?” The same spell may trigger both mechanisms, one of them, or neither.</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>A Magician using a Fire spell against Orcs attacking villagers may trigger a Risk Roll without a Corruption Test if the use is appropriate. The same Magician using spectacular magic to terrify a peasant into providing information logically triggers both mechanisms.</p><p>A Mentalism user imposing a command on a target may trigger a Corruption Test even if the spell is visually discreet. A List explicitly classified as Sorcery triggers the test automatically, regardless of how spectacular the spell appears.</p><h2>MERP-RMU Automation</h2><p>When a spell is cast, the module can check the List and spell, their Sorcery flag, RMU type, level, current CP, racial bonus, and Empathy. Objective cases will be calculated automatically; for ordinary spells, the GM retains the decision regarding intent.</p><p>Corruption Points should be stored on the Actor along with a history of gains and losses. A campaign option may choose between an RR against Essence and an RR against the spell's Realm.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>The way a character chooses to exercise power ultimately acts upon the one who exercises it. Corruption is not a punishment for all magic; it represents growing proximity to Morgoth's fundamental logic: domination, control, and alteration of the natural order.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Morgoth Element and Corruption","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Morgoth Element and Corruption</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Morgoth dispersed much of his power into Arda in order to dominate it. This influence, called the <strong>Morgoth Element</strong>, remains present in varying degrees throughout the world.</p><ul><li>Corruption Test for <strong>Sorcery</strong>.</li><li>Possible test when a spell is used to terrify, deceive, subjugate, compel, control, or dominate free will.</li><li>The spell is resolved normally, then the caster makes an <strong>RR against Essence</strong>, with attack level equal to the spell level.</li><li>Sorcery: <strong>-15 to the RR</strong> and <strong>Corruption Points gained are doubled</strong>.</li><li>Corruption can also be reduced through <strong>voluntary purification</strong>: renunciation, reparation, an appropriate ritual, or aid from an exceptional place or spiritual guide.</li><li>A character may not deliberately provoke Corruption Tests solely in the hope of reducing CP through a high success.</li></ul><p>The GM may exempt an appropriate spell used for a clearly pure purpose. Corruption Points normally begin at 0 and, at 100+, the character is considered evil enough to be affected by spells that specifically target Evil. Corruption is easier to acquire than to lose, but redemption must remain possible through meaningful choices.</p></section><h2>Morgoth and Arda</h2><p>Morgoth's desire to dominate Arda and reshape it according to his own vision led him to disperse a great part of his natural power into the world itself. By committing himself ever more deeply to domination of the material world, he became profoundly bound to Arda and invested a considerable portion of his own power into its substance.</p><h2>An Influence Present Everywhere</h2><p>The Morgoth Element is not confined to a territory, fortress, or object. It is present, in varying quantities, in everything that belongs to Arda or derives from it. Some substances, places, and creations can bear this influence far more intensely than others.</p><h2>Latent Corruption</h2><p>This influence often remains dormant. Merely existing in Arda does not automatically cause a constant accumulation of Corruption Points. What becomes dangerous is the way certain actions, certain objects, and above all certain uses of magic open the character to that influence.</p><h2>Magic Is Not Inherently Corrupting</h2><p>Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism are not by their nature expressions of Morgoth. Corruption enters when the way power is sought or used resonates with the will to dominate that characterises Morgoth.</p><h2>Intent</h2><p>A Corruption Test may be required when the character uses magic to <strong>terrify, deceive, subjugate, compel, control, or dominate the free will of another being</strong>. The fundamental criterion is therefore not only the spell, but also the intent with which it is used.</p><h3>Dominating Free Will</h3><p>Domination is one of the central themes of the Morgoth Element. A caster who replaces another being's will with their own reproduces, on a smaller scale, the same principle of domination.</p><h3>Terrifying and Deceiving</h3><p>Ordinary fear is not necessarily corrupting; the rule concerns the deliberate use of power to break or manipulate behaviour through terror. Not every illusion is automatically corrupting either: an illusion that protects refugees does not carry the same meaning as one intended to subjugate a person.</p><h3>Compelling and Controlling</h3><p>Compulsion becomes dangerous when magic forces another will to follow the caster's. Control spells can be extremely useful, which is precisely what makes their convenience tempting and the Corruption rule important.</p><h2>Sorcery</h2><p>Spells classified as Sorcery have an even more direct relationship with the Morgoth Element. They were designed to dominate, control, or destroy the natural order of Arda and draw their power from that Element. They are always subject to a Corruption Test.</p><h2>Normal Resolution of the Spell</h2><p>The Corruption Test does not replace casting. The spell is first resolved normally according to RMU. MERP-RMU then determines whether its use triggers a Corruption Test.</p><h2>Resistance Roll</h2><p>When a test is required, the caster makes an <strong>RR against Essence</strong> with an attack level equal to the spell's level. The GM may prefer the variant of making the RR against the spell's Realm; that variant should remain optional.</p><h2>Corruption Points</h2><table><thead><tr><th>RR Result</th><th>Corruption Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Failure by 101+</td><td>+5</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 51–100</td><td>+4</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 26–50</td><td>+3</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 11–25</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><td>Failure by 1–10</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 1–10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 11–25</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 26–50</td><td>-2</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 51–100</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>Success by 101+</td><td>-4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For a spell or List classified as Sorcery, Corruption Points <strong>gained</strong> are doubled. CP lost through a high successful roll are not doubled.</p><h2>RR Modifiers</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Modifier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DE / BE Spell</td><td>-20</td></tr><tr><td>E / F Spell</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>P Spell</td><td>-5</td></tr><tr><td>U Spell</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>I Spell</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>Sorcery</td><td>-15</td></tr><tr><td>Current CP Total</td><td>0 to -100</td></tr><tr><td>Racial Bonus</td><td>variable</td></tr><tr><td>3 × Empathy Bonus</td><td>-30 to +30</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Aggressive spells impose the strongest penalties. Sorcery adds a further -15. The current CP total itself is also applied as a modifier, so a character already corrupted becomes progressively less able to resist further exposure.</p><h2>Empathy and Racial Bonus</h2><p>The test adds three times the Empathy bonus, within a range of -30 to +30, as well as a variable racial bonus. During integration, these values should be retrieved from RMU and MERP data rather than reconstructed approximately.</p><h2>Pure Intentions</h2><p>The GM may choose not to assign Corruption when an <strong>appropriate</strong> spell is used for a clearly pure purpose: directly fighting Evil, protecting the weak, or healing the wounded. Intent alone is not enough; the means must also be proportionate and appropriate.</p><h3>Example of Proportion</h3><p>A Fire Bolt is not an appropriate response to a mere local tyrant or thief, whereas a Sleep spell may be. Conversely, powerful magic used against a genuine force of Darkness can be entirely appropriate.</p><h2>Starting Points and the 100 Threshold</h2><p>The Corruption Point total normally begins at zero. At <strong>100 CP or more</strong>, the character is considered evil enough to be affected by spells that specifically target Evil.</p><h2>Evil Items</h2><p>Corruption can also come from evil items. When an item is subject to this rule, using it may require an RR against Essence with an attack level corresponding to the item's level; if no more precise rule applies, the source suggests level 10.</p><h2>Non-Magical Actions</h2><p>The GM may use an optional Corruption Test rule for certain non-magical actions or particularly corrupting exposures. The test then uses an appropriate Static Maneuver modified by three times the Self Discipline bonus. This option must remain distinct from the normal rule.</p><h2>Purification and Reducing Corruption</h2><p>A high success on a Corruption RR can remove Corruption Points, but it should not be the only route by which a character can purify themselves. The MERP rule explicitly provides that certain rituals performed in certain places can remove Corruption Points. It does not, however, provide a universal procedure for such purifications.</p><p>MERP-RMU develops this possibility as a process of <strong>renunciation, reparation, and purification</strong>. This extension is an adaptation rule intended to prevent a character from having to seek out further corrupting activity merely in the hope of lowering their CP total.</p><h3>General Principle</h3><p>Purification does not function as an automatic reward for a good deed. It becomes possible when the character voluntarily chooses to oppose what has corrupted them, gives up the advantage gained from it, and undertakes, where possible, to repair the consequences of their actions.</p><p>A character corrupted through powers of domination may therefore have to stop using them, free those they controlled, or give up a position acquired through them. A character bound to an evil item will generally need to break that bond or abandon the item before lasting purification can be considered.</p><h3>Rituals and Places of Purification</h3><p>Certain places exceptionally preserved from the influence of the Shadow may permit or facilitate purification. These might be an ancient Eldarin sanctuary, a place consecrated to a faithful Power, an especially protected refuge, or a site possessing genuine spiritual significance in the campaign.</p><p>Simply being in such a place never removes CP automatically. The place provides the necessary conditions for the process; the character's will, renunciation, and the nature of the ritual remain decisive.</p><h3>Spiritual Guides</h3><p>Exceptional individuals may also assist with purification: certain ancient Eldar, great healers, sages, or Channeling users possessing particular spiritual authority. Their intervention does not replace the character's choice and should not function as a simple “remove Corruption” spell.</p><h3>Purification Attempt</h3><p>Once a meaningful period of renunciation, repentance, or reparation has been completed and circumstances justify it, the GM may allow a <strong>Purification Attempt</strong>. By default, MERP-RMU treats this as an <strong>Absolute Self Discipline Maneuver</strong>, potentially modified by the quality of the ritual, the place, the assistance received, and the current CP total.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Purification Attempt Result</th><th>Suggested Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Failure</td><td>No CP removed</td></tr><tr><td>Partial Success</td><td>No CP removed, but the process progresses and may justify another attempt later</td></tr><tr><td>Success</td><td>-1 CP</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute Success</td><td>-2 CP</td></tr><tr><td>Exceptional Success</td><td>-3 CP</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This table is a MERP-RMU rule, not a table reproduced from the MERP text. The GM may adapt it to the severity of the situation, but reduction should remain slow: Corruption is deliberately easier to acquire than to erase.</p><h3>Difficulty According to Current Corruption</h3><p>The more corrupted a character is, the harder it becomes to turn back. The GM should therefore increase the difficulty of the Purification Attempt progressively as the CP total rises. A lightly affected character may still turn away from the influence relatively easily, while a character close to 100 CP should require a profound change, exceptional aid, or a major event.</p><p>As a narrative guide: <strong>1–10 CP</strong> represent still-limited influence; <strong>11–25 CP</strong> a habit or compromise that has become real; <strong>26–50 CP</strong> established corruption; <strong>51–75 CP</strong> deep spiritual dependence; <strong>76–99 CP</strong> a critical situation in which purification becomes extremely difficult. These thresholds guide the GM and do not replace already-defined mechanical effects.</p><h3>Acts of Renunciation and Sacrifice</h3><p>A genuinely selfless act involving substantial personal cost may exceptionally justify a direct reduction in Corruption or immediately open the possibility of a Purification Attempt. This may include voluntarily abandoning an item of power, destroying a corrupting source the character could have exploited, refusing a major temptation, or making a sacrifice to protect others without personal benefit.</p><p>Such reductions should remain rare and be decided by the GM. They never constitute an economy of “good deeds in exchange for CP” and must not be something that can be planned or repeated mechanically.</p><h3>Breaking with the Source of Corruption</h3><p>When a source of Corruption remains active, purification may be impossible or only temporary. A character who continues to use an evil item, a Sorcery List, or a corrupting relationship regularly cannot normally claim genuine purification without first voluntarily breaking that bond.</p><h3>Prohibition on Provoking Tests</h3><p>A character may never deliberately provoke a Corruption Test solely to obtain a high success and lose CP. The test represents a reaction to genuine exposure to corrupting influence, not a purification procedure. When exposure is sought only to exploit the table, the GM grants no reduction in CP.</p><h3>The Role of High RR Successes</h3><p>CP losses resulting from a high success on a Corruption RR remain valid. They represent an immediate inner victory: confronted with a real corrupting influence, the character rejects it strongly enough to weaken some of the Corruption already present. This possibility complements voluntary purification but does not replace it.</p><h2>Progressive Corruption</h2><p>A first Corruption Point does not immediately turn a character into a servant of Sauron. CP represent a progressive tendency toward Evil. As they increase, domination, selfishness, and the destruction of the natural order may become easier to justify.</p><h2>Difference from Sauron's Attention</h2><p>The Risk of Attracting the Shadow answers “have Sauron's forces detected this spell?” Corruption answers “does this use of power expose the caster to Morgoth's influence?” The same spell may trigger both mechanisms, one of them, or neither.</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>A Magician using a Fire spell against Orcs attacking villagers may trigger a Risk Roll without a Corruption Test if the use is appropriate. The same Magician using spectacular magic to terrify a peasant into providing information logically triggers both mechanisms.</p><p>A Mentalism user imposing a command on a target may trigger a Corruption Test even if the spell is visually discreet. A List explicitly classified as Sorcery triggers the test automatically, regardless of how spectacular the spell appears.</p><h2>MERP-RMU Automation</h2><p>When a spell is cast, the module can check the List and spell, their Sorcery flag, RMU type, level, current CP, racial bonus, and Empathy. Objective cases will be calculated automatically; for ordinary spells, the GM retains the decision regarding intent.</p><p>Corruption Points should be stored on the Actor along with a history of gains and losses. A campaign option may choose between an RR against Essence and an RR against the spell's Realm.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>The way a character chooses to exercise power ultimately acts upon the one who exercises it. Corruption is not a punishment for all magic; it represents growing proximity to Morgoth's fundamental logic: domination, control, and alteration of the natural order.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"J9yN8vqcgr8vSTHQ"}
{"name":"Nature of Magic","sort":10000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"nature-magie","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"nature-magie","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Nature de la magie","pages":[{"name":"Nature de la magie","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Nature de la magie</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>La magie en Terre du Milieu procède du <strong>fairë</strong>, la radiance spirituelle dont la source ultime est <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. Elle est façonnée par la volonté et appartient à la structure même d’Arda.</p><ul><li><strong>Essence</strong> : accès au Nárë Ufirima, la Flamme Impérissable.</li><li><strong>Canalisation</strong> : puissance investie dans Arda par les Valar et les Maiar.</li><li><strong>Mentalisme</strong> : fairë produit directement par la fëa du lanceur.</li></ul><p>La magie devient progressivement plus rare et plus discrète au fil des Âges, particulièrement au Troisième Âge. RMU fournit la mécanique ; MERP-RMU en précise la place cosmologique, culturelle et historique.</p></section><h2>Une puissance intégrée à Arda</h2><p>La magie en Terre du Milieu ne constitue pas une force étrangère au monde ni un ensemble de techniques séparées de sa nature profonde. Elle procède directement de la structure d’Arda, de l’existence des esprits qui l’habitent et de la puissance première dont toute création tire finalement son origine.</p><p>Les Terres du Milieu possèdent en outre un lien particulier avec les Terres Immortelles, ce qui a rendu possible l’apparition et l’usage de puissances considérables. Cette puissance s’est manifestée de manière spectaculaire au Premier Âge, puis encore largement au Deuxième Âge. Au cours du Troisième Âge, son expression devient progressivement plus discrète. À la fin de cet Âge, les manifestations ouvertement extraordinaires sont principalement associées à des êtres d’une puissance exceptionnelle tels que les Dragons, le Balrog, Saruman ou Sauron.</p><h2>Une puissance de plus en plus discrète</h2><p>Le caractère discret de la magie au Troisième Âge ne signifie pas que les grandes puissances ont disparu. Les Valar ont progressivement renoncé à exercer une tutelle directe sur les Terres du Milieu, d’abord à la fin du Premier Âge, puis plus encore après la chute de Númenor. Ils continuent néanmoins à observer les événements et interviennent indirectement au Troisième Âge par l’envoi des Istari.</p><p>Les Istari possèdent eux-mêmes une puissance considérable, mais suivent une règle comparable à celle de leurs maîtres : ils évitent d’employer directement leur pouvoir sauf lorsque cela devient nécessaire dans la lutte contre les Ténèbres, et même alors seulement dans la mesure requise. Cette retenue explique qu’ils puissent paraître beaucoup moins puissants qu’ils ne le sont réellement.</p><h2>La perception de la magie</h2><p>Les différents peuples ne considèrent pas tous la magie de la même manière. Pour de nombreux Hommes, elle demeure mystérieuse et mal comprise. Beaucoup en connaissent surtout les récits, les légendes ou les manifestations suffisamment extraordinaires pour avoir été conservées dans la mémoire collective.</p><p>Les Hobbits en ont généralement une perception encore plus distante. La magie appartient facilement pour eux au domaine des anciennes histoires, même si des formes extrêmement modestes de pouvoir peuvent exister beaucoup plus près de leur quotidien qu’ils ne le pensent.</p><p>Les Elfes entretiennent un rapport très différent avec elle. Ils parlent de <strong>Nolwë</strong>, « Sagesse » ou « Savoir secret », et considèrent l’usage du pouvoir comme une composante normale de leur existence. Pour eux, la frontière entre une œuvre merveilleuse, une connaissance profondément maîtrisée et ce qu’un Homme appellerait « magie » peut être beaucoup moins nette.</p><h2>Fairë</h2><p>Dans les règles MERP-RMU, la magie est définie comme <strong>l’usage de la volonté pour manipuler directement une énergie appelée fairë</strong>. Le terme quenya fairë peut être traduit par « radiance ». Cette énergie est produite par les <strong>fëar</strong>, les esprits individuels des êtres vivants ou incarnés.</p><p>La magie n’est donc pas seulement une énergie extérieure que le lanceur de sorts puise dans un environnement abstrait. Elle est liée aux esprits eux-mêmes et à leur capacité à exercer leur volonté sur le monde. Les trois Royaumes de magie utilisés par RMU — Essence, Canalisation et Mentalisme — correspondent à différentes sources ou différents niveaux de fairë.</p><h2>Fëa et hröa</h2><p>La compréhension de la magie repose notamment sur la distinction entre <strong>fëa</strong> et <strong>hröa</strong>. La fëa est l’esprit de l’individu. Le hröa est son corps incarné.</p><p>La puissance magique trouve son origine ou son intermédiaire dans le fairë, mais son utilisation reste influencée par la condition incarnée du personnage. Cette relation est particulièrement importante pour le Mentalisme, dont la puissance provient directement de la fëa du lanceur mais doit néanmoins agir à travers les limites de son corps, de ses sens et de ses perceptions.</p><h2>Eru Ilúvatar</h2><p>La source ultime du fairë est <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. Toute puissance dérive finalement de lui. Cependant, une fois amenés à l’existence, les êtres issus de sa création possèdent également leur propre fairë distinctif. Cela concerne aussi bien les Ainur que les peuples incarnés tels que les Elfes, les Hommes ou les Khazâd.</p><p>La puissance magique n’est donc pas distribuée uniformément. Chaque être possède une nature propre, une puissance propre et une relation particulière à la structure d’Arda. Cette différence contribue à expliquer pourquoi certaines Races ou certains individus possèdent une aptitude beaucoup plus importante que d’autres à percevoir, comprendre ou manipuler la magie.</p><h2>Le Nárë Ufirima</h2><p>Le Royaume de l’<strong>Essence</strong> tire sa puissance du <strong>Nárë Ufirima</strong>, la Flamme Impérissable. Cette Flamme constitue le fairë primordial placé par Eru au cœur d’Eä.</p><p>L’utilisateur de l’Essence entre en relation avec cette puissance, la façonne et la détourne momentanément vers le sort qu’il cherche à produire. Cette origine explique pourquoi les manifestations les plus puissantes de l’Essence concernent souvent les grandes forces élémentaires : feu, terre, eau, vent, lumière et froid.</p><h2>La puissance des Valar et des Maiar</h2><p>Le Royaume de la <strong>Canalisation</strong> tire sa puissance de l’action créatrice des Valar et Maiar et du fairë qu’ils ont insufflé dans la substance même d’Arda. L’utilisateur de Canalisation entre en relation avec une puissance déjà inscrite dans le monde par l’action des Ainur.</p><p>Cette distinction permet de représenter les pouvoirs liés à la guérison, aux protections, aux créations, aux bénédictions ou à certaines formes d’autorité surnaturelle sans les réduire à une autre forme de manipulation élémentaire.</p><h2>La puissance intérieure</h2><p>Le Royaume du <strong>Mentalisme</strong> repose sur le fairë produit par la propre fëa du lanceur. Le Mentaliste emploie donc directement sa propre puissance spirituelle pour infléchir la réalité qui l’entoure.</p><p>Cette magie est naturellement plus intérieure que l’Essence ou la Canalisation. Le lanceur doit maintenir la puissance en lui-même et reste soumis aux limites de son corps, de ses sens et de sa perception. Ses effets s’étendent donc rarement très loin et sont fréquemment limités au lanceur ou à une cible unique.</p><h2>Une seule réalité magique</h2><p>Les trois Royaumes ne représentent pas trois magies totalement séparées. Ils décrivent trois manières différentes d’accéder au fairë et de l’utiliser. L’Essence mobilise la puissance primordiale associée à la Flamme Impérissable ; la Canalisation utilise la puissance inscrite dans Arda par l’action des Valar et des Maiar ; le Mentalisme emploie directement le fairë propre à la fëa du lanceur.</p><h2>Magie et volonté</h2><p>L’emploi de la magie exige toujours une volonté capable de donner une forme à la puissance. Le sort n’est pas uniquement une formule qu’il suffirait de réciter. Les paroles, les gestes, les Listes de Sorts et les techniques apprises permettent au personnage de canaliser correctement sa volonté, mais celle-ci reste le principe actif du processus.</p><h2>Savoir et magie</h2><p>Le rapport entre savoir et puissance est particulièrement important en Terre du Milieu. Chez les Elfes, le mot Nolwë associe directement magie, sagesse et savoir secret.</p><p>Une œuvre peut sembler magique parce que son créateur possède une compréhension de la matière ou de la nature dépassant largement celle de celui qui l’observe. Un objet peut conserver une puissance parce que son artisan savait comment inscrire sa volonté dans sa fabrication. Une chanson peut produire un effet que d’autres peuples qualifieraient de sort. La frontière entre compétence exceptionnelle, connaissance ancienne et magie n’est donc pas toujours évidente.</p><h2>Une magie intégrée au monde</h2><p>La magie en Terre du Milieu ne doit pas être considérée comme une couche ajoutée au monde ordinaire. Elle fait partie de sa nature. Une lumière inhabituelle, une voix portant au-delà de ce qui semble possible, une lame conservant une qualité extraordinaire pendant des siècles ou une œuvre artisanale dépassant toutes les capacités connues peuvent être des manifestations de la même réalité fondamentale.</p><h2>Déclin au fil des Âges</h2><p>La connaissance et l’utilisation de la magie diminuent progressivement au cours du Troisième Âge. Cette évolution provient en partie de changements subtils dans les lois naturelles et en partie de la politique délibérée du Seigneur des Ténèbres, qui cherche à corrompre, entraver ou soumettre les utilisateurs de pouvoir qui échappent à son contrôle.</p><p>Le déclin ne signifie pas que la nature fondamentale de la magie change. Le fairë demeure. Ce sont l’accès au savoir, la capacité à l’utiliser et la présence d’individus suffisamment puissants ou instruits qui deviennent plus rares.</p><h2>Une puissance qui attire l’attention</h2><p>L’usage de la magie n’est pas neutre au Troisième Âge. Les manifestations de puissance peuvent attirer l’attention de ceux qui savent les reconnaître, notamment les serviteurs de Sauron. Cette menace contribue elle aussi à expliquer la prudence des utilisateurs de magie.</p><h2>Magie et corruption</h2><p>La structure même d’Arda porte les conséquences de l’action de Morgoth. Certaines utilisations de la magie, notamment celles qui cherchent à dominer, corrompre ou détruire l’ordre naturel, mettent directement le lanceur en contact avec cette influence.</p><p>La magie n’est pas intrinsèquement corruptrice. C’est la source particulière de certains pouvoirs et surtout la manière dont ils sont employés qui peuvent exposer le personnage à la corruption.</p><h2>Application à RMU</h2><p>Pour MERP-RMU, les règles fondamentales de Rolemaster Unified restent le moteur mécanique de la magie. Les Professions déterminent les coûts de développement et l’accès aux Listes. Les Listes de Sorts définissent les pouvoirs disponibles. Les Compétences de Développement du Pouvoir, Sort dirigé, Runes, Harmonisation et autres capacités RMU continuent à fonctionner normalement.</p><p><strong>RMU détermine comment la magie fonctionne mécaniquement ; MERP-RMU détermine comment cette magie existe et s’inscrit dans la Terre du Milieu.</strong></p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Nature of Magic","pages":[{"name":"Nature of Magic","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Nature of Magic</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth proceeds from <strong>fairë</strong>, the spiritual radiance whose ultimate source is <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. It is shaped by will and belongs to the very structure of Arda.</p><ul><li><strong>Essence</strong>: access to the Nárë Ufirima, the Imperishable Flame.</li><li><strong>Channeling</strong>: power invested in Arda by the Valar and the Maiar.</li><li><strong>Mentalism</strong>: fairë produced directly by the caster's fëa.</li></ul><p>Magic becomes progressively rarer and more discreet as the Ages pass, particularly in the Third Age. RMU provides the mechanics; MERP-RMU defines its cosmological, cultural, and historical place.</p></section><h2>A Power Woven into Arda</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth is not a force foreign to the world, nor a set of techniques separate from its deepest nature. It proceeds directly from the structure of Arda, from the existence of the spirits that dwell within it, and from the primal power from which all creation ultimately derives.</p><p>Middle-earth also possesses a particular connection with the Undying Lands, which made the emergence and use of considerable powers possible. That power manifested spectacularly in the First Age and remained widespread in the Second Age. During the Third Age, its expression gradually becomes more discreet. By the end of that Age, openly extraordinary manifestations are chiefly associated with beings of exceptional power such as Dragons, the Balrog, Saruman, or Sauron.</p><h2>An Increasingly Discreet Power</h2><p>The discreet nature of magic in the Third Age does not mean that the great powers have disappeared. The Valar gradually renounced direct guardianship over Middle-earth, first at the end of the First Age and still more after the Downfall of Númenor. They nevertheless continue to observe events and intervene indirectly in the Third Age through the sending of the Istari.</p><p>The Istari themselves possess considerable power, but follow a principle similar to that of their masters: they avoid using their power directly unless it becomes necessary in the struggle against the Darkness, and even then only to the extent required. This restraint explains why they can appear far less powerful than they truly are.</p><h2>The Perception of Magic</h2><p>The different peoples do not all regard magic in the same way. For many Men, it remains mysterious and poorly understood. Most know it chiefly through stories, legends, or manifestations extraordinary enough to have been preserved in collective memory.</p><p>Hobbits generally have an even more distant perception of it. Magic readily belongs, for them, to the realm of old tales, even though extremely modest forms of power may exist much closer to their daily lives than they realise.</p><p>Elves have a very different relationship with it. They speak of <strong>Nolwë</strong>, “Wisdom” or “Secret Knowledge,” and regard the use of power as a normal part of their existence. For them, the boundary between a marvellous work, knowledge mastered to extraordinary depth, and what a Man would call “magic” can be far less distinct.</p><h2>Fairë</h2><p>In MERP-RMU, magic is defined as <strong>the use of will to manipulate directly an energy called fairë</strong>. The Quenya term fairë may be translated as “radiance.” This energy is produced by <strong>fëar</strong>, the individual spirits of living or incarnate beings.</p><p>Magic is therefore not merely an external energy that a spell-user draws from some abstract environment. It is tied to spirits themselves and to their capacity to exert their will upon the world. The three Realms of magic used by RMU — Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism — correspond to different sources or different levels of fairë.</p><h2>Fëa and Hröa</h2><p>Understanding magic depends in particular on the distinction between <strong>fëa</strong> and <strong>hröa</strong>. The fëa is the individual's spirit. The hröa is the incarnate body.</p><p>Magical power finds its source or intermediary in fairë, but its use remains influenced by the character's incarnate condition. This relationship is especially important for Mentalism, whose power comes directly from the caster's fëa but must nevertheless act through the limitations of the body, senses, and perceptions.</p><h2>Eru Ilúvatar</h2><p>The ultimate source of fairë is <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. All power ultimately derives from him. Once brought into existence, however, the beings of his creation also possess their own distinctive fairë. This is true both of the Ainur and of incarnate peoples such as Elves, Men, or the Khazâd.</p><p>Magical power is therefore not distributed uniformly. Every being possesses its own nature, its own power, and a particular relationship with the structure of Arda. This difference helps explain why certain Races or individuals have a much greater aptitude than others for perceiving, understanding, or manipulating magic.</p><h2>The Nárë Ufirima</h2><p>The Realm of <strong>Essence</strong> draws its power from the <strong>Nárë Ufirima</strong>, the Imperishable Flame. This Flame is the primordial fairë placed by Eru at the heart of Eä.</p><p>The Essence user enters into relationship with this power, shapes it, and momentarily diverts it toward the spell being produced. This origin explains why the most powerful manifestations of Essence often concern the great elemental forces: fire, earth, water, wind, light, and cold.</p><h2>The Power of the Valar and Maiar</h2><p>The Realm of <strong>Channeling</strong> draws its power from the creative action of the Valar and Maiar and from the fairë they infused into the very substance of Arda. A Channeling user enters into relationship with power already inscribed in the world through the action of the Ainur.</p><p>This distinction makes it possible to represent powers connected with healing, protection, creation, blessings, or certain forms of supernatural authority without reducing them to another form of elemental manipulation.</p><h2>Inner Power</h2><p>The Realm of <strong>Mentalism</strong> is based on the fairë produced by the caster's own fëa. The Mentalist therefore uses personal spiritual power directly to bend the reality around them.</p><p>This magic is naturally more inward than Essence or Channeling. The caster must hold the power within and remains subject to the limitations of body, senses, and perception. Its effects therefore rarely extend very far and are frequently limited to the caster or to a single target.</p><h2>A Single Magical Reality</h2><p>The three Realms do not represent three completely separate magics. They describe three different ways of accessing fairë and using it. Essence mobilises the primordial power associated with the Imperishable Flame; Channeling uses the power inscribed in Arda through the action of the Valar and Maiar; Mentalism uses directly the fairë belonging to the caster's own fëa.</p><h2>Magic and Will</h2><p>The use of magic always requires a will capable of giving form to power. A spell is not merely a formula that need only be recited. Words, gestures, Spell Lists, and learned techniques allow the character to channel will correctly, but that will remains the active principle of the process.</p><h2>Knowledge and Magic</h2><p>The relationship between knowledge and power is especially important in Middle-earth. Among Elves, the word Nolwë directly associates magic, wisdom, and secret knowledge.</p><p>A work may appear magical because its creator possesses an understanding of matter or nature far beyond that of the observer. An object may retain power because its maker knew how to inscribe will into its creation. A song may produce an effect that other peoples would call a spell. The boundary between exceptional skill, ancient knowledge, and magic is therefore not always obvious.</p><h2>Magic as Part of the World</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth should not be regarded as a layer added onto the ordinary world. It is part of the world's nature. An unusual light, a voice carrying farther than seems possible, a blade retaining extraordinary quality for centuries, or a crafted work surpassing all known ability can all be manifestations of the same fundamental reality.</p><h2>Decline Through the Ages</h2><p>Knowledge and use of magic gradually diminish during the Third Age. This development results partly from subtle changes in the natural laws and partly from the deliberate policy of the Dark Lord, who seeks to corrupt, hinder, or subjugate users of power who remain outside his control.</p><p>This decline does not mean that the fundamental nature of magic changes. Fairë remains. What becomes rarer is access to knowledge, the ability to use it, and the presence of individuals powerful or learned enough to do so.</p><h2>A Power That Draws Attention</h2><p>The use of magic is not neutral in the Third Age. Manifestations of power can draw the attention of those able to recognise them, especially the servants of Sauron. This threat also helps explain the caution of magic-users.</p><h2>Magic and Corruption</h2><p>The very structure of Arda bears the consequences of Morgoth's action. Certain uses of magic, especially those that seek to dominate, corrupt, or destroy the natural order, bring the caster directly into contact with this influence.</p><p>Magic is not inherently corrupting. It is the particular source of certain powers, and above all the manner in which they are used, that can expose a character to Corruption.</p><h2>Application to RMU</h2><p>For MERP-RMU, the fundamental rules of Rolemaster Unified remain the mechanical engine of magic. Professions determine development costs and access to Lists. Spell Lists define the powers available. Power Development, Directed Spells, Runes, Attunement, and other RMU Skills continue to function normally.</p><p><strong>RMU determines how magic works mechanically; MERP-RMU determines how that magic exists and fits within Middle-earth.</strong></p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Nature of Magic","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Nature of Magic</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth proceeds from <strong>fairë</strong>, the spiritual radiance whose ultimate source is <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. It is shaped by will and belongs to the very structure of Arda.</p><ul><li><strong>Essence</strong>: access to the Nárë Ufirima, the Imperishable Flame.</li><li><strong>Channeling</strong>: power invested in Arda by the Valar and the Maiar.</li><li><strong>Mentalism</strong>: fairë produced directly by the caster's fëa.</li></ul><p>Magic becomes progressively rarer and more discreet as the Ages pass, particularly in the Third Age. RMU provides the mechanics; MERP-RMU defines its cosmological, cultural, and historical place.</p></section><h2>A Power Woven into Arda</h2><p>Magic in Middle-earth is not a force foreign to the world, nor a set of techniques separate from its deepest nature. It proceeds directly from the structure of Arda, from the existence of the spirits that dwell within it, and from the primal power from which all creation ultimately derives.</p><p>Middle-earth also possesses a particular connection with the Undying Lands, which made the emergence and use of considerable powers possible. That power manifested spectacularly in the First Age and remained widespread in the Second Age. During the Third Age, its expression gradually becomes more discreet. By the end of that Age, openly extraordinary manifestations are chiefly associated with beings of exceptional power such as Dragons, the Balrog, Saruman, or Sauron.</p><h2>An Increasingly Discreet Power</h2><p>The discreet nature of magic in the Third Age does not mean that the great powers have disappeared. The Valar gradually renounced direct guardianship over Middle-earth, first at the end of the First Age and still more after the Downfall of Númenor. They nevertheless continue to observe events and intervene indirectly in the Third Age through the sending of the Istari.</p><p>The Istari themselves possess considerable power, but follow a principle similar to that of their masters: they avoid using their power directly unless it becomes necessary in the struggle against the Darkness, and even then only to the extent required. This restraint explains why they can appear far less powerful than they truly are.</p><h2>The Perception of Magic</h2><p>The different peoples do not all regard magic in the same way. For many Men, it remains mysterious and poorly understood. Most know it chiefly through stories, legends, or manifestations extraordinary enough to have been preserved in collective memory.</p><p>Hobbits generally have an even more distant perception of it. Magic readily belongs, for them, to the realm of old tales, even though extremely modest forms of power may exist much closer to their daily lives than they realise.</p><p>Elves have a very different relationship with it. They speak of <strong>Nolwë</strong>, “Wisdom” or “Secret Knowledge,” and regard the use of power as a normal part of their existence. For them, the boundary between a marvellous work, knowledge mastered to extraordinary depth, and what a Man would call “magic” can be far less distinct.</p><h2>Fairë</h2><p>In MERP-RMU, magic is defined as <strong>the use of will to manipulate directly an energy called fairë</strong>. The Quenya term fairë may be translated as “radiance.” This energy is produced by <strong>fëar</strong>, the individual spirits of living or incarnate beings.</p><p>Magic is therefore not merely an external energy that a spell-user draws from some abstract environment. It is tied to spirits themselves and to their capacity to exert their will upon the world. The three Realms of magic used by RMU — Essence, Channeling, and Mentalism — correspond to different sources or different levels of fairë.</p><h2>Fëa and Hröa</h2><p>Understanding magic depends in particular on the distinction between <strong>fëa</strong> and <strong>hröa</strong>. The fëa is the individual's spirit. The hröa is the incarnate body.</p><p>Magical power finds its source or intermediary in fairë, but its use remains influenced by the character's incarnate condition. This relationship is especially important for Mentalism, whose power comes directly from the caster's fëa but must nevertheless act through the limitations of the body, senses, and perceptions.</p><h2>Eru Ilúvatar</h2><p>The ultimate source of fairë is <strong>Eru Ilúvatar</strong>. All power ultimately derives from him. Once brought into existence, however, the beings of his creation also possess their own distinctive fairë. This is true both of the Ainur and of incarnate peoples such as Elves, Men, or the Khazâd.</p><p>Magical power is therefore not distributed uniformly. Every being possesses its own nature, its own power, and a particular relationship with the structure of Arda. This difference helps explain why certain Races or individuals have a much greater aptitude than others for perceiving, understanding, or manipulating magic.</p><h2>The Nárë Ufirima</h2><p>The Realm of <strong>Essence</strong> draws its power from the <strong>Nárë Ufirima</strong>, the Imperishable Flame. This Flame is the primordial fairë placed by Eru at the heart of Eä.</p><p>The Essence user enters into relationship with this power, shapes it, and moment