actical rule concerns the metal protections explicitly described.</p><h3>Channeling and Effects</h3><p>Channeling covers areas such as purification, creation, protection, healing, or spiritual defence. It can nevertheless also take destructive forms: the Realm describes a source of power, not a morality.</p><h2>Mentalism</h2><p>The Realm of Mentalism draws its power directly from the fairë produced by the caster's fëa. The Mentalist therefore uses personal spiritual power to alter the surrounding reality.</p><h3>Inner Power</h3><p>This origin makes Mentalism especially dependent on the individual. Power must be held within the caster before being directed toward its effect. The Mentalist works through the limitations of incarnate being; senses, perception, and body all play a direct role.</p><h3>The Hröa</h3><p>The hröa interferes with the use of Mentalism. The source does not, however, provide a list of armour prohibitions comparable to those for Essence or Channeling. An additional equipment restriction should therefore not be invented where none is explicitly given.</p><h3>Senses, Perception, and Range</h3><p>Mentalism users are rarely able to direct their spells beyond themselves or a single target. This rule provides a general orientation; the ranges and areas of effect actually written into RMU Lists remain the mechanical reference.</p><h3>Mentalism and the Eldar</h3><p>The Eldar possess a particularly natural relationship with Mentalism. Under certain conditions they may learn Mentalism Lists through self-teaching, practice, and experimentation. This exception is developed in the Learning Spell Lists Journal.</p><h2>Comparison of the Three Realms</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Realm</th><th>Immediate Source</th><th>MERP-RMU Constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Essence</td><td>Nárë Ufirima — Imperishable Flame</td><td>No armour, helmet, or greaves while casting or using spells.</td></tr><tr><td>Channeling</td><td>Fairë invested in Arda by the Valar and Maiar</td><td>No metal armour, helmet, or greaves while casting.</td></tr><tr><td>Mentalism</td><td>Fairë of the caster's fëa</td><td>Limits of the hröa, senses, and perception; no additional armour prohibition defined here.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Professions and Realms</h2><p>Pure Spell-user Professions exploit one Realm as their primary domain. Semi Spell-user Professions use one Realm while retaining substantial non-magical training. Hybrid Professions combine two Realms and will require specific verification of casting restrictions before automation.</p><h2>Realm and Morality</h2><p>None of the three Realms is inherently good or evil. Morality depends on the List, the spell, its origin, and the use made of it. Sorcery is therefore not a fourth Realm but a corrupting category that can appear within several Realms.</p><h2>Application in MERP-RMU</h2><p>Every magical Profession must display its Realm or Realms, and every List must retain its RMU <code>realms</code> field. Equipment limitations are documented immediately and will only be automated after RMU mechanisms have been verified, in order to avoid any double counting.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"5u3MAwUtSme1nN19"}
{"name":"Herbs & Substances — Traveller’s Guide","sort":100000,"pages":[{"name":"Herbs & Substances — Traveller’s Guide","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>The Book of Simples</h2>\n<p>For those who know how to look, the wild lands are a vast apothecary. A small leaf that is difficult to spot may stop a hemorrhage, a bitter root may break the effect of a poison, while a berry mistaken for another can sometimes kill a traveler. Healers, rangers, and herbalists therefore learn less to “search for an herb” than to read a landscape: cold, moisture, soil, altitude, and even the nearness of the sea all reveal which plants may grow there.</p>\n<h3>Reading the Land</h3><p>Every plant favors particular climates and environments. Some thrive in coniferous or mixed forests, others on freshwater banks, moors, high mountains, glaciers, warm sea coasts, volcanic ground, or underground. A plant can only be found in a suitable environment. Its entry gives that environment in plain language together with the actual difficulty of recognizing and gathering it.</p>\n<h3>Search Without Exhausting the Land</h3><p>In MERP-RMU, one hour spent searching for a plant calls for a <strong>Survival</strong> Maneuver appropriate to the biome. <strong>Herbalism</strong> may be used as a Complementary Skill: it represents the eye that distinguishes the useful leaf from a worthless cousin, knows the seasons, and knows where to look.</p><p>An area harvested within the last six months imposes <strong>−50</strong>. Each additional searcher provides <strong>+2</strong>, and every ten hours of unsuccessful searching grants <strong>+5</strong>. An <strong>Absolute Success</strong> finds 1–5 doses; a <strong>Success</strong>, one dose; a <strong>Near Success</strong> permits a new roll for the same hour at +20. Partial Success or Failure finds nothing; Absolute Failure or Fumble means no dose can be found in that area until the next day.</p>\n<h3>Gathering and Preparation</h3><p>A plant’s virtue often depends as much on preparation as on species. An <strong>infusion or decoction</strong> becomes effective after steeping in hot water; a substance meant for <strong>ingestion</strong> may be eaten, chewed, or drunk; an <strong>inhaled</strong> powder acts through the respiratory tract. An <strong>application</strong> generally requires 1–10 rounds of preparation before being placed on a wound. A <strong>paste</strong> may remain effective for up to a week on food, a tool, or a weapon; a comparable <strong>liquid</strong> usually remains active for only an hour. A <strong>powder</strong> is intended for food or drink and is not normally used on a weapon.</p>\n<h3>Doses, Poisons, and Addiction</h3><p>Unless stated otherwise, only one dose may produce its effect in a given round. Poisons must still overcome the victim’s resistance; each entry gives their nature, level, and consequences.</p><p>Some substances create dependency. Their <strong>Addiction Factor (AF)</strong> is a guide for the GM: after a dose, 1d100 + AF above 100 means the user develops an addiction. Repeated use may strengthen it, and withdrawal can become difficult.</p>\n<h3>Living Knowledge</h3><p>The catalogue descriptions translate old codes into travel information: “cold forest,” “temperate moor,” “warm sea coast,” “very difficult to find,” and so on. The original codes remain in MERP-UI data for future automation, but players do not need to decipher them to understand where and how to search.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Finding Herbs in the Wild","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Finding Herbs in the Wild</h2><p>Climate and terrain are always the first condition: searching for an alpine plant in a damp lowland will find nothing, regardless of the searcher’s skill. Once suitable ground is found, apply the plant’s own difficulty to the Survival Maneuver.</p><p><strong>One attempt represents one hour.</strong> Herbalism may support the search as a Complementary Skill. An area harvested recently imposes <strong>−50</strong>; each additional searcher grants <strong>+2</strong>; after ten unsuccessful hours, experience with the ground grants <strong>+5</strong> to later searches.</p><ul><li><strong>Absolute Success:</strong> 1–5 doses.</li><li><strong>Success:</strong> 1 dose.</li><li><strong>Near Success:</strong> make another roll for that hour at +20.</li><li><strong>Partial Success or Failure:</strong> no herb found.</li><li><strong>Absolute Failure or Fumble:</strong> that area yields no more of the plant until the next day.</li></ul></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Drinks & Provisions","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Drinks &amp; Provisions</h2><p>Travelers in Endor carry far more than ordinary rations. Some foods are designed to endure weeks on the road; others still preserve part of the lore of the Eldar or the Khazâd.</p>\n<h3>Alcohol</h3><p>Alcoholic drinks vary enormously by people and region. Addiction is normally tested only when a character actually becomes drunk; moderate consumption does not by itself require an addiction roll. Some drinks are nevertheless stronger or more habit-forming than others.</p>\n<h3>Cram</h3><p>This dense and highly nourishing Dwarven travel bread has a strong mushroom-and-pepper flavor. A slice of roughly four ounces counts as one dose and provides the equivalent of <strong>five days of food</strong>. A properly stored loaf remains edible for about seven weeks.</p>\n<h3>Lembas (Coimas)</h3><p>Lembas is the enchanted waybread of the Eldar, whose making is never sold as a commercial secret. One ration feeds one person for a day and strengthens the body against fatigue, poison, and disease. Elves give it to their own when long travel through the wild or grave peril requires it; a mortal receives it only if truly a friend of the Elves and in exceptional need.</p>\n<h3>Miruvor</h3><p>This Elven cordial is prepared in Noldorin lands and is not a trade good. A dose deeply revives the drinker, supports Constitution, and temporarily strengthens resistance to disease and poison.</p>\n<h3>Pandis</h3><p>This liquor is brewed in the marshes of the Marais and Midgewater from the bark of [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Drunk in quantity, it repels insects and greatly strengthens resistance to snake bites. Abuse causes a particularly unpleasant hangover, often with greenish discoloration around the eyes and mouth. As with other alcohol, addiction checks begin only once drunkenness is reached.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Medicinal Preparations","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Medicinal Preparations</h2><p>Herbs are not always used exactly as gathered. Healers and apothecaries turn them into salves, powders, tinctures, and compounds whose preparation may concentrate or alter their virtues.</p>\n<h3>Alambas Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-alambas-spike|Alambas]], this salve treats up to four square feet of burns, whatever their severity.</p>\n<h3>Aloe Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-aloe-heat-heal|Aloe]], it doubles the natural healing rate of minor burns and cuts and also heals 5 hits caused by burns. It is common in north-western Endor.</p>\n<h3>Caranan</h3><p>This blend of several common ingredients from western, north-western, and central Endor heals 2–8 hits and relieves pain and swelling. No more than ten doses should be taken in a day.</p>\n<h3>Derrly Salve</h3><p>This lotion is made from [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Spread on the skin, it repels insects; they must overcome resistance equivalent to a 7th-level effect to approach within ten feet.</p>\n<h3>Gilder Dust (Ilyalma)</h3><p>A magical mixture of rare substances, this powder repairs injuries to organs, arteries, and veins in 1–3 minutes when sprinkled over the wound. It is normally available only from Elven healers and a few Mannish apothecaries or alchemists. It is also known as Astannûn, Númenasto, and Panaglu.</p>\n<h3>Golden Cress Salve</h3><p>[[HERB:herb-golden-cress-gold-chute|Golden Cress]] is dried, ground, and mixed with tallow. The salve closes and heals bleeding flesh wounds, restoring 1–5 hits in 1–5 rounds.</p>\n<h3>Harlindar</h3><p>This powder, mixed with water and drunk, is used to prevent miscarriage. It is made from herbs of southern Eriador.</p>\n<h3>Joef</h3><p>This inhaled powder allows the user to send a mental call to a conscious friend known to them. Range is one hundred feet multiplied by the user’s level. Its Addiction Factor is particularly high.</p>\n<h3>Maiana</h3><p>This decongestant liquid, prepared from plants of the northern slopes of the Misty Mountains, halves the dangerous symptoms caused by pulmonary complaints and respiratory disease.</p>\n<h3>Melem Loza Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-melem-loza-balm-vine|Melem Loza]], this salve doubles the effect of healing magic applied to the same wound or ailment.</p>\n<h3>Rune Salve</h3><p>Also called Arkasu, All-heal, or Mrim, this salve heals 2–12 hits, speeds the closing and healing of large open wounds by about half, acts as an antiseptic, and halves pain penalties from minor injuries. It is prepared from several herbs of the plains of Rhûn.</p>\n<h3>Sindiluin Tincture</h3><p>A secret process turns Sindiluin into a styptic tincture capable of stopping any bleeding. Healers who know the preparation seldom reveal its details.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Diseases","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Diseases</h2><p>Diseases in Endor may come from parasites, animals, plants, or invisible agents. Their level is used as a reference for Resistance Rolls, and their severity is not always proportional to how harmless they appear.</p>\n<h3>Angurth — level 2</h3><p>Spread by fleas, this affliction causes a slow and painful death if not resisted.</p>\n<h3>Bukandas — level 4</h3><p>Contracted from certain wolves, it causes severe asthma.</p>\n<h3>Blue Hand (E Luincam) — level 4</h3><p>Spread by the bite of the Death Shrew, this disease is not contagious. A minor failure on the Resistance Roll turns the skin blue, imposes <strong>−20</strong> to Appearance, and may temporarily slow activity. A more severe failure adds outbreaks of large boils that can greatly reduce Appearance and Presence bonus, reduce available hits, and cause bleeding from the nose and ears, with doubled bleeding rate.</p>\n<h3>Grelnixar — level 8</h3><p>Associated with the Vrel plant, this “running death” compels the victim to run whenever able, eventually causing exhaustion and possibly death.</p>\n<h3>Gurth-nu-Fuin — level 3</h3><p>This viral infection causes a slow and painful death.</p>\n<h3>Igturfas — level 5</h3><p>Spread by certain snakes, it causes severe intellectual impairment and reduces Reasoning by 20 points while the disease persists.</p>\n<h3>Jadaras — level 2</h3><p>Associated with the Janar herb, it reduces Agility by 5 points.</p>\n<h3>Shutinis — level 3</h3><p>Spread by Hultif insects, it causes madness.</p>\n<h3>Vemaak — level 7</h3><p>Spread by certain hornets, it causes loss of hearing.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"rules-herbs","collection":"merp-rmu-herb-rules","contentVersion":2,"compendiumSourceKey":"rules-herbs","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/herbs-v1.json","compendiumFolderKey":"Règles MERP - RMU","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Herbes et Substances, guide des simples","pages":[{"name":"Herbes et Substances, guide des simples","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Le Livre des simples</h2>\n<p>Pour qui sait regarder, les terres sauvages sont une vaste apothicairerie. Une petite feuille difficile à repérer peut arrêter une hémorragie, une racine amère briser l’effet d’un poison, tandis qu’une baie mal reconnue suffit parfois à tuer un voyageur. Les guérisseurs, rôdeurs et herboristes apprennent donc moins à « chercher une herbe » qu’à lire un paysage : le froid, l’humidité, la nature du sol, l’altitude et même la proximité de la mer disent quelles plantes ont une chance d’y pousser.</p>\n<h3>Lire le pays</h3><p>Chaque plante possède un climat et un milieu de prédilection. Certaines aiment les forêts de conifères ou les bois mêlés, d’autres les rives d’eau douce, les landes, les hautes montagnes, les glaciers, les marais côtiers, les terres volcaniques ou les profondeurs souterraines. Une plante ne peut être trouvée que dans un environnement qui lui convient. Sa fiche indique désormais ce milieu en toutes lettres, ainsi que la difficulté réelle de la reconnaître et de la récolter.</p>\n<h3>Chercher sans épuiser la terre</h3><p>Dans MERP-RMU, une heure passée à chercher une plante demande une Manœuvre de <strong>Survie</strong> adaptée au biome. <strong>Herboristerie</strong> peut être utilisée comme Compétence Complémentaire : elle représente l’œil qui distingue la bonne feuille de sa cousine inutile, connaît les saisons et sait où regarder.</p><p>Une zone récoltée au cours des six derniers mois impose <strong>−50</strong>. Chaque chercheur supplémentaire apporte <strong>+2</strong>, et chaque tranche de dix heures de recherche infructueuse accorde <strong>+5</strong>. Une <strong>Réussite Absolue</strong> permet de trouver 1 à 5 doses ; une <strong>Réussite</strong>, une dose ; une <strong>Réussite Proche</strong> autorise un nouveau jet pour la même heure à +20. Une Réussite Partielle ou un Échec ne donne rien ; un Échec Absolu ou une Bévue signifie qu’aucune dose ne sera trouvée dans cette zone avant le lendemain.</p>\n<h3>Récolter et préparer</h3><p>La vertu d’une plante dépend souvent autant de sa préparation que de son espèce. Une <strong>infusion ou décoction</strong> devient efficace après passage dans l’eau chaude ; une plante à <strong>ingérer</strong> peut être mangée, mâchée ou bue ; une poudre à <strong>inhaler</strong> agit par les voies respiratoires. Une <strong>application</strong> demande généralement 1 à 10 rounds de préparation avant d’être posée sur la blessure. Une <strong>pâte</strong> peut conserver son efficacité jusqu’à une semaine sur un aliment, un outil ou une arme ; un <strong>liquide</strong> comparable ne reste généralement actif qu’une heure. Une <strong>poudre</strong> est destinée à la nourriture ou à la boisson et ne s’emploie normalement pas sur une arme.</p>\n<h3>Doses, poisons et accoutumance</h3><p>Sauf indication contraire, une seule dose peut produire son effet au cours d’un même round. Les poisons doivent encore vaincre la résistance de leur victime : leur fiche indique leur nature, leur niveau et leurs conséquences.</p><p>Certaines substances créent une dépendance. Leur <strong>Facteur d’Accoutumance (FA)</strong> sert de repère au MJ : après une prise, 1d100 + FA au-delà de 100 signifie que l’utilisateur développe une dépendance. Les prises répétées peuvent renforcer cette dépendance et le manque peut entraîner un sevrage difficile.</p>\n<h3>Une connaissance vivante</h3><p>Les descriptions du catalogue traduisent les anciens codes en indications de voyage : « forêt froide », « lande tempérée », « rivage marin chaud », « plante très difficile à trouver », etc. Les codes d’origine restent conservés dans les données MERP-UI pour les automatisations futures, mais un joueur n’a pas besoin de les déchiffrer pour comprendre où et comment chercher.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Trouver des herbes dans la nature","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Trouver des herbes dans la nature</h2><p>Le climat et le terrain sont toujours la première condition : chercher une plante alpine dans une plaine humide ne donnera rien, quelle que soit la qualité du chercheur. Une fois le lieu approprié trouvé, la difficulté propre à la plante s’applique à la Manœuvre de Survie.</p><p><strong>Une tentative représente une heure.</strong> Herboristerie peut soutenir la recherche comme Compétence Complémentaire. Une zone déjà récoltée récemment impose une pénalité de <strong>−50</strong> ; chaque chercheur supplémentaire accorde un bonus de <strong>+2</strong> ; après dix heures infructueuses, la patience et l’expérience du terrain accordent un bonus de <strong>+5</strong> aux recherches suivantes.</p><ul><li><strong>Réussite Absolue :</strong> 1 à 5 doses.</li><li><strong>Réussite :</strong> 1 dose.</li><li><strong>Réussite Proche :</strong> nouveau jet pour cette heure à +20.</li><li><strong>Réussite Partielle ou Échec :</strong> aucune plante trouvée.</li><li><strong>Échec Absolu ou Bévue :</strong> la zone ne livrera plus cette plante avant le lendemain.</li></ul></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Boissons & Vivres","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Boissons & Vivres</h2><p>Les voyageurs d’Endor emportent bien plus que des rations ordinaires. Certains aliments sont conçus pour supporter des semaines de route ; d’autres portent encore une part du savoir des Eldar ou des Khazâd.</p>\n<h3>Alcool</h3><p>Les alcools varient énormément selon le peuple et la région. L’accoutumance n’est normalement testée que lorsqu’un personnage atteint réellement l’ivresse ; une consommation modérée n’entraîne pas à elle seule de jet de dépendance. Certaines boissons sont toutefois plus fortes ou plus addictives que d’autres.</p>\n<h3>Cram</h3><p>Ce pain de voyage nain, dense et très nourrissant, a un goût marqué de champignon et de poivre. Une tranche d’environ quatre onces constitue une dose et fournit l’équivalent de <strong>cinq jours de nourriture</strong>. Un pain correctement conservé reste consommable pendant environ sept semaines.</p>\n<h3>Lembas (Coimas)</h3><p>Le lembas est le pain de route enchanté des Eldar, dont le secret de fabrication n’est pas vendu. Une ration nourrit une personne pendant une journée et fortifie son corps contre la fatigue, les poisons et les maladies. Les Elfes le donnent aux leurs lorsqu’un long voyage dans les terres sauvages ou un péril grave l’exige ; un mortel ne peut en recevoir que s’il est véritablement ami des Elfes et dans un besoin exceptionnel.</p>\n<h3>Miruvor</h3><p>Ce cordial elfique est préparé dans les terres noldorines et n’est pas une marchandise. Une dose revigore profondément celui qui la boit, soutient sa Constitution et renforce temporairement sa résistance aux maladies et aux poisons.</p>\n<h3>Pandis</h3><p>Cette liqueur est brassée dans les marais du Marais et de Midgewater à partir de l’écorce de [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Bue en quantité, elle éloigne les insectes et renforce fortement la résistance aux morsures de serpent. L’abus laisse une gueule de bois particulièrement désagréable, souvent accompagnée de teintes verdâtres autour des yeux et de la bouche. Comme pour les autres alcools, les jets d’accoutumance ne commencent qu’à partir de l’ivresse.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Préparations médicinales","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Préparations médicinales</h2><p>Les herbes ne sont pas toujours employées telles qu’elles sont cueillies. Guérisseurs et apothicaires les transforment en baumes, poudres, teintures et mélanges dont la préparation peut concentrer ou modifier les vertus.</p>\n<h3>Baume d’Alambas</h3><p>Préparé à partir d’[[HERB:herb-alambas-spike|Alambas]], ce baume traite jusqu’à quatre pieds carrés de brûlures, quelle que soit leur gravité.</p>\n<h3>Baume d’Aloès</h3><p>Préparé à partir d’[[HERB:herb-aloe-heat-heal|Aloès]], il double la vitesse naturelle de guérison des brûlures et coupures mineures et soigne également 5 points de dégâts provoqués par les brûlures. Il est commun dans le nord-ouest d’Endor.</p>\n<h3>Caranan</h3><p>Ce mélange de plusieurs ingrédients communs de l’ouest, du nord-ouest et du centre d’Endor soigne 2 à 8 points de dégâts et soulage douleur et gonflement. Il ne faut pas dépasser dix doses par jour.</p>\n<h3>Baume de Derrly</h3><p>Cette lotion est fabriquée à partir de [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Étendue sur la peau, elle repousse les insectes ; ceux-ci doivent vaincre une résistance équivalente à un effet de niveau 7 pour s’approcher à moins de dix pieds.</p>\n<h3>Poussière dorée (Gilder Dust, Ilyalma)</h3><p>Mélange magique de substances rares, cette poudre répare en 1 à 3 minutes les lésions des organes, artères et veines lorsqu’elle est répandue sur la blessure. Elle n’est généralement disponible qu’auprès de guérisseurs elfes et de quelques apothicaires ou alchimistes humains. Elle est aussi connue sous les noms d’Astannûn, Númenasto et Panaglu.</p>\n<h3>Onguent de Cresson Doré</h3><p>Le [[HERB:herb-golden-cress-gold-chute|Cresson Doré]] est séché, broyé puis mélangé à du suif. L’onguent referme et soigne les plaies de chair qui saignent, restaurant 1 à 5 points en 1 à 5 rounds.</p>\n<h3>Harlindar</h3><p>Cette poudre, mêlée à de l’eau puis bue, est utilisée pour prévenir les fausses couches. Elle est composée d’herbes du sud de l’Eriador.</p>\n<h3>Joef</h3><p>Cette poudre inhalée permet à l’utilisateur d’adresser mentalement un appel à un ami conscient qu’il connaît. La portée est de cent pieds multipliés par le niveau de l’utilisateur. Son Facteur d’Accoutumance est particulièrement élevé.</p>\n<h3>Maiana</h3><p>Ce liquide décongestionnant, préparé à partir de plantes des pentes septentrionales des Monts Brumeux, réduit de moitié les symptômes dangereux provoqués par les affections pulmonaires et les maladies respiratoires.</p>\n<h3>Baume de Melem Loza</h3><p>Préparé à partir de [[HERB:herb-melem-loza-balm-vine|Melem Loza]], ce baume double l’effet d’une magie de guérison appliquée à la même blessure ou affection.</p>\n<h3>Baume runique (Rune Salve)</h3><p>Aussi appelé Arkasu, All-heal ou Mrim, ce baume soigne 2 à 12 points, accélère d’environ moitié la fermeture et la guérison des grandes plaies ouvertes, agit comme antiseptique et atténue de moitié les pénalités de douleur causées par des blessures mineures. Il est préparé à partir de plusieurs herbes des plaines de Rhûn.</p>\n<h3>Teinture de Sindiluin</h3><p>Un procédé secret transforme le Sindiluin en une teinture styptique capable d’arrêter toute hémorragie. Les guérisseurs qui connaissent sa préparation en révèlent rarement les détails.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Maladies","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Maladies</h2><p>Les maladies d’Endor peuvent provenir d’un parasite, d’un animal, d’une plante ou d’un agent invisible. Leur niveau sert de référence pour les Jets de Résistance et leur gravité n’est pas toujours proportionnelle à leur apparente banalité.</p>\n<h3>Angurth — niveau 2</h3><p>Transmise par des puces, cette affection entraîne une mort lente et douloureuse si elle n’est pas combattue.</p>\n<h3>Bukandas — niveau 4</h3><p>Contractée auprès de certains loups, elle provoque un asthme sévère.</p>\n<h3>Main Bleue (Blue Hand, E Luincam) — niveau 4</h3><p>Transmise par la morsure de la Musaraigne de Mort, cette maladie n’est pas contagieuse. Un échec léger du Jet de Résistance bleuit la peau, impose une pénalité de <strong>−20</strong> à l’Apparence et peut provoquer un ralentissement temporaire de l’activité. Un échec plus grave ajoute des poussées de gros furoncles pouvant réduire fortement l’Apparence et le bonus de Présence, diminuer les points de vie disponibles et provoquer des saignements par le nez et les oreilles, avec un taux d’hémorragie doublé.</p>\n<h3>Grelnixar — niveau 8</h3><p>Associée à la plante Vrel, cette « mort en courant » pousse la victime à courir chaque fois qu’elle en est capable, jusqu’à l’épuisement et éventuellement la mort.</p>\n<h3>Gurth-nu-Fuin — niveau 3</h3><p>Cette infection virale entraîne une mort lente et douloureuse.</p>\n<h3>Igturfas — niveau 5</h3><p>Transmise par certains serpents, elle provoque un affaiblissement intellectuel sévère et réduit le Raisonnement de 20 points tant que l’affection persiste.</p>\n<h3>Jadaras — niveau 2</h3><p>Liée à l’herbe Janar, elle réduit l’Agilité de 5 points.</p>\n<h3>Shutinis — niveau 3</h3><p>Transmise par les insectes Hultif, elle provoque la folie.</p>\n<h3>Vemaak — niveau 7</h3><p>Transmise par certains frelons, elle entraîne une perte de l’audition.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Herbs & Substances — Traveller’s Guide","pages":[{"name":"Herbs & Substances — Traveller’s Guide","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>The Book of Simples</h2>\n<p>For those who know how to look, the wild lands are a vast apothecary. A small leaf that is difficult to spot may stop a hemorrhage, a bitter root may break the effect of a poison, while a berry mistaken for another can sometimes kill a traveler. Healers, rangers, and herbalists therefore learn less to “search for an herb” than to read a landscape: cold, moisture, soil, altitude, and even the nearness of the sea all reveal which plants may grow there.</p>\n<h3>Reading the Land</h3><p>Every plant favors particular climates and environments. Some thrive in coniferous or mixed forests, others on freshwater banks, moors, high mountains, glaciers, warm sea coasts, volcanic ground, or underground. A plant can only be found in a suitable environment. Its entry gives that environment in plain language together with the actual difficulty of recognizing and gathering it.</p>\n<h3>Search Without Exhausting the Land</h3><p>In MERP-RMU, one hour spent searching for a plant calls for a <strong>Survival</strong> Maneuver appropriate to the biome. <strong>Herbalism</strong> may be used as a Complementary Skill: it represents the eye that distinguishes the useful leaf from a worthless cousin, knows the seasons, and knows where to look.</p><p>An area harvested within the last six months imposes <strong>−50</strong>. Each additional searcher provides <strong>+2</strong>, and every ten hours of unsuccessful searching grants <strong>+5</strong>. An <strong>Absolute Success</strong> finds 1–5 doses; a <strong>Success</strong>, one dose; a <strong>Near Success</strong> permits a new roll for the same hour at +20. Partial Success or Failure finds nothing; Absolute Failure or Fumble means no dose can be found in that area until the next day.</p>\n<h3>Gathering and Preparation</h3><p>A plant’s virtue often depends as much on preparation as on species. An <strong>infusion or decoction</strong> becomes effective after steeping in hot water; a substance meant for <strong>ingestion</strong> may be eaten, chewed, or drunk; an <strong>inhaled</strong> powder acts through the respiratory tract. An <strong>application</strong> generally requires 1–10 rounds of preparation before being placed on a wound. A <strong>paste</strong> may remain effective for up to a week on food, a tool, or a weapon; a comparable <strong>liquid</strong> usually remains active for only an hour. A <strong>powder</strong> is intended for food or drink and is not normally used on a weapon.</p>\n<h3>Doses, Poisons, and Addiction</h3><p>Unless stated otherwise, only one dose may produce its effect in a given round. Poisons must still overcome the victim’s resistance; each entry gives their nature, level, and consequences.</p><p>Some substances create dependency. Their <strong>Addiction Factor (AF)</strong> is a guide for the GM: after a dose, 1d100 + AF above 100 means the user develops an addiction. Repeated use may strengthen it, and withdrawal can become difficult.</p>\n<h3>Living Knowledge</h3><p>The catalogue descriptions translate old codes into travel information: “cold forest,” “temperate moor,” “warm sea coast,” “very difficult to find,” and so on. The original codes remain in MERP-UI data for future automation, but players do not need to decipher them to understand where and how to search.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Finding Herbs in the Wild","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Finding Herbs in the Wild</h2><p>Climate and terrain are always the first condition: searching for an alpine plant in a damp lowland will find nothing, regardless of the searcher’s skill. Once suitable ground is found, apply the plant’s own difficulty to the Survival Maneuver.</p><p><strong>One attempt represents one hour.</strong> Herbalism may support the search as a Complementary Skill. An area harvested recently imposes <strong>−50</strong>; each additional searcher grants <strong>+2</strong>; after ten unsuccessful hours, experience with the ground grants <strong>+5</strong> to later searches.</p><ul><li><strong>Absolute Success:</strong> 1–5 doses.</li><li><strong>Success:</strong> 1 dose.</li><li><strong>Near Success:</strong> make another roll for that hour at +20.</li><li><strong>Partial Success or Failure:</strong> no herb found.</li><li><strong>Absolute Failure or Fumble:</strong> that area yields no more of the plant until the next day.</li></ul></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Drinks & Provisions","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Drinks &amp; Provisions</h2><p>Travelers in Endor carry far more than ordinary rations. Some foods are designed to endure weeks on the road; others still preserve part of the lore of the Eldar or the Khazâd.</p>\n<h3>Alcohol</h3><p>Alcoholic drinks vary enormously by people and region. Addiction is normally tested only when a character actually becomes drunk; moderate consumption does not by itself require an addiction roll. Some drinks are nevertheless stronger or more habit-forming than others.</p>\n<h3>Cram</h3><p>This dense and highly nourishing Dwarven travel bread has a strong mushroom-and-pepper flavor. A slice of roughly four ounces counts as one dose and provides the equivalent of <strong>five days of food</strong>. A properly stored loaf remains edible for about seven weeks.</p>\n<h3>Lembas (Coimas)</h3><p>Lembas is the enchanted waybread of the Eldar, whose making is never sold as a commercial secret. One ration feeds one person for a day and strengthens the body against fatigue, poison, and disease. Elves give it to their own when long travel through the wild or grave peril requires it; a mortal receives it only if truly a friend of the Elves and in exceptional need.</p>\n<h3>Miruvor</h3><p>This Elven cordial is prepared in Noldorin lands and is not a trade good. A dose deeply revives the drinker, supports Constitution, and temporarily strengthens resistance to disease and poison.</p>\n<h3>Pandis</h3><p>This liquor is brewed in the marshes of the Marais and Midgewater from the bark of [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Drunk in quantity, it repels insects and greatly strengthens resistance to snake bites. Abuse causes a particularly unpleasant hangover, often with greenish discoloration around the eyes and mouth. As with other alcohol, addiction checks begin only once drunkenness is reached.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Medicinal Preparations","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Medicinal Preparations</h2><p>Herbs are not always used exactly as gathered. Healers and apothecaries turn them into salves, powders, tinctures, and compounds whose preparation may concentrate or alter their virtues.</p>\n<h3>Alambas Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-alambas-spike|Alambas]], this salve treats up to four square feet of burns, whatever their severity.</p>\n<h3>Aloe Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-aloe-heat-heal|Aloe]], it doubles the natural healing rate of minor burns and cuts and also heals 5 hits caused by burns. It is common in north-western Endor.</p>\n<h3>Caranan</h3><p>This blend of several common ingredients from western, north-western, and central Endor heals 2–8 hits and relieves pain and swelling. No more than ten doses should be taken in a day.</p>\n<h3>Derrly Salve</h3><p>This lotion is made from [[HERB:herb-delrean-delrian|Delrean]]. Spread on the skin, it repels insects; they must overcome resistance equivalent to a 7th-level effect to approach within ten feet.</p>\n<h3>Gilder Dust (Ilyalma)</h3><p>A magical mixture of rare substances, this powder repairs injuries to organs, arteries, and veins in 1–3 minutes when sprinkled over the wound. It is normally available only from Elven healers and a few Mannish apothecaries or alchemists. It is also known as Astannûn, Númenasto, and Panaglu.</p>\n<h3>Golden Cress Salve</h3><p>[[HERB:herb-golden-cress-gold-chute|Golden Cress]] is dried, ground, and mixed with tallow. The salve closes and heals bleeding flesh wounds, restoring 1–5 hits in 1–5 rounds.</p>\n<h3>Harlindar</h3><p>This powder, mixed with water and drunk, is used to prevent miscarriage. It is made from herbs of southern Eriador.</p>\n<h3>Joef</h3><p>This inhaled powder allows the user to send a mental call to a conscious friend known to them. Range is one hundred feet multiplied by the user’s level. Its Addiction Factor is particularly high.</p>\n<h3>Maiana</h3><p>This decongestant liquid, prepared from plants of the northern slopes of the Misty Mountains, halves the dangerous symptoms caused by pulmonary complaints and respiratory disease.</p>\n<h3>Melem Loza Salve</h3><p>Prepared from [[HERB:herb-melem-loza-balm-vine|Melem Loza]], this salve doubles the effect of healing magic applied to the same wound or ailment.</p>\n<h3>Rune Salve</h3><p>Also called Arkasu, All-heal, or Mrim, this salve heals 2–12 hits, speeds the closing and healing of large open wounds by about half, acts as an antiseptic, and halves pain penalties from minor injuries. It is prepared from several herbs of the plains of Rhûn.</p>\n<h3>Sindiluin Tincture</h3><p>A secret process turns Sindiluin into a styptic tincture capable of stopping any bleeding. Healers who know the preparation seldom reveal its details.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}},{"name":"Diseases","text":{"content":"<section class=\"fdrmu-journal\"><h2>Diseases</h2><p>Diseases in Endor may come from parasites, animals, plants, or invisible agents. Their level is used as a reference for Resistance Rolls, and their severity is not always proportional to how harmless they appear.</p>\n<h3>Angurth — level 2</h3><p>Spread by fleas, this affliction causes a slow and painful death if not resisted.</p>\n<h3>Bukandas — level 4</h3><p>Contracted from certain wolves, it causes severe asthma.</p>\n<h3>Blue Hand (E Luincam) — level 4</h3><p>Spread by the bite of the Death Shrew, this disease is not contagious. A minor failure on the Resistance Roll turns the skin blue, imposes <strong>−20</strong> to Appearance, and may temporarily slow activity. A more severe failure adds outbreaks of large boils that can greatly reduce Appearance and Presence bonus, reduce available hits, and cause bleeding from the nose and ears, with doubled bleeding rate.</p>\n<h3>Grelnixar — level 8</h3><p>Associated with the Vrel plant, this “running death” compels the victim to run whenever able, eventually causing exhaustion and possibly death.</p>\n<h3>Gurth-nu-Fuin — level 3</h3><p>This viral infection causes a slow and painful death.</p>\n<h3>Igturfas — level 5</h3><p>Spread by certain snakes, it causes severe intellectual impairment and reduces Reasoning by 20 points while the disease persists.</p>\n<h3>Jadaras — level 2</h3><p>Associated with the Janar herb, it reduces Agility by 5 points.</p>\n<h3>Shutinis — level 3</h3><p>Spread by Hultif insects, it causes madness.</p>\n<h3>Vemaak — level 7</h3><p>Spread by certain hornets, it causes loss of hearing.</p></section>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["4 - Herbes et Substances, guide des simples"],"en":["4 - Herbs & Substances, Traveller’s Guide"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-04-herbs"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"_id":"dTkaVO6T3GvcmE2G"}
{"name":"Sorcery","sort":60000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"sorcellerie","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"sorcellerie","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Sorcellerie","pages":[{"name":"Sorcellerie","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Sorcellerie</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>La <strong>Sorcellerie</strong> n’est pas un quatrième Royaume. Elle désigne des sorts ou des Listes conçus pour dominer, contrôler ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda et liés à l’Élément de Morgoth.</p><ul><li>Toute utilisation déclenche automatiquement un test de Corruption.</li><li><strong>-15</strong> supplémentaire à la RR de Corruption.</li><li>Les <strong>Points de Corruption gagnés sont doublés</strong>.</li><li>La catégorie RMU <code>Evil</code> reste distincte : elle ne devient Sorcellerie que lorsque la source MERP le confirme.</li></ul><p>MERP-RMU représentera la Sorcellerie par un flag séparé sur la Liste ou sur le sort, sans modifier le <code>listType</code> natif RMU.</p></section><h2>Définition</h2><p>La Sorcellerie désigne les formes de magie qui s’accordent directement avec la logique de Morgoth : imposer une volonté étrangère au monde, soumettre les êtres, corrompre ce qui existe ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda.</p><p>Elle ne se définit pas simplement par la puissance du sort, son apparence inquiétante ou son caractère offensif. Un sort très destructeur peut ne pas être de la Sorcellerie ; un sort discret peut l’être pleinement s’il a été conçu pour briser la volonté, dégrader la vie ou imposer une altération contraire à l’ordre naturel.</p><h2>Une magie liée à l’Élément de Morgoth</h2><p>Les sorts de Sorcellerie utilisent directement l’influence de Morgoth diffusée dans Arda. Ils ne se contentent pas d’exposer occasionnellement leur utilisateur à la Corruption par un mauvais usage d’un pouvoir autrement neutre : ils sont eux-mêmes construits autour d’une source et d’une logique liées à Morgoth.</p><h2>Mauvais usage et Sorcellerie</h2><p>Un sort ordinaire peut devenir corrupteur selon l’intention du lanceur. La Sorcellerie fonctionne différemment : lorsqu’un sort appartient à une Liste explicitement sorcière ou est lui-même défini comme tel, le test de Corruption est requis parce que le sort relève intrinsèquement de cette tradition.</p><h2>Sorcellerie et Royaumes</h2><p>La Sorcellerie n’est pas un Royaume. Elle peut apparaître dans l’Essence, la Canalisation ou le Mentalisme. Le Royaume décrit la source et le mode d’accès au fairë ; la Sorcellerie décrit la nature corruptrice particulière d’un sort ou d’une Liste.</p><h2>Pas de Domaine Arcane</h2><p>La Sorcellerie ne doit pas non plus être confondue avec un domaine Arcane. Dans l’organisation retenue pour la Terre du Milieu, elle s’applique à l’intérieur des Royaumes et catégories de Listes existants.</p><h2>Listes de Sorcellerie</h2><p>Lorsque le corpus MERP identifie une Liste comme entièrement constituée de Sorcellerie, chaque sort de la Liste doit être traité comme sorcier. La Liste Wounding constitue un exemple clair dans notre travail d’intégration.</p><h2>Catégorie RMU Evil</h2><p><code>Evil</code> est un type de Liste RMU. La Sorcellerie est une classification MERP liée à l’Élément de Morgoth et à la Corruption. Les deux notions se recoupent fortement mais ne doivent pas être fusionnées mécaniquement sans preuve explicite.</p><h2>Métadonnée MERP</h2><p>Chaque Liste ou sort explicitement identifié comme Sorcellerie devra recevoir une métadonnée dédiée, par exemple <code>flags.merp-rmu.sorcery = true</code>. Cette donnée s’ajoute au <code>system.listType</code> natif et ne le remplace pas.</p><h3>Au niveau de la Liste</h3><p>Lorsqu’une Liste entière est sorcière, le flag est placé sur la Liste et tous ses sorts héritent de ce statut.</p><h3>Au niveau du sort</h3><p>Si une Liste ordinaire contient un sort explicitement sorcier, le flag peut être ajouté au sort seul. L’automatisation vérifiera d’abord le sort puis la Liste.</p><h2>Test de Corruption automatique</h2><p>Un sort de Sorcellerie déclenche automatiquement un test de Corruption après la résolution normale du sort. Le niveau d’attaque de la RR correspond au niveau du sort et la règle principale utilise une RR contre l’Essence, sauf variante de campagne.</p><h2>Pénalité spécifique</h2><p>La Sorcellerie impose un modificateur supplémentaire de <strong>-15</strong> à la RR. Cette pénalité s’ajoute aux modificateurs de Type, aux PC actuels, au bonus racial et à l’Empathie.</p><h2>Doublement des PC</h2><p>Lorsqu’un échec produit des Points de Corruption, le montant obtenu est doublé : +1 devient +2, +2 devient +4, +3 devient +6, +4 devient +8 et +5 devient +10. Les pertes de PC obtenues sur une réussite ne sont pas doublées.</p><h2>Spirale de Corruption</h2><p>La combinaison du -15 et du doublement rend la Sorcellerie particulièrement dangereuse. Les PC gagnés pénalisent les tests futurs, ce qui peut créer une accélération progressive de la Corruption.</p><h2>L’attrait de l’efficacité</h2><p>La Sorcellerie doit souvent être efficace. C’est précisément ce qui la rend tentante. Domination, malédiction ou destruction directe peuvent résoudre rapidement des problèmes que des méthodes ordinaires rendraient longues ou incertaines.</p><h2>Une bonne intention ne purifie pas automatiquement la Sorcellerie</h2><p>L’exception permettant parfois d’éviter la Corruption pour un sort ordinaire approprié doit être appliquée avec beaucoup plus de prudence à la Sorcellerie. Un sort intrinsèquement sorcier reste lié à l’Élément de Morgoth ; la nature du moyen employé compte autant que l’objectif.</p><h2>Domination, malédictions et nécromancie</h2><p>La domination est l’expression la plus claire de la logique de Morgoth. Les malédictions imposent une altération nuisible durable. La nécromancie intervient sur la mort, les esprits ou les corps d’une manière susceptible de violer l’ordre naturel. Lorsque le corpus classe ces Listes comme Sorcellerie, leurs sorts reçoivent le traitement correspondant.</p><h2>Blessures et destruction du corps</h2><p>Des Listes comme Wounding illustrent une magie conçue pour infliger directement blessures, saignements, fractures, dommages sensoriels, paralysies ou drains vitaux. Elle ne se contente pas de projeter une force extérieure contre une cible mais impose directement une dégradation précise de l’organisme.</p><h2>Tromperie et peur</h2><p>Toutes les illusions et tous les effets de peur ne sont pas automatiquement Sorcellerie. La classification doit venir de la source MERP lorsqu’elle existe. Le contexte et l’intention peuvent déclencher la Corruption sans transformer rétroactivement la Liste en Sorcellerie.</p><h2>Listes Maléfiques et Listes de Profession</h2><p>Les Listes Maléfiques sont le premier endroit où rechercher des Listes sorcières mais ne sont pas automatiquement synonymes. Des Listes de Profession peuvent aussi relever de la Sorcellerie ; elles devront être examinées individuellement.</p><h2>Apprentissage</h2><p>Les Listes de Sorcellerie ne devraient pas être traitées comme des connaissances auxquelles les personnages accèdent automatiquement parce qu’ils peuvent payer leur coût. Maître, texte ou source de savoir appropriée restent nécessaires, avec une provenance souvent liée à l’Ombre ou à des traditions interdites.</p><h2>Rareté et réactions sociales</h2><p>La Sorcellerie doit rester rare, particulièrement parmi les Peuples Libres. La découverte qu’un personnage l’emploie peut provoquer des réactions plus graves que la simple découverte de capacités magiques ordinaires.</p><h2>Peuples et Sorcellerie</h2><p>Les Elfes peuvent reconnaître avec gravité une magie opposée à leur rapport harmonieux au Nolwë. Les Khazâd ne doivent pas voir leurs enchantements ordinaires confondus avec la Sorcellerie. Chez les Hommes, la connaissance de la distinction varie fortement. Les serviteurs de l’Ombre peuvent en transmettre certaines traditions à leurs agents les plus utiles.</p><h2>Sauron et Saruman</h2><p>Sauron représente un maître exceptionnel de la domination et de la corruption, mais la Sorcellerie trouve sa racine plus profondément dans l’Élément de Morgoth. L’exemple de Saruman montre qu’un individu peut s’engager progressivement dans cette logique sans avoir commencé comme serviteur de l’Ombre.</p><h2>Relation avec le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</h2><p>Un sort sorcier reste un lancement de sort et peut donc déclencher simultanément un Jet de Risque et un test de Corruption. Le premier concerne ce que l’Ombre détecte ; le second ce que l’usage du pouvoir fait au lanceur.</p><h2>Automatisation</h2><p>Le module vérifiera le flag Sorcellerie du sort puis de sa Liste. Si le statut est actif, il déclenchera le test de Corruption, appliquera -15 et doublera les PC positifs. L’historique de l’Actor conservera idéalement Liste, sort, niveau, résultat de RR et PC gagnés.</p><h2>Identification visuelle</h2><p>Les fiches de Listes pourront afficher un encadré ou badge Sorcellerie afin que le joueur sache avant l’utilisation que tous les sorts concernés sont soumis aux règles renforcées de Corruption.</p><h2>Principe de référence</h2><p>La Sorcellerie représente une manière d’exercer le pouvoir qui reproduit directement la logique fondamentale de Morgoth : substituer sa propre volonté à l’ordre du monde et à la liberté de ceux qui l’habitent.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Sorcery","pages":[{"name":"Sorcery","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Sorcery</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p><strong>Sorcery</strong> is not a fourth Realm. It refers to spells or Lists designed to dominate, control, or destroy the natural order of Arda and tied to the Morgoth Element.</p><ul><li>Every use automatically triggers a Corruption Test.</li><li>An additional <strong>-15</strong> to the Corruption RR.</li><li><strong>Corruption Points gained are doubled</strong>.</li><li>The RMU <code>Evil</code> category remains distinct: it becomes Sorcery only when the MERP source confirms it.</li></ul><p>MERP-RMU represents Sorcery through a separate flag on the List or spell, without altering RMU's native <code>listType</code>.</p></section><h2>Definition</h2><p>Sorcery refers to forms of magic that accord directly with Morgoth's logic: imposing an alien will upon the world, subjugating beings, corrupting what exists, or destroying the natural order of Arda.</p><p>It is not defined simply by a spell's power, sinister appearance, or offensive nature. A highly destructive spell may not be Sorcery; a subtle spell may be entirely Sorcerous if it was designed to break the will, degrade life, or impose an alteration contrary to the natural order.</p><h2>Magic Tied to the Morgoth Element</h2><p>Sorcery spells draw directly upon Morgoth's influence dispersed throughout Arda. They do not merely expose their user occasionally to Corruption through the misuse of otherwise neutral power: they are themselves built around a source and a logic tied to Morgoth.</p><h2>Misuse and Sorcery</h2><p>An ordinary spell can become corrupting depending on the caster's intent. Sorcery works differently: when a spell belongs to a List explicitly identified as Sorcerous, or is itself defined as such, a Corruption Test is required because the spell intrinsically belongs to that tradition.</p><h2>Sorcery and Realms</h2><p>Sorcery is not a Realm. It can appear within Essence, Channeling, or Mentalism. The Realm describes the source of and access to fairë; Sorcery describes the particular corrupting nature of a spell or List.</p><h2>Not an Arcane Domain</h2><p>Sorcery should not be confused with an Arcane domain either. In the organisation adopted for Middle-earth, it applies within the existing Realms and List categories.</p><h2>Sorcery Lists</h2><p>When the MERP corpus identifies a List as being entirely composed of Sorcery, every spell on that List must be treated as Sorcerous. The Wounding List is a clear example in our integration work.</p><h2>RMU Evil Category</h2><p><code>Evil</code> is an RMU List type. Sorcery is a MERP classification tied to the Morgoth Element and Corruption. The two concepts overlap strongly, but they must not be mechanically merged without explicit evidence.</p><h2>MERP Metadata</h2><p>Every List or spell explicitly identified as Sorcery should receive dedicated metadata, for example <code>flags.merp-rmu.sorcery = true</code>. This data is added alongside the native <code>system.listType</code> and does not replace it.</p><h3>At List Level</h3><p>When an entire List is Sorcerous, the flag is placed on the List and all of its spells inherit that status.</p><h3>At Spell Level</h3><p>If an ordinary List contains a spell explicitly identified as Sorcery, the flag can be added to that spell alone. Automation checks the spell first and then the List.</p><h2>Automatic Corruption Test</h2><p>A Sorcery spell automatically triggers a Corruption Test after the spell has been resolved normally. The RR's attack level is equal to the spell's level, and the main rule uses an RR against Essence unless a campaign variant is in use.</p><h2>Specific Penalty</h2><p>Sorcery imposes an additional <strong>-15</strong> modifier to the RR. This penalty is added to Type modifiers, current CP, the racial bonus, and Empathy.</p><h2>Doubling CP</h2><p>When a failure produces Corruption Points, the amount gained is doubled: +1 becomes +2, +2 becomes +4, +3 becomes +6, +4 becomes +8, and +5 becomes +10. CP losses resulting from a successful roll are not doubled.</p><h2>Corruption Spiral</h2><p>The combination of -15 and doubling makes Sorcery particularly dangerous. CP gained penalise future tests, which can create a progressively accelerating spiral of Corruption.</p><h2>The Appeal of Efficiency</h2><p>Sorcery should often be effective. That is precisely what makes it tempting. Domination, cursing, or direct destruction can resolve quickly problems that ordinary methods would make slow or uncertain.</p><h2>Good Intent Does Not Automatically Purify Sorcery</h2><p>The exception that can sometimes avoid Corruption when an appropriate ordinary spell is used must be applied far more cautiously to Sorcery. An intrinsically Sorcerous spell remains tied to the Morgoth Element; the nature of the means employed matters as much as the objective.</p><h2>Domination, Curses, and Necromancy</h2><p>Domination is the clearest expression of Morgoth's logic. Curses impose a lasting harmful alteration. Necromancy interferes with death, spirits, or bodies in ways that may violate the natural order. When the corpus classifies such Lists as Sorcery, their spells receive the corresponding treatment.</p><h2>Wounds and Destruction of the Body</h2><p>Lists such as Wounding illustrate magic designed to inflict wounds, bleeding, fractures, sensory damage, paralysis, or vital drains directly. It does not merely project an external force against a target but imposes a precise degradation directly upon the organism.</p><h2>Deception and Fear</h2><p>Not every illusion and not every fear effect is automatically Sorcery. The classification must come from the MERP source when one exists. Context and intent can trigger Corruption without retroactively turning the List into Sorcery.</p><h2>Evil Lists and Profession Lists</h2><p>Evil Lists are the first place to look for Sorcery Lists, but the terms are not automatically synonymous. Profession Lists can also belong to Sorcery and must be examined individually.</p><h2>Learning</h2><p>Sorcery Lists should not be treated as knowledge characters gain automatically merely because they can pay the cost. An appropriate master, text, or source of knowledge remains necessary, with an origin often connected to the Shadow or forbidden traditions.</p><h2>Rarity and Social Reactions</h2><p>Sorcery should remain rare, particularly among the Free Peoples. Discovering that a character uses it can provoke reactions more severe than merely discovering ordinary magical abilities.</p><h2>Peoples and Sorcery</h2><p>Elves may gravely recognise magic opposed to their harmonious relationship with Nolwë. The Khazâd should not see their ordinary enchantments confused with Sorcery. Among Men, awareness of the distinction varies greatly. Servants of the Shadow may transmit certain traditions to their most useful agents.</p><h2>Sauron and Saruman</h2><p>Sauron is an exceptional master of domination and corruption, but Sorcery has deeper roots in the Morgoth Element. Saruman's example shows that an individual can move progressively into this logic without having begun as a servant of the Shadow.</p><h2>Relationship with the Risk of Attracting the Shadow</h2><p>A Sorcerous spell remains a spell cast and can therefore trigger both a Risk Roll and a Corruption Test at the same time. The first concerns what the Shadow detects; the second concerns what the use of power does to the caster.</p><h2>Automation</h2><p>The module checks the Sorcery flag on the spell and then on its List. If the status is active, it triggers the Corruption Test, applies -15, and doubles positive CP. Ideally, the Actor history records the List, spell, level, RR result, and CP gained.</p><h2>Visual Identification</h2><p>List sheets may display a Sorcery box or badge so that the player knows before use that all affected spells are subject to the reinforced Corruption rules.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Sorcery represents a way of exercising power that directly reproduces Morgoth's fundamental logic: replacing the order of the world and the freedom of those who inhabit it with one's own will.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Sorcery","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Sorcery</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p><strong>Sorcery</strong> is not a fourth Realm. It refers to spells or Lists designed to dominate, control, or destroy the natural order of Arda and tied to the Morgoth Element.</p><ul><li>Every use automatically triggers a Corruption Test.</li><li>An additional <strong>-15</strong> to the Corruption RR.</li><li><strong>Corruption Points gained are doubled</strong>.</li><li>The RMU <code>Evil</code> category remains distinct: it becomes Sorcery only when the MERP source confirms it.</li></ul><p>MERP-RMU represents Sorcery through a separate flag on the List or spell, without altering RMU's native <code>listType</code>.</p></section><h2>Definition</h2><p>Sorcery refers to forms of magic that accord directly with Morgoth's logic: imposing an alien will upon the world, subjugating beings, corrupting what exists, or destroying the natural order of Arda.</p><p>It is not defined simply by a spell's power, sinister appearance, or offensive nature. A highly destructive spell may not be Sorcery; a subtle spell may be entirely Sorcerous if it was designed to break the will, degrade life, or impose an alteration contrary to the natural order.</p><h2>Magic Tied to the Morgoth Element</h2><p>Sorcery spells draw directly upon Morgoth's influence dispersed throughout Arda. They do not merely expose their user occasionally to Corruption through the misuse of otherwise neutral power: they are themselves built around a source and a logic tied to Morgoth.</p><h2>Misuse and Sorcery</h2><p>An ordinary spell can become corrupting depending on the caster's intent. Sorcery works differently: when a spell belongs to a List explicitly identified as Sorcerous, or is itself defined as such, a Corruption Test is required because the spell intrinsically belongs to that tradition.</p><h2>Sorcery and Realms</h2><p>Sorcery is not a Realm. It can appear within Essence, Channeling, or Mentalism. The Realm describes the source of and access to fairë; Sorcery describes the particular corrupting nature of a spell or List.</p><h2>Not an Arcane Domain</h2><p>Sorcery should not be confused with an Arcane domain either. In the organisation adopted for Middle-earth, it applies within the existing Realms and List categories.</p><h2>Sorcery Lists</h2><p>When the MERP corpus identifies a List as being entirely composed of Sorcery, every spell on that List must be treated as Sorcerous. The Wounding List is a clear example in our integration work.</p><h2>RMU Evil Category</h2><p><code>Evil</code> is an RMU List type. Sorcery is a MERP classification tied to the Morgoth Element and Corruption. The two concepts overlap strongly, but they must not be mechanically merged without explicit evidence.</p><h2>MERP Metadata</h2><p>Every List or spell explicitly identified as Sorcery should receive dedicated metadata, for example <code>flags.merp-rmu.sorcery = true</code>. This data is added alongside the native <code>system.listType</code> and does not replace it.</p><h3>At List Level</h3><p>When an entire List is Sorcerous, the flag is placed on the List and all of its spells inherit that status.</p><h3>At Spell Level</h3><p>If an ordinary List contains a spell explicitly identified as Sorcery, the flag can be added to that spell alone. Automation checks the spell first and then the List.</p><h2>Automatic Corruption Test</h2><p>A Sorcery spell automatically triggers a Corruption Test after the spell has been resolved normally. The RR's attack level is equal to the spell's level, and the main rule uses an RR against Essence unless a campaign variant is in use.</p><h2>Specific Penalty</h2><p>Sorcery imposes an additional <strong>-15</strong> modifier to the RR. This penalty is added to Type modifiers, current CP, the racial bonus, and Empathy.</p><h2>Doubling CP</h2><p>When a failure produces Corruption Points, the amount gained is doubled: +1 becomes +2, +2 becomes +4, +3 becomes +6, +4 becomes +8, and +5 becomes +10. CP losses resulting from a successful roll are not doubled.</p><h2>Corruption Spiral</h2><p>The combination of -15 and doubling makes Sorcery particularly dangerous. CP gained penalise future tests, which can create a progressively accelerating spiral of Corruption.</p><h2>The Appeal of Efficiency</h2><p>Sorcery should often be effective. That is precisely what makes it tempting. Domination, cursing, or direct destruction can resolve quickly problems that ordinary methods would make slow or uncertain.</p><h2>Good Intent Does Not Automatically Purify Sorcery</h2><p>The exception that can sometimes avoid Corruption when an appropriate ordinary spell is used must be applied far more cautiously to Sorcery. An intrinsically Sorcerous spell remains tied to the Morgoth Element; the nature of the means employed matters as much as the objective.</p><h2>Domination, Curses, and Necromancy</h2><p>Domination is the clearest expression of Morgoth's logic. Curses impose a lasting harmful alteration. Necromancy interferes with death, spirits, or bodies in ways that may violate the natural order. When the corpus classifies such Lists as Sorcery, their spells receive the corresponding treatment.</p><h2>Wounds and Destruction of the Body</h2><p>Lists such as Wounding illustrate magic designed to inflict wounds, bleeding, fractures, sensory damage, paralysis, or vital drains directly. It does not merely project an external force against a target but imposes a precise degradation directly upon the organism.</p><h2>Deception and Fear</h2><p>Not every illusion and not every fear effect is automatically Sorcery. The classification must come from the MERP source when one exists. Context and intent can trigger Corruption without retroactively turning the List into Sorcery.</p><h2>Evil Lists and Profession Lists</h2><p>Evil Lists are the first place to look for Sorcery Lists, but the terms are not automatically synonymous. Profession Lists can also belong to Sorcery and must be examined individually.</p><h2>Learning</h2><p>Sorcery Lists should not be treated as knowledge characters gain automatically merely because they can pay the cost. An appropriate master, text, or source of knowledge remains necessary, with an origin often connected to the Shadow or forbidden traditions.</p><h2>Rarity and Social Reactions</h2><p>Sorcery should remain rare, particularly among the Free Peoples. Discovering that a character uses it can provoke reactions more severe than merely discovering ordinary magical abilities.</p><h2>Peoples and Sorcery</h2><p>Elves may gravely recognise magic opposed to their harmonious relationship with Nolwë. The Khazâd should not see their ordinary enchantments confused with Sorcery. Among Men, awareness of the distinction varies greatly. Servants of the Shadow may transmit certain traditions to their most useful agents.</p><h2>Sauron and Saruman</h2><p>Sauron is an exceptional master of domination and corruption, but Sorcery has deeper roots in the Morgoth Element. Saruman's example shows that an individual can move progressively into this logic without having begun as a servant of the Shadow.</p><h2>Relationship with the Risk of Attracting the Shadow</h2><p>A Sorcerous spell remains a spell cast and can therefore trigger both a Risk Roll and a Corruption Test at the same time. The first concerns what the Shadow detects; the second concerns what the use of power does to the caster.</p><h2>Automation</h2><p>The module checks the Sorcery flag on the spell and then on its List. If the status is active, it triggers the Corruption Test, applies -15, and doubles positive CP. Ideally, the Actor history records the List, spell, level, RR result, and CP gained.</p><h2>Visual Identification</h2><p>List sheets may display a Sorcery box or badge so that the player knows before use that all affected spells are subject to the reinforced Corruption rules.</p><h2>Reference Principle</h2><p>Sorcery represents a way of exercising power that directly reproduces Morgoth's fundamental logic: replacing the order of the world and the freedom of those who inhabit it with one's own will.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"8drtcDpRqhjEGyty"}
{"name":"Discreet Use of Magic","sort":30000,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"collection":"merp-rmu-rules","key":"usage-discret-magie","section":"magie","compendiumSourceKey":"usage-discret-magie","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-rules","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"regles-magie","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Usage discret de la magie","pages":[{"name":"Usage discret de la magie","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Usage discret de la magie</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Résumé pratique</h2><p>Au Troisième Âge, la magie est <strong>rare, prudente et discrète</strong>. Un effet spectaculaire peut attirer les témoins, créer une réputation et surtout exposer le lanceur à l’attention de l’Ombre.</p><ul><li>La discrétion ordinaire concerne ce que les témoins voient et entendent.</li><li>Le Risque d’attirer l’Ombre représente une détection surnaturelle distincte.</li><li>Le <strong>Subterfuge magique</strong> RMU peut masquer certaines manifestations, mais ne supprime pas automatiquement le Facteur de Risque MERP.</li></ul><p>La retenue n’interdit pas la magie : elle donne un poids au choix du moment, du lieu et de la méthode d’utilisation.</p></section><h2>Une puissance rarement montrée</h2><p>L’usage de la magie en Terre du Milieu est généralement rare, prudent et discret, particulièrement au Troisième Âge. Les utilisateurs de magie eux-mêmes sont relativement peu nombreux, et les manifestations ouvertes de pouvoir le sont davantage encore.</p><p>Les Elfes emploient plus naturellement certaines formes de magie dans leur vie quotidienne, tandis que les Khazâd peuvent également recourir à des sorts dans des contextes variés, y compris pour produire des objets merveilleux. Chez les Hobbits, la magie appartient beaucoup plus volontiers au domaine des légendes, et de nombreux Hommes n’en ont qu’une connaissance indirecte.</p><h2>Différences entre les peuples</h2><p>Chez les Elfes, le pouvoir peut être intégré à des activités qui paraissent naturelles à ceux qui les pratiquent. Certaines œuvres, chansons, objets ou manifestations peuvent être perçues comme de la magie par un observateur humain alors qu’un Elfe les comprend avant tout comme une expression du Nolwë.</p><p>Chez les Khazâd, l’usage de sorts peut accompagner l’artisanat, la création ou d’autres activités pratiques sans que chaque manifestation devienne une démonstration publique de puissance. Chez de nombreux Hommes, la magie reste beaucoup plus inhabituelle ; chez les Hobbits, elle demeure encore plus souvent liée aux récits.</p><h2>Discrétion et interprétation</h2><p>Une manifestation magique n’est pas toujours immédiatement identifiable comme telle. Une lumière inhabituelle, une porte qui s’ouvre sans contact, une guérison rapide ou un objet possédant une qualité extraordinaire peuvent être interprétés de plusieurs manières selon l’observateur.</p><h2>Le choix de ne pas employer la magie</h2><p>La possession d’un sort ne signifie pas que le personnage doit l’utiliser chaque fois qu’il pourrait résoudre un problème. Une porte peut être ouverte avec une clé, une rivière par un pont et un feu avec du bois. La magie devient particulièrement utile lorsque les moyens ordinaires sont insuffisants, trop dangereux ou trop lents.</p><h2>La prudence des utilisateurs de pouvoir</h2><p>Les raisons de cette retenue ne sont pas seulement sociales. Au Troisième Âge, deux dangers majeurs contribuent directement à limiter l’usage de la magie : l’attention de Sauron et la corruption liée à l’Élément de Morgoth.</p><h2>L’Ombre de Sauron</h2><p>La présence de Sauron en Mordor constitue l’une des principales raisons de l’usage subtil et secret de la magie. Le Seigneur des Ténèbres cherche à surveiller, subvertir ou éliminer les utilisateurs de pouvoir qui ne sont pas sous son contrôle.</p><h2>Risque et visibilité ne sont pas la même chose</h2><p>Une magie visuellement spectaculaire paraît naturellement plus dangereuse, mais la règle MERP ne réduit pas le risque à la simple apparence. Chaque sort possède un Facteur de Risque dépendant de son type et de l’activité des forces de l’Ombre.</p><p>La discrétion possède donc deux dimensions : <strong>ordinaire</strong>, pour éviter que les personnes présentes comprennent qu’un sort vient d’être utilisé ; et <strong>surnaturelle</strong>, pour éviter que l’usage du pouvoir soit détecté par l’Ombre.</p><h2>Subterfuge magique</h2><p>RMU possède la compétence <strong>Subterfuge magique</strong> (Spell Trickery), qui permet de réduire les pénalités liées aux tentatives visant à dissimuler ou modifier les manifestations visuelles, gestuelles ou vocales d’un sort. Elle doit être spécialisée dans une Liste particulière.</p><p>Cette compétence ne doit pas être interprétée comme supprimant automatiquement le Facteur de Risque. Le Subterfuge magique dissimule l’incantation et ses manifestations ; le Facteur de Risque représente la possibilité que l’usage du pouvoir lui-même soit remarqué par l’Ombre.</p><h2>Gestes, voix et manifestations</h2><p>Un utilisateur souhaitant rester discret peut chercher à réduire l’amplitude de ses mouvements, murmurer les paroles nécessaires ou employer une technique de Subterfuge magique. Certains effets, comme un mur de feu ou un éclair, restent toutefois visibles une fois produits.</p><h2>Effets subtils</h2><p>D’autres sorts sont naturellement beaucoup moins évidents : amélioration personnelle, perception surnaturelle, protection invisible ou modification mentale. Ils sont particulièrement adaptés aux personnages souhaitant préserver le secret de leur nature, sans être pour autant automatiquement dépourvus de risque surnaturel.</p><h2>Objets magiques</h2><p>L’usage d’un objet magique peut être plus discret que le lancement direct d’un sort. Une arme exceptionnelle, une cape ou un objet d’aide peut fonctionner sans produire de grande manifestation visible. Les témoins peuvent attribuer le résultat à la qualité de l’objet, à la compétence de son utilisateur ou à la chance.</p><h2>Magie et artisanat</h2><p>La discrétion peut aussi venir du fait que la magie est incorporée à un travail ordinaire. Chez un Alchimiste ou un artisan possédant des connaissances particulières, l’effet magique peut apparaître seulement lorsque l’objet est terminé ou utilisé.</p><h2>Pas de démonstration gratuite</h2><p>Une manifestation ouverte possède des conséquences possibles : réactions des témoins, rumeurs, peur, envie, intérêt d’une autorité ou attention de l’Ombre. Cela ne constitue pas une interdiction mais donne un poids réel à la décision de montrer son pouvoir.</p><h2>Témoins et réputation</h2><p>Une personne impressionnée peut raconter ce qu’elle a vu. La nouvelle peut se diffuser et le lanceur peut acquérir progressivement une réputation. Des ennemis peuvent préparer des contre-mesures, des autorités souhaiter l’employer ou le surveiller, et certaines communautés le traiter avec prestige ou méfiance.</p><h2>La retenue n’est pas la faiblesse</h2><p>Un utilisateur qui choisit de ne pas lancer un sort ne renonce pas nécessairement à sa puissance. La retenue peut être une forme de maîtrise. Les utilisateurs expérimentés comprennent souvent mieux les conséquences d’une manifestation inutile.</p><h2>Combat contre les Ténèbres</h2><p>La discrétion n’est jamais une règle absolue. Lorsque la situation l’exige, le personnage peut décider que la nécessité de vaincre immédiatement un adversaire dépasse le risque d’attirer l’attention. Les grands affrontements peuvent justifier des manifestations de puissance considérables.</p><h2>Équilibre</h2><p>Certains utilisateurs limitent également leur magie pour ne pas perturber l’équilibre des choses. Le monde possède un ordre naturel ; le modifier constamment par la force de la volonté peut être considéré comme imprudent même lorsqu’aucune intention malveillante n’est présente.</p><h2>Application pratique en jeu</h2><p>Lorsqu’un personnage envisage de lancer un sort, le contexte peut être évalué rapidement : qui peut voir ou entendre l’incantation ? l’effet sera-t-il visible ? existe-t-il une solution ordinaire ? la région rend-elle la révélation de ses pouvoirs dangereuse ? le sort risque-t-il d’attirer l’Ombre ou d’exposer le lanceur à la Corruption ?</p><h2>Application à RMU</h2><p>RMU continue à déterminer normalement les conditions d’incantation et les compétences pouvant modifier leur usage. MERP-RMU ajoute un contexte dans lequel cette discrétion possède une véritable importance. La magie reste puissante ; sa pratique devient culturellement et stratégiquement mesurée.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>Source MERP :</strong> règles de magie, pp. 225–228. Adaptation documentaire pour RMU ; les mécaniques natives RMU restent la base de résolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]},"en":{"name":"Discreet Use of Magic","pages":[{"name":"Discreet Use of Magic","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Discreet Use of Magic</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>In the Third Age, magic is <strong>rare, cautious, and discreet</strong>. A spectacular effect can attract witnesses, create a reputation, and above all expose the caster to the attention of the Shadow.</p><ul><li>Ordinary discretion concerns what witnesses see and hear.</li><li>The Risk of Attracting the Shadow represents a distinct supernatural form of detection.</li><li>RMU <strong>Spell Trickery</strong> can conceal certain manifestations, but does not automatically remove the MERP Risk Factor.</li></ul><p>Restraint does not forbid magic: it gives weight to the choice of when, where, and how to use it.</p></section><h2>A Power Rarely Displayed</h2><p>The use of magic in Middle-earth is generally rare, cautious, and discreet, particularly in the Third Age. Magic-users themselves are relatively few, and open manifestations of power are rarer still.</p><p>Elves use certain forms of magic more naturally in everyday life, while the Khazâd may also employ spells in varied contexts, including the creation of wondrous objects. Among Hobbits, magic belongs much more readily to the realm of legend, and many Men know of it only indirectly.</p><h2>Differences Between Peoples</h2><p>Among Elves, power may be integrated into activities that seem natural to those who practise them. Certain works, songs, objects, or manifestations may be perceived as magic by a human observer while an Elf understands them primarily as an expression of Nolwë.</p><p>Among the Khazâd, the use of spells may accompany craft, creation, or other practical activities without every manifestation becoming a public display of power. Among many Men, magic remains far more unusual; among Hobbits, it is still more often associated with stories.</p><h2>Discretion and Interpretation</h2><p>A magical manifestation is not always immediately identifiable as such. An unusual light, a door opening without contact, rapid healing, or an object possessing an extraordinary quality can be interpreted in several ways depending on the observer.</p><h2>The Choice Not to Use Magic</h2><p>Knowing a spell does not mean that a character must use it every time it could solve a problem. A door can be opened with a key, a river crossed by a bridge, and a fire lit with wood. Magic becomes especially useful when ordinary means are insufficient, too dangerous, or too slow.</p><h2>The Caution of Power-Users</h2><p>The reasons for this restraint are not merely social. In the Third Age, two major dangers directly contribute to limiting the use of magic: Sauron's attention and the Corruption associated with the Morgoth Element.</p><h2>The Shadow of Sauron</h2><p>Sauron's presence in Mordor is one of the chief reasons for the subtle and secret use of magic. The Dark Lord seeks to watch, subvert, or eliminate users of power who are not under his control.</p><h2>Risk and Visibility Are Not the Same Thing</h2><p>Visually spectacular magic naturally appears more dangerous, but the MERP rule does not reduce risk to appearance alone. Every spell has a Risk Factor based on its type and on the activity of the forces of the Shadow.</p><p>Discretion therefore has two dimensions: <strong>ordinary</strong>, to prevent those present from understanding that a spell has just been used; and <strong>supernatural</strong>, to prevent the use of power from being detected by the Shadow.</p><h2>Spell Trickery</h2><p>RMU includes the <strong>Spell Trickery</strong> Skill, which can reduce penalties associated with attempts to conceal or alter the visual, gestural, or vocal manifestations of a spell. It must be specialised in a particular List.</p><p>This Skill should not be interpreted as automatically removing the Risk Factor. Spell Trickery conceals the casting and its manifestations; the Risk Factor represents the possibility that the use of power itself is noticed by the Shadow.</p><h2>Gestures, Voice, and Manifestations</h2><p>A user wishing to remain discreet can try to reduce the amplitude of movements, whisper the necessary words, or employ Spell Trickery. Some effects, such as a wall of fire or a lightning bolt, nevertheless remain visible once produced.</p><h2>Subtle Effects</h2><p>Other spells are naturally much less obvious: self-enhancement, supernatural perception, invisible protection, or mental alteration. They are particularly suitable for characters who wish to preserve the secrecy of their nature, without thereby being automatically free of supernatural risk.</p><h2>Magic Items</h2><p>Using a magic item can be more discreet than casting a spell directly. An exceptional weapon, a cloak, or an item of aid may function without producing a major visible manifestation. Witnesses may attribute the result to the quality of the object, the skill of its user, or luck.</p><h2>Magic and Craft</h2><p>Discretion can also arise because magic is incorporated into ordinary work. For an Alchemist or a craftsman with specialised knowledge, the magical effect may become apparent only when the object is completed or used.</p><h2>No Gratuitous Displays</h2><p>An open manifestation can have consequences: reactions from witnesses, rumours, fear, envy, the interest of an authority, or the attention of the Shadow. This is not a prohibition, but it gives real weight to the decision to display one's power.</p><h2>Witnesses and Reputation</h2><p>An impressed observer may tell others what was seen. The story can spread and the caster may gradually acquire a reputation. Enemies can prepare countermeasures, authorities may wish to employ or watch the caster, and some communities may respond with prestige or suspicion.</p><h2>Restraint Is Not Weakness</h2><p>A user who chooses not to cast a spell is not necessarily renouncing power. Restraint can be a form of mastery. Experienced users often understand the consequences of an unnecessary manifestation more clearly.</p><h2>Fighting the Darkness</h2><p>Discretion is never an absolute rule. When the situation demands it, a character may decide that the need to defeat an opponent immediately outweighs the risk of drawing attention. Great confrontations may justify considerable displays of power.</p><h2>Balance</h2><p>Some users also limit their magic so as not to disturb the balance of things. The world possesses a natural order; constantly altering it through force of will can be regarded as unwise even when no malicious intent is present.</p><h2>Practical Application in Play</h2><p>When a character considers casting a spell, the context can be assessed quickly: who can see or hear the casting? Will the effect be visible? Is there an ordinary solution? Does the region make revealing one's powers dangerous? Could the spell attract the Shadow or expose the caster to Corruption?</p><h2>Application to RMU</h2><p>RMU continues to determine the normal casting conditions and the Skills that can modify their use. MERP-RMU adds a context in which this discretion has genuine importance. Magic remains powerful; its practice becomes culturally and strategically measured.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}]}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["1 - Magie dans MERP"],"en":["1 - Magic in MERP"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["rules-01-magic"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"pages":[{"name":"Discreet Use of Magic","text":{"content":"<div class=\"merp-rmu-rules\"><h1>Discreet Use of Magic</h1><section class=\"merp-rmu-rule-summary\"><h2>Practical Summary</h2><p>In the Third Age, magic is <strong>rare, cautious, and discreet</strong>. A spectacular effect can attract witnesses, create a reputation, and above all expose the caster to the attention of the Shadow.</p><ul><li>Ordinary discretion concerns what witnesses see and hear.</li><li>The Risk of Attracting the Shadow represents a distinct supernatural form of detection.</li><li>RMU <strong>Spell Trickery</strong> can conceal certain manifestations, but does not automatically remove the MERP Risk Factor.</li></ul><p>Restraint does not forbid magic: it gives weight to the choice of when, where, and how to use it.</p></section><h2>A Power Rarely Displayed</h2><p>The use of magic in Middle-earth is generally rare, cautious, and discreet, particularly in the Third Age. Magic-users themselves are relatively few, and open manifestations of power are rarer still.</p><p>Elves use certain forms of magic more naturally in everyday life, while the Khazâd may also employ spells in varied contexts, including the creation of wondrous objects. Among Hobbits, magic belongs much more readily to the realm of legend, and many Men know of it only indirectly.</p><h2>Differences Between Peoples</h2><p>Among Elves, power may be integrated into activities that seem natural to those who practise them. Certain works, songs, objects, or manifestations may be perceived as magic by a human observer while an Elf understands them primarily as an expression of Nolwë.</p><p>Among the Khazâd, the use of spells may accompany craft, creation, or other practical activities without every manifestation becoming a public display of power. Among many Men, magic remains far more unusual; among Hobbits, it is still more often associated with stories.</p><h2>Discretion and Interpretation</h2><p>A magical manifestation is not always immediately identifiable as such. An unusual light, a door opening without contact, rapid healing, or an object possessing an extraordinary quality can be interpreted in several ways depending on the observer.</p><h2>The Choice Not to Use Magic</h2><p>Knowing a spell does not mean that a character must use it every time it could solve a problem. A door can be opened with a key, a river crossed by a bridge, and a fire lit with wood. Magic becomes especially useful when ordinary means are insufficient, too dangerous, or too slow.</p><h2>The Caution of Power-Users</h2><p>The reasons for this restraint are not merely social. In the Third Age, two major dangers directly contribute to limiting the use of magic: Sauron's attention and the Corruption associated with the Morgoth Element.</p><h2>The Shadow of Sauron</h2><p>Sauron's presence in Mordor is one of the chief reasons for the subtle and secret use of magic. The Dark Lord seeks to watch, subvert, or eliminate users of power who are not under his control.</p><h2>Risk and Visibility Are Not the Same Thing</h2><p>Visually spectacular magic naturally appears more dangerous, but the MERP rule does not reduce risk to appearance alone. Every spell has a Risk Factor based on its type and on the activity of the forces of the Shadow.</p><p>Discretion therefore has two dimensions: <strong>ordinary</strong>, to prevent those present from understanding that a spell has just been used; and <strong>supernatural</strong>, to prevent the use of power from being detected by the Shadow.</p><h2>Spell Trickery</h2><p>RMU includes the <strong>Spell Trickery</strong> Skill, which can reduce penalties associated with attempts to conceal or alter the visual, gestural, or vocal manifestations of a spell. It must be specialised in a particular List.</p><p>This Skill should not be interpreted as automatically removing the Risk Factor. Spell Trickery conceals the casting and its manifestations; the Risk Factor represents the possibility that the use of power itself is noticed by the Shadow.</p><h2>Gestures, Voice, and Manifestations</h2><p>A user wishing to remain discreet can try to reduce the amplitude of movements, whisper the necessary words, or employ Spell Trickery. Some effects, such as a wall of fire or a lightning bolt, nevertheless remain visible once produced.</p><h2>Subtle Effects</h2><p>Other spells are naturally much less obvious: self-enhancement, supernatural perception, invisible protection, or mental alteration. They are particularly suitable for characters who wish to preserve the secrecy of their nature, without thereby being automatically free of supernatural risk.</p><h2>Magic Items</h2><p>Using a magic item can be more discreet than casting a spell directly. An exceptional weapon, a cloak, or an item of aid may function without producing a major visible manifestation. Witnesses may attribute the result to the quality of the object, the skill of its user, or luck.</p><h2>Magic and Craft</h2><p>Discretion can also arise because magic is incorporated into ordinary work. For an Alchemist or a craftsman with specialised knowledge, the magical effect may become apparent only when the object is completed or used.</p><h2>No Gratuitous Displays</h2><p>An open manifestation can have consequences: reactions from witnesses, rumours, fear, envy, the interest of an authority, or the attention of the Shadow. This is not a prohibition, but it gives real weight to the decision to display one's power.</p><h2>Witnesses and Reputation</h2><p>An impressed observer may tell others what was seen. The story can spread and the caster may gradually acquire a reputation. Enemies can prepare countermeasures, authorities may wish to employ or watch the caster, and some communities may respond with prestige or suspicion.</p><h2>Restraint Is Not Weakness</h2><p>A user who chooses not to cast a spell is not necessarily renouncing power. Restraint can be a form of mastery. Experienced users often understand the consequences of an unnecessary manifestation more clearly.</p><h2>Fighting the Darkness</h2><p>Discretion is never an absolute rule. When the situation demands it, a character may decide that the need to defeat an opponent immediately outweighs the risk of drawing attention. Great confrontations may justify considerable displays of power.</p><h2>Balance</h2><p>Some users also limit their magic so as not to disturb the balance of things. The world possesses a natural order; constantly altering it through force of will can be regarded as unwise even when no malicious intent is present.</p><h2>Practical Application in Play</h2><p>When a character considers casting a spell, the context can be assessed quickly: who can see or hear the casting? Will the effect be visible? Is there an ordinary solution? Does the region make revealing one's powers dangerous? Could the spell attract the Shadow or expose the caster to Corruption?</p><h2>Application to RMU</h2><p>RMU continues to determine the normal casting conditions and the Skills that can modify their use. MERP-RMU adds a context in which this discretion has genuine importance. Magic remains powerful; its practice becomes culturally and strategically measured.</p><hr><p class=\"merp-rmu-source\"><strong>MERP Source:</strong> magic rules, pp. 225–228. Documentary adaptation for RMU; native RMU mechanics remain the basis of resolution.</p></div>"},"title":{"show":false}}],"_id":"RkHOwSTonncopt1t"}
